export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "2 Kings",
  display: "Second Kings",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 01"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"There is not much division between 1 Kings and 2 Kings, so if you have not taken the time to read through 1 Kings first, I highly suggest you do so before entering into this book. We literally start off this chapter where 1 Kings 22 leaves it with the new king. Ahab's son takes the throne of the northern kingdom, and every king (this far) for the northern kingdom has been nothing but rotten. They have done some heinous and evil things to their own people, and have caused their own people to go against the Lord and worship false gods. Ahab being the worst of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have seen in the southern kingdom that God has somewhat blessed them. They have had a couple good kings so far, and the current king (Jehoshaphat) was doing mostly what was right by the Lord. Of course that won't last forever, but they have a lot going for them at the moment with their rightful kings. Elijah (a prophet of the Lord) was a prominent character in our last book as well, leading up to some pretty intense scenes with proving that the Lord was the true God, and left with the task of warning Ahab about his and his wife's death, while training a replacment for him (which will be Elisha, and that will come to pass in this book)."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are going to pick straight back up with the kings of Israel, and we are going to see that things have not gotten better with each passing king, but continually become more and more difficult for the people, and for the leadership as every king continues to spiral further and further away from the Lord. That is a warning to every person reading. Life does not get easier the further you get from the Lord. Life gets more complicated, the more we try to reject God and go our own way."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Moab Rebels","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moab and Israel have not been great friends for a good portion of the scriptures. Moab (if you don't remember) comes from the line of Lot. So they still have some relations to Israel, but they are certainly not a part of the chosen lineage. It was through Abraham (Lot's uncle) that God calls His people. With that said, there has been plenty of purpose for the land of Moab as far as God has tried to use them. They have been a mostly reject-filled group though, until Ruth came out of it. If it weren’t for this land, we would probably not be reading about these kings in the first place. David comes from a Moab line, let's not forget. But at this particular juncture, Moab has had enough of Israel and is ready to try and take them down."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Baalzebub\" means \"lord of the fly.\" Certainly sounds like a great and wonderful god to worship out of all of them. Obviously, this god doesn't decide whether someone is going to die or not. This god doesn't exist. It only exists because someone made him up. There has been no proof of this god ever actually supporting the people and giving answers like this. It just goes to show how far disconnected the kings of northern Israel were from the Lord. That fall from the lattice didn't seem to correct anything in his brain, though, so he could search out the Lord of creation."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"title":"God Intervenes","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God always intervenes with someone’s false beliefs. He always makes Himself present and available. He gives every person a chance, and even if Ahaziah had never heard of the God of creation (which is highly unlikely), then he will certainly get to know that He exists today. Notice that God is using Elijah still. Elijah was done with this work in our last book. He was ready to die after the whole incident with the fire coming down to prove against the false worshippers of Baal. God has been working on him over time, and as he has been preparing Elisha, he has been doing God's work still. God has been keeping Elijah busy. His mind has been stayed on ministry. He has had the difficult task of speaking to Ahab about some of the terrible things that would come to him and his family, and now he has the important task of revealing the Lord to Ahab's son. You'd think someone in the family tree would eventually get it... right?"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, that message took a turn quite quickly. Baalzebub probably would have given a completely different message. He would have said, \"No, King, you will be fine. Baalzebub's messengers will bring good tidings to try and make you feel better.\" Not the Lord, though. The Lord is telling the truth. Why would this king die from this kind of fall? We aren't told how far the fall was, but it was certainly far enough to inflict some pain on him. The Lord knows that this king is going to be just the same as all the other kings before him, and he will never turn to the Lord. God is preparing the next leader already, and now Elijah has the next difficult task of telling these messengers the bad news about their current king. Even still, I believe the Lord could have mercy on this king if the king would just turn to Him. I think the Lord would have mercy on his children too if the king would speak to his kids about the God of creation."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":8,"title":"The Messengers Respond","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The messengers weren't gone long enough. The king knew that there was no chance they could have made it all the way down to meet with these Baalzebub freaks quickly enough for them to be back so soon."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At least the messengers were being honest. They could have been worried about what the king might say or do with them if they didn't fulfill the task they were told to go fulfill, but they don't seem to be worried at all. Maybe it's because the king is sick, and they know that he is not able to get up to do anything. Maybe this king wasn't as terribly angry as the last. Perhaps these messengers knew that the Baalzebub god was not something to put any trust in. I don't really know, but I know that they were ready to give the simple answer from the man they just met up the road."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This proves that Ahaziah KNOWS about the Lord of creation. He has either heard the stories of Elijah, or he has personally SEEN Elijah in his life. His dad was not a big fan of Elijah. Ahab considered Elijah an enemy on several occasions. It wasn't because Elijah was mean to Ahab, it was because Elijah spoke the truth of the Lord to Ahab and Ahab didn't like it. I'm sure Ahaziah has the same bitter taste about Elijah in his own mouth. He has learned nothing from his family’s history and continues to taint that name and lineage."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":12,"title":"Fire From The Sky","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahaziah sent a whole army after Elijah. This was quite dramatic. It's just a show of power, yet there is no power higher than God, the creator. I don't think this is going to intimidate Elijah as much as Ahaziah was thinking it would. It is funny that this captain refers to Elijah as a \"man of God.\" He probably said it in a mocking way. He must not know Elijah all that well."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, that was over quickly. This is quite the amazing scene. Elijah has God's full power and authority on this matter. He just asks God for fire, and God gives it. God really cares about this man, Elijah. We don't hear about these types of events all that often in Scripture, but when we get these glimpses, we know it will be for a purpose. I could only wish I had this type of power with the Lord. The reason I don't is because I don't believe enough. I have some doubts about whether God will answer by fire or not. Elijah didn't have any doubts."},{"kind":"para","text":"He probably didn't doubt because he had direct communication with the Lord. He could literally HEAR the voice of the Lord. I don't have that kind of communication with Him. I struggle with some doubts, so I don't think fire will actually come down if I commanded it. This is the second time that Elijah calls fire down, and the Lord provides it. You'd think this would be amazing to everyone else standing by, but obviously, they didn't even see the awe of it the first time he does it with the children of Baal."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahaziah doesn't seem to learn the lesson. If my first captain was consumed by spontaneous combustion, I probably wouldn't attempt to send another captain. I would be very worried about the fact that Elijah has the power to just send fire down from the sky and consume whomever he sees fit. Of course, the Lord is going to use this as a means to grab the king’s attention, but obviously the king isn't too concerned about the proof of the Lord..."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So now the king has lost one hundred and two of his soldiers in a single day from miraculous fire falling from the sky. Three times Elijah has used fire so far, and it has been quite simple for the Lord to respond accordingly. This was righteous judgment, mind you. No matter how much proof the Lord provides to these people, they will never believe. Much of the world is like this today. They say, \"I won't believe in the Lord unless there is an obvious sign.\" Literal fire could fall from Heaven as an obvious sign, and they STILL wouldn't believe. In this case, fire had literally come down from the sky in EXACT, precise locations three different times, and the majority still had reason to reject the Lord. I just don't get it..."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"Elijah Responds","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This captain has learned some lessons from the first one hundred and two people. This is so funny to me. He is begging Elijah to show some mercy here. He doesn't want to be consumed like the rest. He is not showing power, he is showing faith. That's important to note. God's fire stirred up some faith in this captain that likely wasn't there before. There was obviously something a little more to this Elijah man than what others were assuming, and so this captain is serious about his request. He was just told to do a job, and he doesn't want to put him and his soldiers’ lives on the line for this small task."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, this is a true sign of a leader. He is begging for compassion and peace from the man of God. He knows that this is a risk for him and his men, and he's still putting his life on the line by coming and asking anything from Elijah at all. So he's looking for mercy, whereas the other captains didn't seek mercy at all."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This goes to prove that the king knew what was going on. He knew that Elijah had taken care of these men prior to an amazing miracle of fire coming from the sky. He just didn't care. He simply didn't want to look at the proof."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Whenever scripture speaks about \"the angel of the Lord,\" we can rightfully assume this is speaking about the pre-incarnate Christ. Whether or not that is the case here could be argued, but I'm not going to focus on it. I, personally, believe this is Christ speaking to Elijah. I think Christ is giving Elijah the command here. But the important thing to note is that Elijah is not listening to man to determine what direction he is supposed to go in. He is only waiting for the Lord to reveal his direction. He knows that there is nowhere worth going unless God makes it obvious."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elijah said the same message to the king that the messengers said to the king. Nothing has changed. I don't know what the king was hoping to accomplish here, but he certainly wasn't able to accomplish it. I think he was trying to prove his own strength, but in so doing he had made himself look weaker and weaker. Every captain and the fifty men that followed show the attempts of strength this world tries to muster up against the Lord, but those attempts are all feeble and useless. The Lord (alone) is the strength of mankind, and without Him we are like Chihuahuas barking at ghosts. If you've ever owned a Chihuahua or know someone that has, then you know exactly what I'm talking about. They are all bark and no bite, and if they DO (for some reason) bite, it would not be that hard to punt the little fellow. They are not very strong dogs. They are just loud. The world is not strong, they are just loud."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"title":"Jehoram Reigns","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We can get into the deeper spiritual implications of Ahaziah by reading through Chronicles, but it's sufficient to say that Ahaziah was not a good king. Ahaziah didn't have any children, so they had to elect a new lineage as king. They elected this man named \"Jehoram.\" \"Jehoram\" means \"Jehovah is exalted.\" With a name like that, you'd think that he would focus the people back on the Lord! Could this be the northern kingdom's chance at redemption?"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the brother of Ahaziah. Ahab had two sons, and this man will obviously be no different from his lineage. He will continue to bring shame upon Israel by his leading. He will NOT live up to his name, sadly. He will exalt himself above the Lord, and it will do nothing but bring more pain and suffering on his lineage, as well as the people he is leading. It makes you wonder how he got a name like this. Well, I think it has to do with Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat has a son whose name is similar. But I'm getting ahead of myself."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18}]},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we picked the book of 2 Kings right back up where we last left off in 1 Kings. We see the rule of Ahaziah, and it is ended short from a fall that he takes. He goes to seek a fake god for some wisdom, and Elijah stops him short and explains to him what will really happen to him because he has not turned to the God of Israel. Obviously Ahaziah doesn't like this and tries to intimidate Elijah by sending over one hundred and fifty men to bring him to the king. The first one hundred and two try and force Elijah, but the Lord deals with them quickly. The last fifty-one plead with Elijah, and Elijah relays the same message to the king in person."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we pick back up with Elijah training his replacement Elisha. This chapter has plenty of twists and turns, and it is such an astounding chapter of miracles that it almost seems hard for the reader to believe. It's written in the word of God though, so we can be assured that it is true."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"A Test of Faith","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's pause for a moment. Was there a time when this was mentioned before? Of course, the writer knows about this and is foretelling the coming events. He is preparing the reader for what is about to happen. The writer knows this is going to take place because it has already taken place. But we will see in the coming verses that OTHERS know that it will take place BEFORE it actually comes to pass. For some reason, they have found out about it in some way, and we are just now learning about it today. It's a strange thing to consider. I feel like this is really important knowledge for us to get quite a while back. I'm curious to know how these people knew about it."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord has been working on Elijah. He has also made it clear that Elijah was supposed to teach and prepare Elisha for the ministry after Elijah is gone. We are told that Elisha will take Elijah's place, but we are not told how that will happen (until this chapter). So why would Elijah tell Elisha to stay? Well, I think Elijah was preparing Elisha in this moment. Whether on purpose or not isn’t clear, but I think this will help strengthen Elisha's faith. Elijah will do this to Elisha before every city they go to, to TEST Elisha's desire for ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a true test, by the way. If someone tells you to stay away from your God-given calling, do you do it? Pastors and ministers have made it quite difficult for me (personally) to do ministry in the church. Yet the Lord has blessed me with opportunities OUTSIDE of the church to do what He has called me to do. It's sad that the church wouldn't equip and encourage ministers (except for the few selected who meet their qualifications). That is not what the church is supposed to be about. Elisha will either have to follow the Lord's prompting or follow Elijah's holding back."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Bethel is an interesting place to stop at first. It means \"House of God\" if you don't remember. The first stop that Elijah makes on this journey through training is God's house. I think that is a good place to start every believer in training if I can be honest. God's house should be a place of equipping and preparation for God's people. It hardly is today, but that is where it should start."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are assured (in this verse) that there are several people who know about the prophecy of Elijah being taken up and taken away from this world. These \"sons of the prophets\" were told some stories about what was supposed to happen to Elijah. Whether they actually believed it or not is unclear. I assume they probably did it in a mocking way. They were ALSO (unknowingly) testing the faith of Elisha. Elisha was going to have to deal with critics if he was going to follow the Lord. That's just the life of a minister (sadly)."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jericho\" means \"its moon.\" Remember, Jericho was a place that was destroyed so many years ago. It was a place that God did not want to exist. Joshua and the Israelites took it down. It has been rebuilt quite recently (in 1 Kings in fact), and Elijah wants to pass through this way. He, once again, tests Elisha and tells him to stay."},{"kind":"para","text":"After being prepared in the house of God, we are to be like the moon. We are to reflect the Lord's light. Jericho is an interesting name for such a dark place. But that is what a Christian is to do. He is supposed to reflect the light of the Lord in the darkest of places, and the most evil of times. Elijah has had to do that for quite some time now (alone), and Elisha will have to learn to do that same thing if he wants to follow the Lord when Elijah is gone."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's a repeat in every city. Elisha likely noticed it at this point. He's probably thinking, \"Do these people talk to each other or something? Why do they keep asking me this?\" Of course (as we have already illustrated), this was a test for Elisha's faith stamina. He will have to bear the burden of people's foolish questions and lack of faith. I don't believe these sons of the prophets really believed in what was going to take place. They are just tempting Elisha. The prophets themselves probably really believed it though. Unless they were false prophets. But how do they know about the future events of Elijah if they are false prophets?"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jordan\" means \"descender.\" There has been one who has descended. It was not the people of this Earth. It was God Himself. He came in human flesh. He descended from His throne, from eternity, and lived among us. Because of this, we now have a friend (a brother) who walks along with us in ministry. Elijah felt alone, but he always had the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Jordan River had always been an example of God's descent to mankind. It has always been an image of new life. That is the image being represented to Elisha as well. He started off in the house of God, then reflected the light of God, and is now walking with the Descender."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The sons of the prophets are looking onward to see if it's real. They don't quite believe, though, because they are not going closer than the Jordan. They are just onlookers. They have secondhand information. That is the difference between the knowledge of the two, by the way. We have secondhand information of this event. We can only believe that scripture is accurately displaying the truth in the events about to come to pass."},{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha, on the other hand, has firsthand information about this event from the Lord personally. He is told by God that this is what will take place. He will know that it is to come to pass because God has said it directly to him. The sons of the prophets were just going to have to trust their parents’ information. That's our choice today, by the way. We can get secondhand information about the Lord from our pastors and priests, and Bible teachers. OR, we can come directly to the Lord and get the information from His own mouth written down in His OWN Word."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Every time they pass this river, God seems to do a miracle of dividing the water. I think the river itself illustrates sin. God provides safe passage through sin, safely to the other side, if we are willing to follow that path. Elijah has the power to stop the flow of water and pass over the Jordan safely, and anyone that follows will also pass through with him to safety. We have the ability to walk across dry ground in a world full of sin rushing every way around us. We might be splashed from time to time, but the Lord is good about pulling us out of the water."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said, Come. And when Peter was come down out of the ship, he walked on the water, to go to Jesus. But when he saw the wind boisterous, he was afraid; and beginning to sink, he cried, saying, Lord, save me. And immediately Jesus stretched forth his hand, and caught him, and said unto him, O thou of little faith, wherefore didst thou doubt?","ref":"Matthew 14:29-31"}]}]},{"start":9,"end":14,"title":"Elijah Taken","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we started off in 1 Kings, we see Solomon address a similar question from the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In Gibeon the LORD appeared to Solomon in a dream by night: and God said, Ask what I shall give thee.","ref":"1 Kings 3:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this instance, it is not the Lord specifically who is asking Elisha about what he wants. It's Elijah. But if you haven't noticed, Elijah has such a close connection to the Lord that his word holds some authority. I think that is great news for Elisha. This means that whatever Elisha asks for will be met by a stamp of righteousness by the Lord. Of course, what Elisha is asking for will be under the umbrella of righteous living and used for the Lord's glory. He wants a \"double portion\" of the spirit of Elijah."},{"kind":"para","text":"The spirit of Elijah is not much different from the spirit of Solomon. It's the spirit of Wisdom. Wisdom is Jesus Christ. I know we have spoken pretty heavily about this already. We can see Christ in the Proverbs of Solomon, and we can see Wisdom whenever we read the gospels of Christ. Wisdom and Jesus are one and the same. So Elisha is asking for a DOUBLE portion of Wisdom. That's an astounding thing! How can anyone top the wisdom of God? How can Elisha have a DOUBLE portion of it? We will see that it is up to the Lord to provide the means here."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's a hard thing for Elijah to give him, because it was not in Elijah's control. It had to be in the Lord's control. We had spoken about looking and living a few chapters back. That is the case for Elisha here. If he sees the Lord's prophet being taken up, then he is going to be granted his request. That is up to the Lord to reveal."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems to happen without any sort of preparation. They were in the middle of talking when this all came to pass. They knew it was coming, but they didn't know where or when. That is the way in which the Lord will come to us. He will come quickly. I suppose if there is a way I would like to go from this Earth, it would be from chariots of fire and a whirlwind. What an extravagant way to exit this Earth. Elisha was pushed away from Elijah by the chariot and horses. It's such a unique thing for the Lord to send."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why horses? Why a chariot? Why not angels or just a simple ball of fire? Elijah was good at reigning fire down, but they were not chariots of fire that were falling. I assume they are balls of fire coming down. So why a chariot and horse of fire? I... I don't know... I seriously couldn't tell you why it was in this symbol that they were separated. The whirlwind (to me) makes sense. That was the lifestyle that Elijah lived. He was taken in the same manner that he portrayed in his prophecy. One commentator pointed out the similarities of the ministry of Moses and the ministry of Elijah, saying, \"Both Moses and Elijah stood alone for righteousness. They were associated with fire upon mountains. They were associated with the desert. They met God on Sinai. They were chased out of their countries by pagan rulers. They knew God's miraculous provision for food and water. They wandered in the desert for a period measured by forty. They fasted for forty days. They were powerful examples of praying men. They parted waters. They had close associates who succeeded them. They had successors who parted waters also. They had mysterious or strange deaths.\" Enduring Word Commentary But the chariots of fire is what will grasp the attention of Elisha:"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Every word of God is used for US (the readers)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"All scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness: That the man of God may be perfect, throughly furnished unto all good works.","ref":"2 Timothy 3:16-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, this is for the sake of Elisha as well. This happened in this way to grab the attention of Elisha. God's power is blatantly and obviously revealed. But scripture is given to perfect and furnish the reader too. So how does this apply to us? Perhaps we can view this and say, “Well, God's power is immensely shown here in this moment, and if God's Word is accurate and true, we can be assured that THIS is what Elisha saw.\" Perhaps the chariots of fire and the horsemen are symbols of God's power and moving. His chariot moves us, and his horsemen guides us. He is the author of our direction and purpose. His presence is made obvious to the world, but it's up to the world to accept or reject it."},{"kind":"para","text":"If Elisha simply saw a normal horse and chariot, the image would not be quite as powerful. We would see it and say, “Well, that could simply be anyone out in the wilderness with them.\" But, if you view them on FIRE, that's a little bit of a different story. It certainly gives new meaning to the song title \"Swing Low Sweet Chariot.\" Interestingly enough, Elisha has now torn his own clothes in half. Hopefully, he has something underneath to cover him. He is not going to be very presentable when he returns to civilization."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Mantle\" means \"cloak.\" So at least he has Elijah's mantle to cover him if anything. The tearing of his garments is a pretty prominent sign that what Elisha saw was quite astounding. It was too much for him to bear. That is a symbol of ministry if I have ever seen one. Ministry is quite too much for a man to bear alone. He is going to face some seriously heavy trials and burdens. Ministry could easily make me tear my clothes in half if I allowed it. God's immense and obvious presence would certainly push me over the edge. Taking the mantle of Elijah is also a very important symbol for Elisha. He was literally taking the mantle of Elijah and spiritually taking Elijah's mantle. Perhaps that is where the saying comes from."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":18,"title":"Students Don't Believe","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They could tell there was something different about Elisha when he returned. They may have had doubts before, but they were met with a change that made it obvious to them. Elisha's countenance and spirit just seemed different to them. They might have even seen Elisha part the waters to come back and knew that was a symbol of Elijah's spirit. But they still don't fully believe."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha said, \"There is no body to be found. God took Elijah. There's nothing for you to do. God is very clear that this was the prophecy, and you will have to take God's Word for it or suffer the consequences.\" The students will not listen to Elisha, though:"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They would not let up. So Elisha gives them what they wanted. It was not for their own benefit, though. There was nothing to be found. They needed to trust the Lord in faith. They had learned nothing about faith. There is only so much you can learn from teachers of God's Word, but one thing you certainly CAN'T learn is how to have faith in God. We can tell you what faith is, but it will always be a personal thing between you and the Lord. No one else can build or interfere with your faith."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He had warned them to stay away. They were not willing to listen. You might say, \"But in verse 17 it says that he let them go. His exact words were 'send.' So Elisha is lying here.\" No, he told them over and over again that they were wasting their time. They simply needed to believe. That was not sufficient for them, though. He says, \"I have no reason to lie to you; you should have just believed.\""}]}]},{"start":19,"end":22,"title":"A Water Miracle","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha's new power is going to be put to the test in this moment. He has the ability to help this city. According to the men of the city, the city was a peaceful place. It was a place that didn't have quarrels or problems. That was simply not true. Perhaps there were nice and pleasant people there, but that city was accursed with false prophets as well. They were in a place where idolatry was still a problem. That city (Jericho) was an idol itself. Yet, the miracles of God can certainly get the attention of people and help their faith."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Recognize that Elisha gives the glory to God here. It was not his own personal power that is fixing these waters. It is the power of God doing it. That is important to note. If a prophet of the Lord does not give the Lord recognition, you could safely bet that he is a false prophet. That applies to our preachers today as well."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, the water became sweet. I don't really know. I DO know, however, that adding salt to water doesn't usually make it sweet. Yet that seems to be what happens here. The water was bitter, and the Lord changed it to sweet."},{"kind":"para","text":"View this in the workings of your soul. Perhaps you have a bitter spirit today. The Lord can make your spirit sweet if you would allow Him. He's just going to have to pour a little salt on it first. That is not pleasant. That is not easy. If you have poured salt on a wound, you know it is painful. That is what God will do to an afflicted spirit. It will heal quicker, but it will be painful in the process."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the second miracle of Elisha, and he had just been handed the mantle that week. It should be obvious to everyone at that point that the Lord was with him. It won't be all that obvious to them, though... The miracle of changing water is an interesting touch. We know that one of the first recorded miracles of the Lord is turning water into wine. God is good at changing stale, bitter, and simple water into something glorious for His use."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":25,"title":"Another Miracle","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Bethel was the first place he went to on his learning journey with Elijah, remember? He's returning to this place. He's going back to the House of God. He had descended, he had reflected the Lord's light, and now he is back in God's house. But God's house is full of people who are anything but God's children. Look how they treat him. They mock his bald head. These “little children” are being trained in evil ways. They have teachers and leaders around them that are not raising up their children properly."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it.","ref":"Proverbs 22:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"These men were throwing schoolyard insults at the prophet. Now, I suppose this is not something that should really irritate the prophet, but they were not leaving him alone. They were just following along and trying to irritate the prophet. So this is how the prophet reacts:"}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is crazy! Apparently, there were forty-two people throwing these insults at him. It was not as if it were a couple. Forty-two evil and cruel childlike men. Was this the answer to the problem? I don't really know. This (to me) seems a bit much for the situation."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've had my fair share of unruly children do stupid things to me and break my belongings, but I never imagined sicking a bear on them. This seems way too far for the Lord to allow. There has to be more to this, right? \"Little\" means \"young\" or \"insignificant.\" “Children\" means \"a boy\" or \"servant.\" McGhee’s commentary points out that this same word is used to describe “Isaac when he was twenty-eight, Joseph when he was thirty-nine, the Sodomites who attacked the home of Lot.\" He goes on to say the word for \"Young men\" in 1 Kings 12:8 is the same Hebrew word for \"Little children\" in this portion of scripture. They are synonymous with each other. These COULD be little kids, but it seems quite harsh if it is. Over the top and way too dramatic. Psychopathic even. The rest of scripture makes it very clear that the Lord really takes care of children."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Lo, children are an heritage of the LORD: and the fruit of the womb is his reward.","ref":"Psalm 127:3"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, ye fathers, provoke not your children to wrath: but bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord.","ref":"Ephesians 6:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And all thy children shall be taught of the LORD; and great shall be the peace of thy children.","ref":"Isaiah 54:13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them. But Jesus said, Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven. And he laid his hands on them, and departed thence.","ref":"Matthew 19:13-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, if we found this to be suitable for little children, we should question our faith and ethics. To kill forty-two children because they are throwing out insults is the weakest thing someone can do. When we hear about school shootings today, the majority of the world is disgusted by the prospect. Yet, there are people who justify the actions of the prophet here. If you are a Christian and you find that killing children is okay, there is something mentally wrong with you. That's why I don't believe these \"little children\" were literally \"little children.\" They were certainly ACTING like kids, but they were young men. They knew what was good and bad, and they were mocking Elisha for absolutely no reason other than to get on his nerves. Even in the letters of John, we see him call the church members “Little children.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I write unto you, little children, because your sins are forgiven you for his name's sake. I write unto you, fathers, because ye have known him that is from the beginning. I write unto you, young men, because ye have overcome the wicked one. I write unto you, little children, because ye have known the Father. I have written unto you, fathers, because ye have known him that is from the beginning. I have written unto you, young men, because ye are strong, and the word of God abideth in you, and ye have overcome the wicked one.","ref":"1 John 2:12-14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye are of God, little children, and have overcome them: because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world.","ref":"1 John 4:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"My little children, let us not love in word, neither in tongue; but in deed and in truth.","ref":"1 John 3:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously they weren’t actually little children. They were acting like it though."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Carmel\" means \"garden-land.\" It's a place that Elisha probably visited to refresh himself in the Lord. He needed the refreshment of God after everything that had just taken place. We all need a \"garden-land\" to visit from time to time. We need a place where we can stop and just sit with God and in His beauty. Then he went to Samaria. He had some work to do there. \"Samaria\" means \"watch mountain.\" He is back where he came from. There will be a lot to address in this land, but we will have to wait for the coming chapters to see what happens next."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":25}]},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we got a deeper look at the ministry of Elijah, as it is being passed off to Elisha. We see how the ministry starts, and it's met with a lot of doubt and questions from people throughout the land they travel through. Elijah finally asks what he can do for Elisha before he is taken away, and Elisha request a double portion of Elijah's spirit. Then Elijah is taken in a whirlwind into Heaven never to be seen again. It seems pretty evident (as we read) that the spirit of Elijah had been given to Elisha, and we will see Elisha do some pretty amazing things."},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot forget that these books ARE about the kings though. They focus heavily on the leadership of Israel, but the prophets of Israel are an important part of the direction of the kings. While the kings try and make up their own directions, God is still moving and working things out to get them on course for the coming of the Messiah. Israel will eventually be in a position where they will have NO CHOICE but to lean on the Lord, and seek out His salvation. Sadly, there are people during the time of Christ who LIKE living in their bondage and captivity, and so they fight against Christ. But Christ will free those who accept Him and His offering to mankind. This chapter will see some war, but a lot of obvious provision from the Lord as these kings try and figure out their next moves."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Jehoram Was Evil","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judging by the last few kings, twelve seems to be a pretty round number for the northern kingdom. Even that isn't very long though. Jehoshaphat was on his eighteenth year as king of the southern kingdom. But the real question is, how did he reign? Was he finally a good king? With a name like Jehoram, he surely must be a good king, right?"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Jehoram was an evil king, but he was not as evil as Ahab. That is a bit of a relief. That is an interesting dynamic, isn't it? He can do the right thing and still have the wrong heart. It's true for the sinner today. There are a lot of well-meaning men and women in the church. There are plenty of religious ideologies that seem to be well-meaning. I think a lot of what the Catholic Church teaches is well-meaning. They want to do the right thing, keep the right laws, and perform the right rituals, but they do it for the wrong reasons. They do it out of tradition (something the Lord continuously makes sure to let us know He hates)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the rudiments of the world, and not after Christ.","ref":"Colossians 2:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he answered and said unto them, Why do ye also transgress the commandment of God by your tradition?","ref":"Matthew 15:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"They do it out of necessity to gain salvation (which is the whole purpose of Jesus' death on the cross);"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost; Which he shed on us abundantly through Jesus Christ our Saviour; That being justified by his grace, we should be made heirs according to the hope of eternal life.","ref":"Titus 3:5-7"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast.","ref":"Ephesians 2:8-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"They do it without a real heart for the Lord;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Samuel said, Hath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the LORD? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams.","ref":"1 Samuel 15:22"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Peter and the other apostles answered and said, We ought to obey God rather than men.","ref":"Acts 5:29"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Know ye not, that to whom ye yield yourselves servants to obey, his servants ye are to whom ye obey; whether of sin unto death, or of obedience unto righteousness?","ref":"Romans 6:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Tradition itself is not bad, gaining salvation is not bad, working for the Lord is not bad, but all of these things must be drenched in your love for the Lord. You can go back and take some time to read the verses around these verses mentioned, and you will see that LOVE is the theme. Love for the Lord. That is what should drive a person. So while Jehoram may have taken the altars of Baal down, he still had no real love for God, and THAT was still a very big problem."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jeroboam didn't worship Baal, but he still worshipped fake gods, and one was a calf. That was probably what Jehoram was worshipping as well. He still didn't turn to the Lord of Israel."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":12,"title":"Moab Rebels","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I guess Mesha was trying (at some point) to be a confederate with Israel. You don't just give your enemy one hundred thousand sheep and rams because you hate them. He thought he could help the king of Israel, and they could work together. The kings, so far, have been evil enough to catch the likings of Moab. Moab was not in a good place, obviously. They must have liked the direction that Israel was going in. You attract the wrong friends when you are doing the wrong things."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahab was the worst king that north Israel had ever had, and it was with Ahab that Moab wanted to befriend. Now that they didn't have Ahab, Israel wasn't bad enough for them. Now Israel can become enemies with Moab again. I'm sure there was more to this picture here than just this. I'm sure the king of Moab probably had more of a reason to commence war with Israel than the fact that Ahab was simply dead. I assume Moab felt a bit of a liberation when Ahab died. They might have been in a position of forced tribute. Ahab required that Moab pay them, and when Ahab was finally dead, they felt like they had the power to remove themselves from the thumb of Israel. The kings to follow didn't like it, and so now they are at war. That would make the most sense to me anyway. It was kind of post-apocalyptic in a way. Everyone was out for themselves, and the more dangerous the world got, the more evil the leaders became."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoram is preparing the people for war. He is not going to let Moab go that easily."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoshaphat should have stayed out of it. The northern kingdom was no good for the southern kingdom. But Jehoshaphat is simply a man. He is a sinner just like you and me. He makes dumb mistakes. He had made a dumb mistake with Ahab too, and had already almost gotten himself killed once before for this family. But Jehoshaphat wants to be a help to his own people, so he's going out to battle with them."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Edom has been working with Israel for a bit now. That was not a good thing. Edom will not stay in sweet fellowship with Israel. They will eventually betray Judah. We can look at that in Obadiah."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Seven days without water was going to be a taxing thing on these people and their livestock. If they wanted to continue the journey forward, they would need to take care of themselves and their animals. Strangely enough, Edom didn't have a king. Or at least we are told they don't have a king."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"There was then no king in Edom: a deputy was king.","ref":"1 Kings 22:47"},{"kind":"para","text":"So where did this guy come from? Perhaps they have appointed a king since this was spoken. Or maybe they have appointed a king for this specific cause and battle. They want to try and remain on Israel's good side for as long as possible."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a strange and sudden change of heart from Jehoram. He was never concerned about the thoughts of the Lord before, but he suddenly gains the intellect to think that maybe this is a bad idea. Maybe the Lord will use this against him. So far, every decision the kings have made has been a poor one. The Lord is looking for a merciful king. A king that will turn to Him and request what HE wants. That has not been what the kings of Israel have been doing. They couldn't care less about what the Lord thinks. At least they couldn't until now. He has finally come to realize that God might not actually be on his side in this moment, and he might be leading these other fine leaders straight into captivity."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoshaphat was the voice of reason the last time the king of Israel did something stupid. Ahab brought a prophet of the Lord, and neither of them listened to the prophet. Why would Jehoshaphat listen this time with Elisha? Elijah was able to grasp the attention of his hearers, though, and if Elisha has a double portion of the spirit and prophetic words of Elijah, then Elisha should have no problems getting these three kings to pay attention here."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a smart move. They wasted seven days getting to this point, but at least they won't waste their lives before going in. They will find out what the Lord thinks on the matter before they move forward, and they would be wise to accept God's Word as final authority."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":20,"title":"God's Provision","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha doesn't want to deal with Jehoram. He thinks that this is all just a ruse towards Elisha. He is under the impression that Jehoram is just going to be upset about what the Lord says anyway, and they are just going to reject it for their own personal gods. That has been the theme with the kings anyway. Why all of a sudden would the buck stop here? And, as much as I hate to say it, Jehoram might be right in this moment. It was the Lord that reveals this small glimpse of doubt to the king. He has been trying to get the king’s attention for quite some time now, and they have not recognized the Lord for it. So this is a first for the kings of Israel."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The only reason that Elisha is even speaking to the king of Israel today is because Jehoshaphat was there. I believe the Lord allowed that to take place specifically to get Elisha to speak his mind on the matter. He needed a genuine sign that the Lord was addressing this situation, and Jehoshaphat's presence was that sign. Jehoshaphat SHOULDN'T have been there, but he is nevertheless, and he is going to have to pay close attention to what Elisha says in this moment, because it's from the Lord directly."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting thing to do. Music certainly has an essence to it. It has some spiritual presence to it. I've been learning a lot of this lately. Saul felt better when there was music playing for him. Elisha used music to get into a deeper state with the Lord. Music is a powerful thing that can help even the most hurt of hearts recover quickly. The music for Elisha was a way to calm Elisha and focus better. I think Elisha could have done this in several ways, but this might have just been the way in which Elisha WANTED to do it."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, they haven't had anything to drink for seven days now. They are running close to empty. Somehow, God is going to miraculously provide some water to the people. They will have an abundance of water without rain to fill up these man-made ditches. That will be the first sign that the Lord is speaking and moving in this matter. If they don't believe in the Lord after that first sign, the chances are pretty slim that they will believe in the Lord for any of the rest of the plan."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Moabites won't succeed today. They will be back. There is a future purpose for the Moabites to the Lord. We can read all about that in Isaiah. It's not a fun sight though. It's not a fun sight for anyone in those days really. But to the Lord, taking care of these things is nothing of a burden. It's quite simple to Him honestly. Taking care of YOUR needs today is a light thing in the sight of the Lord. He is able to take care of your needs and go above and BEYOND for you as well. It's whatever He sees fit."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the type of victory that they will receive. But the first step was to dig ditches. They are going to have to trust that the Lord is going to provide. If the Lord provides the water, then He is certainly going to provide the victory. May we remember that wells represent God's Word. Any time we see a well of water, we can rightfully associate it with God's Word. In this case, if God provides His Word, He is certainly going to provide the victory."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It just came rushing in as if a damn broke. They had exactly what they needed, and it was something of a miracle! This should astound all the people. There was no reason for this to take place other than to prove that the Lord was actually with them. This should turn every person's heart towards the Lord."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":27,"title":"The Moabites Spoil","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Moabites felt pretty confident that they could take on these three kings. I don't know why they felt so confident, but they were not willing to simply surrender. This was their freedom they were fighting for anyway. They didn't want to be under the rule of Israel anymore. They wanted to live in their own land and do their own things. Why wouldn't God allow that for them? Well, Moab was a very evil place. We have studied the evils of Moab several times over, and we can be assured that nothing has changed for them. Evil needed to be taken care of. The Lord ALWAYS takes care of evil. Even in His own children."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Moabites were tricked by the water. They thought that the kings had slaughtered each other, and their blood covered the water that was placed there by the Lord. So they were running into enemy territory to take the spoil, but this will just lead them into capture. The Lord literally handed the Moabites over to Israel."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It happens just as the Lord says. What a shocker. This will surely get the king’s attention towards the Lord, right? This will give him a changed heart? I suppose we will find out in the chapters to come."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king of Moab was still trying his best to win the battle, but there was nothing he could do. They were foolish to think that they had received a victory without even entering battle to begin with. They thought the kings had already been killed somehow, and without even taking a second thought, they thought they could just rob Israel. They should have just gone back to Moab, but obviously the spoils of war are what kept them going."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Woah, this was a twist of an ending. The king of Moab takes his oldest son and lights him on fire to a false god as a sacrifice. This goes to show how far deep the Moabites’ idolatry really was. The KING (that is the highest power in Moab) sacrificed his own son to a fake god in the middle of war in hopes that they would gain a victory. That's pretty evil right there."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":27}]},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we get the beginning ministry works of Elisha as he helps the three kings (Northern king, king of Judah, and king of Edom) resolve their problem with Moab running away from their slave labor. The Lord provides them with water and victory through the prophet Elisha. You'd think that would be enough for every person (especially the kings) to turn to the Lord but they really don't."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we are getting a bit of a break from the kings, and looking at the ministry and miracles of Elisha. He is a man that is greatly used of God during a time in the history of Israel where people were running into all sorts of problems. Everyone was confused and lost. They were like lost sheep. They didn't want to turn to the shepherd, and Elisha is a man that is tasked with trying to get their attentions back on the Lord."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Fill The Pots","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha must have known this family. I suppose he knew that the husband was a godly man. Apparently, there was some debt he did not have the chance to pay, and now she was in danger of losing her two sons because of it. She got worried and went to the man of God for help. Honestly, that is where Christians should be able to go. It is sad that Christians cannot go to their church for help these days. There are not a lot of churches that want to help their congregants financially. They usually have the means, but they don't want to use it for that. They want to use it for the lost."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is another example of doing the right thing with the wrong heart. Giving to the lost and poor is a right thing, but taking care of the house of God is far more important. If you are more concentrated on taking care of the world first, you have your priorities out of whack. God's children come first. I will not sway on that conviction until my death. The Lord takes care of His own before He takes care of the rest of the world. That is an example for every pastor, every father, and every teacher. If God has blessed you with people, you MUST take care of them."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A pot of oil seems useless. Elisha wanted to see what kind of miracle could be performed by the Lord in this instance. Maybe she could sell some stuff to make up for the money for these collectors. But that wasn't enough. One single pot of oil was not going to cover the costs of anything."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is such a strange task given to this woman. She has one pot of oil, and Elisha is asking her to go get a bunch of empty pots from her neighbors and pour that ONE pot into all the empty pots and put aside the pots that were full. If I were her, I would have said, \"Okay... The pot that is currently full is the pot that I can set aside. Obviously, if I pour this out into another pot, then the pot that is full will be empty, and the OTHER pot will be full.\" None of this made any sense logically. But spiritually, there is a lot happening here. She was going to have to trust that Elisha knew something she didn't, and she is going to just have to act by faith. That is what the Lord wants, by the way. No matter how foolish the task may seem, faith is the only means that God will work with."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Somehow, she used her single pot of oil and was able to fill up all the other pots that she borrowed with that ONE pot of oil. This is quite the miracle. I'm sure after she filled two pots she thought, \"Wait... How is that possible? I still have more oil.\" Then she filled three... four... five... six pots and she STILL had oil. ALL the pots were FULL. The Lord was filling her up with more than she thought possible."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She had enough to pay the debt and more leftover to live off of for her future. That is quite the miracle! That is the prayer for every child of God in debt today. God is good at paying debts. We might not know how He will do it, but He always fills our pots with oil."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":17,"title":"A Child for Shunem","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shunem\" means \"double resting place.\" This was a place for people to rest, and it seems that it was certainly a place for Elisha to rest. He had a lady there that wanted to feed him. I think that is a very nice thing to do for the man of God. Sometimes that is what a man of God needs. A lot of the time, the pastor (if he's any good) is busy taking other people out to eat, and rarely is ever given the opportunity to be fed himself. Elisha was given a time of rest and food. Godly leaders need that sometimes. More often than I think they even know."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She wanted to give him a better spot for rest. She was dedicating a whole area for this godly prophet. She didn't want him to just sit and eat bread. She wanted him to actually lie down and rest. She wanted to give him a space in her home, as if they were family together. It's a little odd if I'm being honest. It's also a very sweet thing to consider. She was very compassionate towards this man of God. She was insistent on this idea because she liked the fact that God's presence would be in her home. That is a good thing for the whole world to desire. But would Elisha like this idea? Let's find out:"}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He seems to like it! I mean, he didn't question it any further than that. He came by like usual (clockwork), and the woman said, \"Look, we made a place for you to rest a little longer and easier if you would like!\" He thought, \"I'll give it a try. I really appreciate your hospitality to me.\" And so, now he's lying there, and a thought comes to mind:"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He says, “You have been quite kind to me. What is it that I can do for YOU in return for this kindness? Is there something that you need that I can take care of? Would you like to be in the presence of the king or the captain, because I can make that happen?” Her response is a humble one. She wants to stay with her own people. She has no need to speak to royalty. She doesn't need anything lavish. There is something she would like to request though if he feels obliged to answer:"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So this lady is giving Elisha his privacy, and he is calling out to his servant and asking questions, and he is responding according to her answers. The problem she had was that she was not able to have any children. Again (we've mentioned this many times), children were a measure of wealth to the women of that land in those days. The more children one had, the richer they were in the eyes of the world (and in their own eyes). She just wanted a child, and she didn't know how that would come to be because her husband was old. So Elisha wants to talk face to face with her."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She doesn't understand how that could possibly come to pass. She thought, \"Perhaps he misunderstood. There was no way for me to have a child because my husband is old. How can I have a child with this man?\" So she is humbly asking that this prophet not lie to her about this matter. She has been kind to him, and she did not want her kindness to be abused by false hope from this man of God."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is quite the miracle. It didn't matter the age of her husband, they had a child, just as Elisha had promised. It happened according to the Word of God! You'd think, \"What a beautiful story! God is so good and gracious and kind! That is a good way for this lady to be treated!\" You are right. It's a GREAT way for this lady's care for the man of God to be treated. But the story doesn't end there..."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":31,"title":"The Child Dies","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This took a quick turn. They had quite a bit of time with this young boy, and he suddenly had a head injury. How this took place, we are not sure. Maybe he was in the wrong place at the wrong time, and one of the reapers accidentally nicked him on the head? Maybe he just had some sort of tumor that took his life too soon. I have no idea, but this family was surely distraught at this moment. The one blessing that this woman had been waiting for was taken from her in an instant. I can feel the devastation in the pages..."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She knew that God could do something about this. The boy was (no doubt) dead. But God is capable of anything. She just needed to go see the man of God. She needed to see what he advised. He had to help her boy. He had to do something about this. How cruel of the Lord to dangle this blessing over them and then take it away in an instant. This was certainly a testing of faith for this woman and this family. This was going to stretch her faith quite thin, but it will increase her faith when she receives the answer from the man of God."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, it was only specific times when Elisha came to visit, and this was not one of those times. Her husband was thinking logically, but she was thinking spiritually. It didn't matter if there was a holy day for the man of God; every day should be treated as the Lord's day. That is ANOTHER theme found throughout Scripture. It isn't about worshipping the Lord on the Sabbath alone. It's not about worshipping Him once in a new moon. It's about doing His will every day. This mom needed a man of God to give direction, and I can't blame her for trying to pull out all the stops to help save her son. That is what moms are good at. Moms are always willing to go very far out of their way to save their children."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Full speed ahead! She's not going to take precautions; she is just going to go. The faster she can go, the more likely she is to catch him. Elisha had a routine. That's important to note for a pastor. People can expect the man of God to be in specific places, at specific times, for specific reasons that have to do with the Lord. If you have a pastor (or God forbid you are a pastor) that is sporadic and chooses to do ministry chaotically, or is someone that is hard to get to, you are not a trustworthy minister. No one will be able to depend on you."},{"kind":"para","text":"Samuel had a routine, and people could find him. Elijah had a routine, and people could find him. Isaiah will have a routine where people can find him. Jeremiah will have a routine where people can find him. The disciples will have a routine where people can find them. Jesus had a routine where people could find Him. Elisha will certainly have a routine, and this woman is going to find him."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He knew there had to be something wrong. He asked if the family is okay first and foremost. That should be the first question out of every minister’s mouth. Instead of her saying, \"No. My child is dead. I need your help.\" she simply says, \"It is well.\" Everyone reading this at this moment knows that nothing is well for this woman. She is still being humble. I think she is also understanding her faith in a deeper light. She is trusting that God is not going to leave this story here."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, God has not said anything to Elisha. Elisha seems to be a man with all the answers, and yet he is not aware of what is happening here. He believes that she is in some serious trouble, though, by her actions here. Instead of saying, \"Don't touch me!\" like his servant was about to, he has compassion on her and wants to know more. He instantly hurts with her."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She didn't even say what had happened. She spoke about her child, and that was enough for him to connect the dots. Something bad had happened. He is sending his servant to take care of this problem as quickly as possible. He is going to stay here with the woman and follow her back, while his servant rushes on ahead. He wants to minister to her before he gets back to her home and ministers to the rest of the family. Elisha was a bold man, but we can see deeper in this chapter that he was a tender-hearted man. He was a man that loved people like the Lord loved people. This woman deserved her needs to be met, and he was going to be directed by the Lord to meet those needs."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is going to minister to her. She needs the presence of God in this moment. The closest (she felt) that she could get to God was through Elisha the prophet. She knew that he was a man of God, and that he had direct contact with God, and that is why she trusts him so much in this moment. Sometimes that is what we need today. There are many of us who are hurting and striving for the Lord, who feel absent from the Lord, and we need a good Godly friend that we can spend some time with. We want the presence of God, even if it's through another person. Sometimes we need the example of a Godly person to see God's presence and learn how to obtain that. What a shame if we cannot find a Godly friend to minister to us in our lowest moments."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gehazi\" means \"Valley of Vision.\" He was a servant of Elisha, but he doesn't seem to have the same faith as Elisha. He wants a visual, it seems, and in Christianity, seeing isn't what happens first. Faith is the forefront for the Lord. Then He will let you see. Elisha's continual example is to get the people he is speaking to to test their own faith and lean on the Lord themselves with what they have. He usually doesn't get directly involved. He just teaches them to stretch their faith. He was doing that with his servant, but his servant gave up nearly instantly. It didn't work, so now he is going to tell Elisha that his faith isn't working. All hope is lost for this child."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":37,"title":"The Boy's Sneeze","entries":[{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think Elisha's faith was being tested a bit here. Elisha is still kind of awestruck by the fact that God did not tell him about this situation beforehand. He does know that God is more than able to bring this child back to life though, and he is not going to leave until the Lord reveals what needs to happen. He is trying to resuscitate him. This is still a common means of saving someone today. Usually, you wouldn't do this, though, on a corpse. But Elisha is giving it the age-old try. And it seems to be working. The body was getting warm. A dead body gets colder. This was good news."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is allergic to this world. Is there a significance to the seven sneezes? I won't draw any conclusions. I am not going to fancy the idea that every number mentioned in scripture is important to the theme of salvation. That would be a bit of a stretch for me to assume. Perhaps there is something deeper there, but the most important thing for the reader to note is that the Lord had healed this young boy. He was as sure as dead, and yet he is found sneezing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sneezing is usually not a good sign in a child. In this case, it very much is. It's a good thing that this child could sneeze at all, seeing as they had assumed he took his last breath. It's pretty common today for people to say \"bless you\" when they sneeze. I had an elementary school teacher’s helper tell the class that she didn't like that. She said, \"We are going to now say 'Welcome back' whenever someone sneezes.\" Everyone looked at her in confusion, and finally, one student raised his hand and asked why, and she said, \"Because when you sneeze, you stop breathing for a moment, and it's like you have died and come back to life.\" Whether or not she was right in her assessment of \"blessings\" is not up for me to judge, but perhaps she had a little bit of something there. It is interesting that we say \"bless you\" to sneezes. Even atheists bless a sneeze."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He didn't want to waste any time. He wanted this mother to be comforted as quickly as possible, and she certainly was going to be. Her boy was healed in a miraculous manner! If she was a Sadducee before, she has been converted now. That's a joke, of course."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She was too thankful for words, so her actions gestured it. She knew that God would do something. She just didn't know how it would be done. I believe Elisha could have easily sent this woman back to her home and prayed for the child. I think Elisha didn't need to be there, honestly. God would have healed this boy by faith. We know that He can do that because He does it in the New Testament."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The centurion answered and said, Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldest come under my roof: but speak the word only, and my servant shall be healed. For I am a man under authority, having soldiers under me: and I say to this man, Go, and he goeth; and to another, Come, and he cometh; and to my servant, Do this, and he doeth it. When Jesus heard it, he marvelled, and said to them that followed, Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Israel. And I say unto you, That many shall come from the east and west, and shall sit down with Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, in the kingdom of heaven. But the children of the kingdom shall be cast out into outer darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth. And Jesus said unto the centurion, Go thy way; and as thou hast believed, so be it done unto thee. And his servant was healed in the selfsame hour.","ref":"Matthew 8:8-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord didn't have to be present for the healing. In fact, that was the whole point of His healing. It was to lead people to faith in God, not just to get rid of their problems and diseases. That is all the world should be focused on. We should not focus on our suffering; we should be focusing on our faith in God. But Elisha came, and he would be a representation of the Lord's healing. He would be the one being used by the Lord, and it will be through him that this woman's faith will be made whole. Perhaps that was the final push she needed to believe in the Lord wholeheartedly."}]}]},{"start":38,"end":41,"title":"Poison Pottage","entries":[{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, we see the compassion of this prophet. He was not just a man of God; he was a man of ministry. He was down in the depths of the world’s despair. Whether or not these \"sons of the prophets\" are believers in the Lord is not well understood. I believe the term \"sons\" here means \"students.\" They were likely learning about God or a god. By the way in which Elisha has treated these men so far (and by the fact that they seem to know a lot about prophecy according to what would happen to Elijah in our previous chapter), I would assume they are students of the Lord. Elisha sees their need and is going to meet with them here to help and increase their faith at the same time. If there is \"dearth\" in the land, that means there is likely no crops. Yet, we see Elisha providing “pottage,\" which is a stew full of vegetables. You literally need crops to make pottage. That is quite the miracle if he plans on feeding all these people."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He found a \"wild gourd.\" That means it was poisonous. The man that gathered it likely didn't know. He just saw food and thought it would be great to help sustain them. Now he's guilty of poisoning the whole camp."}]},{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Something tasted off. Somehow they found out that one of the plants placed in that pot was going to kill them. Once again, they go to the man of God for help."}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Somehow, the meal that Elisha puts into the pot makes the pottage harmless. It got rid of the poison. I say “somehow,\" but I think we all know it was the Lord's doing. Take another note here, though; Elisha was having these men do the work themselves. They needed to learn from the Lord personally. He wasn't going to do anything for them that he knew they could personally do with the help of the Lord. The only thing he added himself was the meal to damper the poison. That is the picture we are getting. Man messes things up, and God makes things right. That's the story of Scripture from beginning to end."}]}]},{"start":42,"end":44,"title":"Five Loaves and Two Fish","entries":[{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Baalshalisha\" means \"thrice-great lord.\" If one lord was good, two lords are better and three lords are thrice-great... I guess... It doesn't sound like a pleasant place to live. This place is (no doubt) given wholly over to idolatry, and yet we have a single man coming with provisions for the people. Perhaps this is the only godly man in the whole city?"}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Servitor\" means \"minister\" or \"server.\" He brought five loaves and two fish. Well, actually, he brought bread of his first-fruits, twenty loaves of barley, and a few full ears of corn. Compared to the multitude, that doesn't seem like much. The people looked at Elisha and thought, \"What a waste. People are going to be fighting over this because there is certainly not enough for everyone.\" But Elisha was confident that there would be some sort of miracle of multiplying here."}]},{"start":44,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There were even leftovers. God will do this again and again. Every time the Lord does it, it doesn't seem to get the attention of anyone. Literally right after the Lord provides this miracle (twice) in front of His disciples, they are seen asking about food..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Which when Jesus perceived, he said unto them, O ye of little faith, why reason ye among yourselves, because ye have brought no bread? Do ye not yet understand, neither remember the five loaves of the five thousand, and how many baskets ye took up? Neither the seven loaves of the four thousand, and how many baskets ye took up? How is it that ye do not understand that I spake it not to you concerning bread, that ye should beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and of the Sadducees?","ref":"Matthew 16:8-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are all like this, honestly. God can provide again and again for us, and yet we still find reason to doubt His provision. We are too simple for the Lord's mercy."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":41},{"start":42,"end":44}]},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 05"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter saw quite a few miracles come from Elisha. This man certainly had his ministry cut out for him. He was travelling everywhere and meeting needs for people far and wide. He was a man that certainly (and obviously) had the heart and power fo the Lord in his life, and he was using it to showcase and display the presence of God, and the necessity for people to have faith in the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Elisha's reach extends a little further going into Syria. A man with leprosy desires healing, and he is told that the best man for that job would be Elisha, so he is going to be sent down to see if it's true. It's a mostly wholesome chapter, but it does not end on a high note..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Naaman's Leprosy","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Naaman\" means \"pleasantness.\" What a great name for a man. I assume he was a pleasant person to be around, just by the simple fact that this verse really talks him up. You are not called a \"great man with his master, and honourable\" unless that is truly what he was, and I assume the writer of this book thought quite highly of him as well. The Lord seems to bless him, but the one place the Lord DOESN'T bless him is in his leprosy. That is not something that you would want, especially back then. It was contagious, and it was deadly, and you were treated like a plague. They would lock you away and you would not be allowed to contact anyone. It is a common issue even into the coming of the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, as he went to Jerusalem, that he passed through the midst of Samaria and Galilee. And as he entered into a certain village, there met him ten men that were lepers, which stood afar off: And they lifted up their voices, and said, Jesus, Master, have mercy on us. And when he saw them, he said unto them, Go shew yourselves unto the priests. And it came to pass, that, as they went, they were cleansed. And one of them, when he saw that he was healed, turned back, and with a loud voice glorified God, And fell down on his face at his feet, giving him thanks: and he was a Samaritan. And Jesus answering said, Were there not ten cleansed? but where are the nine? There are not found that returned to give glory to God, save this stranger. And he said unto him, Arise, go thy way: thy faith hath made thee whole.","ref":"Luke 17:11-19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils: freely ye have received, freely give.","ref":"Matthew 10:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, the Lord allows this to happen to people for a bigger cause. But we will notice that God does not just let this happen and let people suffer. Actually, the Lord was pretty precise about what the priests were supposed to do for the lepers."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When the plague of leprosy is in a man, then he shall be brought unto the priest; And the priest shall see him: and, behold, if the rising be white in the skin, and it have turned the hair white, and there be quick raw flesh in the rising; It is an old leprosy in the skin of his flesh, and the priest shall pronounce him unclean, and shall not shut him up: for he is unclean. And if a leprosy break out abroad in the skin, and the leprosy cover all the skin of him that hath the plague from his head even to his foot, wheresoever the priest looketh; Then the priest shall consider: and, behold, if the leprosy have covered all his flesh, he shall pronounce him clean that hath the plague: it is all turned white: he is clean. But when raw flesh appeareth in him, he shall be unclean. And the priest shall see the raw flesh, and pronounce him to be unclean: for the raw flesh is unclean: it is a leprosy. Or if the raw flesh turn again, and be changed unto white, he shall come unto the priest; And the priest shall see him: and, behold, if the plague be turned into white; then the priest shall pronounce him clean that hath the plague: he is clean.","ref":"Leviticus 13:9-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"There would have to be moments of cleansing and waiting, but the priests were to be the ones to determine the course of action. They were to be \"doctors\" of sorts. That is not really what happens, though. They are more like officers. They just throw unclean people to the side and leave them to die alone. Naaman was not a man that deserved that kind of punishment, as we will soon see. We will also see that these other nations outside of Israel don't live by the same conduct. They never separate Naaman. They keep him working, and they act like everything is normal. But it is obviously hurting him:"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Syrians took a maid from Israel and gave her to Naaman as forced labor. Obviously (as we read in the last verse), this man was blessed by the Lord, and he was not even a follower of the Lord. But because of his dedication and his ability to think (remember God likes to get ahold of thinkers), he was a man ripe for God's use. This situation might seem bleak for this maid. I am assured that she did not want to be captured and used as a slave by anyone, especially the Syrians. She is taken out of her home country and used for a people that don't have any sort of Godly influence in their lives. But she could be the difference-maker coming from Israel."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She knows about the prophets of Israel. We can rightfully assume she is speaking about Elisha at this moment. Elisha was a man that was obviously used by the Lord, and all of Israel knew it. He could do some impossible things, and it wouldn't take much to heal this man. It would be nothing short of a miracle to be sure, but I think it would turn this man over to the Lord in a heartbeat."},{"kind":"para","text":"I find it interesting that this maid says this. She is in captivity, and you would think she would be a bit bitter and resentful towards her captors, but instead she wants to encourage this man to seek spiritual help! He must have been something special if she is willing to even SAY something about healing at all."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even the king of Syria was serious enough to put his own men out there for this man, Naaman. We don't get a very deep picture of this man, but we can read around the edges that this man was something special to these people. There are just some men that are like that. They are just worth your time and effort. When I was in ministry, I knew of a man that was like that. He was obviously on fire for the Lord, and he wanted to be involved as much as possible. There was a lot that needed to be done at the church, and it was just me and my brother on staff. We held a meeting with the church board to try and get this young man on staff with us. They didn't seem thrilled about the prospect. I even offered half my paycheck to get him on staff. The church declined. I won't get into the details of what happened later, but we will just say things did not work out. But he was worth the weight. He was worth the pay cut so that we could have the help from this man. Naaman was a man like that. They wanted to keep him in their midst, and they wanted to utilize him as much as possible. He was a pleasant man to be around."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Syria and Israel had their differences, but at the moment it seems they are at a connective point. They are not seriously attacking each other. They are agreeable and working together, so this wouldn't be a difficult ask from the king of Syria. BUT, the kings of Israel were a bit bipolar, so there's no telling what mood the Syrian king will find Israel's king in today. He is just excited that there is a means to heal this man Naaman and wants this letter to get to him."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ah, the king of Israel is in one of those moods. He has instantly given up after reading the letter. He thinks this letter has a means to cause war against him. The king of Syria didn't look to try and have Jehoram do the healing. He knew there was a man that was there to do it, and what higher authority to get this man to do it unlike the king? But the king is selfish and doesn't see a solution to the problem right off the bat, and starts to freak out thinking there is going to be another war with Syria."},{"kind":"para","text":"Leaders who jump the gun too soon are guilty of arrogance and negligence. All they do is put their own people in harm’s way with their rash decisions. I've seen that happen a lot in churches, honestly. It's a miserable thing. There was one particular church event that we were going to put on, and I knew that it was technically illegal, and so I addressed the legality of it, and instead of them finding a way around it, the pastor simply said, “Well, I would rather just do what God wants me to do and ask for forgiveness later.\" I did not think this was right. Scripture tells us to obey those that have the rule over us, and this was an act of defiance and rebellion. Eventually, we came to terms on the situation, and it was deeper explained to me how we would use loopholes for the situation, but at the time, I could not find a way to agree. I think the pastor was being rash. If I'm honest, I STILL think the pastor was being rash."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think there are a great majority of rash pastors today who are just looking to stir up trouble rather than work with Godly compassion. They just want to do anything and everything without (or against) the authority of anyone, and they are willing to throw their whole congregation under the bus to get there as long as they can follow the Lord. That is the wrong way to lead people. That is the way in which evil kings have been leading people. If that is your way of ministry, you need to change this today. You are no different than these awful pagan kings. All Jehoram needed to do was find a solution, and he KNEW there was a solution found in Elisha if he could just take a second to think."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":14,"title":"Naaman Cleansed","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, the Lord has sent prophets in Israel specifically for needs like this. If God can't get ahold of the king’s heart Himself, then He will certainly keep access to Him another way. That is what God wants to do for His creation. He wants us to continuously have access to Him no matter what, or how evil we may be. He wants there to ALWAYS be an opportunity for us to turn and trust in Him, and Elisha was that man. The king of Israel should have a reason already to turn to the Lord, but he refused to accept it. So God (and Elisha) has to remind him."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of Jehoram going to Elisha and sending Elisha to Naaman, it seems that the king gets word from Elisha, gives that word to the king of Syria, and the king of Syria sends Naaman quickly to be healed. Elisha probably could have easily gone to Syria to meet with Naaman, but he seems to want Naaman's faith to begin this process. Naaman is under the impression that this Elisha fellow is who he says he is. He's willing to try anything to get rid of this disease."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha sends a messenger about what to do. He is told to go to the Jordan and wash seven times. That's it. He didn't even tell him personally; he told him through a servant of his. The whole thing, honestly, seems a bit rude. But as we've learned from the past miracles of Elisha, he wants the people to address their own problems themselves and trust in the Lord for it. That is what Elisha is doing for Naaman as well. Naaman doesn't see it this way. He feels as if he's being mistreated and neglected by the prophet, and this will not be okay for him."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He didn't understand. He wanted some sort of obvious sign that this man was legit, and he didn't even get to meet the man. He says, \"Abana and Pharpar are just as good to wash in, why the Jordan?\" Honestly, he has a point. But the Jordan is (and will continue to be) a representation of God's salvation for His children. That is the meeting spot for God's life change. It's where they had to cross to take this land. David had to cross it when his son Absalom was killed. It will be where Jesus is baptized. Now this leper has to wash in it. Jordan was not a very clean river, and so this man thought that the prophet was just being a jerk for no reason. It would not have been hard for the prophet to simply clean him right then and there, but this man needed the faith of God, not just the healing of God. That is what the whole world needs."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The servant had some sense. He said, \"This is not that difficult of a task. If he had said to climb the highest mountain, I assume you would attempt it for your health’s sake. Worst-case scenario, you do this simple task and just trust the prophet and it doesn't work. At least then we will know.\" It's as simple as going by faith to wash. That was it."},{"kind":"para","text":"There was nothing special about the Jordan, by the way. It was not holy water by any means. You can go to the Jordan River today, and it is nothing to really be amazed by. It isn't much to look at, honestly. It was probably better looking back then than it is today, but it was still likely not much to see. Naaman would just have to give it a shot."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"After the third time of going into the water, he was probably starting to become a little hesitant. He probably thought, \"This is dumb. There is nothing good that could come of this. I'm probably getting other diseases on top of this one by doing this.\" Then the sixth time came, and he thought, “Well, there's only one more opportunity left for me. I don't suspect that anything is going to come out of this, but I have no other choice for my life's sake.\" Then he went in, came out, and likely couldn't believe what he felt and saw. The man of God was right! The glory of God was shown! Elisha never saw the man, he never spoke to the man personally, he never cleansed the man with his own hands. This man did it all by faith in just the prophet’s word. That is such a blessing for this world to hear."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is all it takes. Believing by faith, and you will be healed. In our case, we will be healed from sin. In their case, it is healed from disease. But his healing will certainly give him a different thought about the man of God and God in general."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":19,"title":"A Gift For Ministry","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Naaman's life was changed FOREVER. There was no going back to those pagan gods from before. He had seen a real miracle happen to him this day. He was faithless before, and now he has nothing BUT faith in the God of Israel. He is trying to give payment to Elisha for his work, but Elisha didn't do anything. Thankfully, Elisha will make mention of that and give the glory back to the Lord:"}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I hate taking money for ministry. I can take it as a gift, but not for the purpose of ministering. If a minister or pastor receives money in order for him to do his job, he is not doing it for the right reasons. He does not deserve that money. Honestly (I know this to be true), he will squander that money, and the Lord will give him over to it until it all eventually falls apart and he has nothing left to lean on but the Lord. I've seen that happen quite a bit. The money is fun for a while, but then it becomes a heavy burden."},{"kind":"para","text":"Recently, there was a woman that came to me at church and asked if I could teach her son how to play bass. I said, \"Of course! I can meet with him every Saturday he has available right after our worship practice.\" “Okay,\" she said, \"How much do you charge?\" I said, \"No charge. I will gladly do it for free.\" “No,\" she said, \"You must take something. I am taking your Saturday. We cannot let you do it for free.\" I responded, \"No. It is a ministry, and I don't think I should be paid for ministry.\" “Okay,\" she said, \"Then I will buy you gift cards, and that will be our gift to you.\" And that is what she did. She bought me gift cards to random stores around us and gave them to me as a \"gift.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are being paid by the church, that is all well and fine. I have spoken about this in several settings already. I will remind you that a church should not pay its ministers to do a job. They should only pay their ministers because that job is already being done by them. Sadly, I see the complete opposite of this in the church. Pastors hire ministers to fill a position alone, and they start paying the person right off the bat without vetting them when there are already people who are willing and able to fill that position completely for free. Actually, I've seen this in my own church."},{"kind":"para","text":"I was more than willing to lead worship in our church. I had done it for years prior, and I was leading worship at another church simultaneously. I told the pastor I was willing to lead when our (then) worship leader moved away. There was no one else willing to step up but me and this one other guy. Instead of choosing one of us, our pastor decided (for whatever reason) to hire a man that none of us knew. I have gotten to know this man today, and I am happy for the choice, but I was already doing it for free, and it seems quite strange that the pastor would just hire a random person to fill the position. I believe it was a mistake, honestly. But we live and learn (hopefully)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes that's the way pastors do things. That is not a smart way to do them. A godly man looks for any way possible to minister for free. A worldly man looks to get paid for his efforts. A selfish man looks to pay others to fill a position. That is what Naaman is doing here. He is trying to pay Elisha for what GOD did. Ultimately, Naaman wanted to thank the Lord through this means, but really it was offensive to Elisha."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Naaman is completely changing his entire life. He was sacrificing and worshipping false gods for a majority of his being, and now he's saying, \"There is no one else I would rather worship.\" This is such an amazing and bold change, and I hope we don't miss how rare and astounding this is. Naaman (when he goes back home) will be utterly surrounded by all sorts of pagan rituals and practices. He will not be able to get away from it. But he has made it a point (as of this day) to stand against the grain of culture, and live a life dedicated to this single God (the real God) until the day he dies. That is quite the stance to make. It will be a difficult one at that. I know it's possible, but I know it's not easy."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've seen Eva's own mother in this similar situation. She has a bunch of people living with her that are the complete opposite of her faith in the scripture. They hold to Catholic beliefs, and she holds to Christian beliefs. She is a light (a small single light) in a dark home. That is hard to stay lit with so many people against you in your OWN HOUSE. That is what Naaman will have to face."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's asking for forgiveness in this one particular instance because he will have no choice but to make face at least for this cause. He means nothing by his bowing down in the house of Rimmon. I don't know what god this would represent, but Naaman is not going to mean anything by it. If he doesn't at least show himself here, then he will be abused and mistreated for dishonoring his king’s god. So he is requesting that he would be forgiven for this. He is not bowing for the false god; he is bowing for his master. He is honoring his king."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was forgiven. If Naaman was honest about his loyalty to the Lord alone, this would be a piece of cake for him. He would just serve the Lord and his king. So he turns around and starts heading home, and they all live happily ever after..."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":27,"title":"Gehazi's Greed","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a fool... Gehazi didn't have the faith of the Lord like Elisha had. There was some money to receive from this Syrian, and he didn't think it was right for Elisha to reject it, so he went to go get it for himself. He couldn't just leave well enough alone. He (somehow in his warped brain) thinks he has earned at least SOME reward for being with Elisha. I don't understand this line of thinking. Actually... I don't understand how Elisha could have a servant who was not a godly man. We have not seen a single godly thing come from this man Gehazi. It has been quite odd."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"I mean, I just left. What has happened in this short distance?\" Naaman is still indebted to Elisha. He is indebted to the Lord. He has a completely different look on life and people. Even this servant looks different to Naaman. He has compassion for them like he's never had before. He was probably a pretty compassionate guy before this, but now he has a different means of compassion through the Lord. He saw people as hurting before. He could probably understand the pains of others. But NOW he sees people as valuable, and there is a way to address pain. So he's going to be the man that helps, not just hurts. That’s the type of man all Christians should strive to be."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Two young men? Who was he talking about? These sons of the prophets actually don't exist. He's not getting this reward for anyone but himself. This is a very sad moment in the life of Gehazi..."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Gehazi now has his reward. He robbed himself of any eternal ones by accepting the gift now. That is what a minister does when they accept their gifts today, by the way. They rob themselves of what GOD can give them by taking from what man can give."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"To add insult to injury, Gehazi lies to Elisha (the man of God). As if God is not going to reveal the truth..."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha knew exactly what he had done. He was not stupid. He wanted Gehazi to confess to it. There was no reason for Gehazi to take that money. There was no reason for him to lie to Elisha. There was no reason for him not to have faith. His own greed got the better of him, and now he suffers with Leprosy because of it. What a sad and tragic end to this chapter. It's a sad and tragic end to Gehazi. He was living the good life, and he literally gave it all away for some silver. That is what Judas will do as well. He will give away the presence of the Lord of salvation for his own greed. That risk doesn't seem worth the reward. Sadly, there are a lot of people who think of their faith with very little (to no) value."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":27}]},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 06"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see another miracle of Elisha present itself. A man from Syria comes to try and be healed from his leprosy, and is told that Elisha would be a good choice for that healing. He is sent to meet with Elisha, and Elisha simply tells him to go wash himself in the Jordan seven times. Naaman doesn't seem to like this idea, but he gives it a try anyway (because what left does he have to lose). Lo and behold, it works! He is cleansed and he goes to thank Elisha with a gift, but Elisha refuses it. The chapter ends with Elisha's servant going after Naaman to take the gift, and his greed gains him leprosy."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we start off on a high note with Elisha and his students going to build a place for them to grow. It quickly shifts to a war with Syria, and an enlightened vision of Israel towards the king that sends this king to go and confront Elisha about the problems he is seeing. This chapter goes downhill rather quickly, and we see that Elisha has his work cut out for him."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"The Broken Axe","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again (as we've mentioned), the term for \"sons of the prophets\" likely refers to students of the prophets. They seem to be following Elisha around for quite some time, and there were so many of them at this point that there was not enough room for any more. Actually, there wasn't even enough room for the people that were already there. They had outgrown their territory for learning. It was time to find a larger place. It sounds like these \"sons of the prophets\" were living with Elisha while he was in town, and the more people there, the less space they had."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Seems like a nice spot to set up a house and school for these people. They are going to put in the work and build a place along the Jordan that would suffice for their capacity. You can tell they are serious about what they are learning from this prophet, because they are willing to go out of their own way, spend their own time, and put in the work to build a place for them to grow and learn. If you really care about something, you will put the effort in to help it grow. Sometimes that will be at a risk. Even this would be risky, as people get hurt ALL THE TIME in construction."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a great example. Elisha is going to JOIN them in the work. He is not going to just let them do it alone. He is more than willing to work alongside his congregation. Think of this as more of a church than a school. I think that is really what he is developing here. There will be equipping happening in this new building, but he is going to work to grow this church WITH his congregants. He is not just going to direct them, he is going to lead them."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what a leader does. He does the work WITH his people. He doesn't say, \"You do this, or do that.\" He says, \"Let's do this and that.\" He involves himself with the labor. You are a poor leader if you tell other people what to do without actually doing it yourself. You are no minister if you are not willing to get involved directly. That is how you can tell who is genuine for the Lord and who is just acting the part."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Somebody broke a tool that they borrowed. I mean, it's better that the head of the axe break than the head of the builder. But either way, he couldn't do any more work without the axe, and he would have to pay for what he had just broken."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It fell into the river. If you have ever dropped something heavy in water before, you know it does not float like this. This is quite the miracle. When I was younger, my parents used to love going boating. My dad owned a very nice boat, and we would take it to the lake or river quite often. One particular time we were setting off the dock and my dad threw a bag in the boat. His keys (to his truck and boat) fell out of the pocket and sank into the water. He dove in quickly after them but could not grab them quickly enough. Of course, that's frustrating. He went and talked to a dock employee, and they got some gear on and dove to the bottom of the port to search for the keys. They found them and a pair of sunglasses. My dad had lost his sunglasses diving in after the keys and didn't even realize it. Even the sunglasses were too heavy to stay afloat."},{"kind":"para","text":"This circumstance here is quite the miracle. I don't know why my dad didn't just break off a stick and throw it into the water to make his keys float. I believe that this incident was not necessary for a miracle. There were plenty of students there that could probably easily go through that river and locate the axe head. Or, Elisha could have parted the water (we have seen him do that before with a mantle). Yet there is this big miracle that takes place over something seemingly so small. I don't know why, other than to assume that the Lord simply cares about the little things just as much as He cares about the big things."},{"kind":"para","text":"We sometimes don't think we should burden the Lord with small things because that is what they are... small... We think that God has too much on His plate to care about those things. People have cancer, and there is evil and wars happening all around the world, and people are being martyred. Why would the Lord care about something as small as my car keys not unlocking my car, or my car battery dying? The truth is, the Lord DOES care, and He DOES take recognition of every small detail of your life. He wants to take care of you, and do what is good and right for you. He wants to supply for every need in your life, even if it's small. And you know what? He's good at it. God is not too burdened for every aspect of your life. Actually, God can never be overburdened by your desire to come to Him. That is really all He wants, even if it's something small. He can make a big miracle take place from such a small and seemingly insignificant moment of your life."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"And just like that, problem solved! Just reach out and take it. There's a sermon there, I'm sure."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":13,"title":"Syria's War","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Syria had just received a great healing for a great man in our last chapter. Apparently, something else happened along the way that has now caused conflict between Israel and Syria. It sounds like Syria was trying to make camp in the land of Israel, and Israel was not very pleased with that idea."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha hears about it and sends a warning as quickly as possible. He says, \"Hey, you know that the Syrians are coming into the land in this place and setting up tents in our territory, right?\" These Syrians are trying to form a takeover, and Elisha has the news of it."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This happened several times, it seems, and Elisha had saved some territory for Israel. Once again, this should get the king of Israel on a good track towards the Lord. One could only hope."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In Syria's eyes, there was no way that Israel could find out about their camps unless there was some sort of traitor living with them. There had to be a traitor among them. At least, in their eyes, this man would be considered a traitor. The Lord was giving guidance against them in this moment. It was not any natural thing in their own camp. It was a supernatural thing from Elisha."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, at this point, Syria knows Elisha pretty well. I assume Naaman has spoken quite highly about the prophet. Now that they are going in to try and attack Israel, they are very aware of the ability that Elisha carries with the Lord. At this word (and knowledge), Elisha will be an enemy of Syria. All they would have to do, now, is go to Dothan and cause war there. They were going to try and kill this prophet if they had the chance. How quickly and easily it is for evil men to turn against righteous men, even if God has used the righteous to help the unrighteous."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":19,"title":"Open Eyes","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I assume that Elisha has a new servant. I don't think it is Gehazi worried in this moment (after what took place with him in our last chapter). Israel's law would state that Gehazi would have to be separated from civilization with this disease, so I'm sure he's not hanging around. This new servant doesn't seem to have a name, though. Perhaps that's for the better. But this man sees a large army of the enemy completely surrounding the city, and they have nowhere to run. The first thing he does is what a smart man SHOULD do, and that is go to the man of God and ask for counsel. He says, \"How do you suppose we can get out of this one today, Elisha?\" Elisha would be the man with the answers because God always gives an answer."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The servant thinks, \"I'm sorry... even if we had a large volume of people in this city with us, I don't think our measly few is going to be strong enough to go against a whole host of the army of Syria.\" Through the eyes of the world, they are done for. There is no way they would successfully win this battle. They have no place to run or hide, and they have no strength to fight against these people. They are toast by the standards of the world and it would be obvious to anyone that is seeing it. Yet, according to Elisha, they had far more power and a greater number. How? Well, he enlightens his servant in the next verse:"}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's take a moment to realize that it was the LORD who is winning this battle today. It wasn't just the Lord's army that Elisha is praying to. He is praying directly to the Lord to give them success here. The strength that Elisha was talking about was from God alone. That is his dependency. The servant didn't understand that until now. The Lord gave this servant a new vision."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what salvation does to a man, by the way. It gives us new vision. It gave Naaman a new vision in our last chapter. It will give Paul a new vision in the book of Acts. It is giving this servant a new vision right here in this chapter of 2 Kings. He could see the spiritual things that were not revealed to him before. You see, there is a whole spiritual warfare happening behind the scenes. There is a spiritual battle taking place in this world, that no one else sees unless they come to know the Lord. They can see the mental battle. They can see the physical battle. But without God, you cannot see the spiritual battle. You must have the eyes of faith to see what is truly happening."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is honestly what it is. Every physical battle is caused by a spiritual one. Every mental battle is caused by a spiritual one. The reason so many people lose the physical and mental battles of life is because they don't concern themselves with the spiritual one. They think it doesn't matter. Spirit is what makes up a person’s mind. It is what makes up a person’s actions. If you have the wrong spirit, you will make the wrong actions, and think the wrong things. You will accept the wrong directions, and receive the wrong outcomes. That is just a fact of life. The battle begins at the spirit. That does not mean it will make the mental and physical battles easy. By no means does it become easier. But it DOES mean that we have direction and purpose to keep fighting those other battles, because we have weapons of spiritual warfare that we can use."},{"kind":"para","text":"Weapons of mental warfare and weapons of physical warfare are so dismal and small compared to the weapons of spiritual warfare. The weapons of spiritual warfare are there for EVERYONE to take and use if we would just accept Christ. But that is where it starts. It starts with faith. It starts with a vision of the spiritual battle taking place. This is why Elisha will have no problem taking on this army. The army comes down to try and attack, and he simply prays to the Lord that they would be blind, and that is what happens. The Lord's army is simply standing there ready for battle, but THEY have not done anything yet. It was not THEM that blinds the enemy. It was the Lord alone."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems very strange. Elisha has the ability to literally destroy these people, but he chooses not to. We will see that Elisha has a different plan of attack against these people. He leads them away from the city and takes them to Samaria to let the king deal with them. But what happens next is even more strange:"}]}]},{"start":20,"end":23,"title":"Feeding The Enemy","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's a very important spiritual application here. These Syrians don't deserve the vision of the Lord, and yet that is what they are getting. Their eyes are opened again so they can see. Elisha could have left them blind and led them into the middle of the wilderness where they could try and fend for themselves, but that is not the theme of God's men. God's men want to give others a clearer vision. Elisha wants them to see the power of God."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king is ready to destroy these people. He sees them and says, \"Hey Elisha, we are ready to destroy them. Do you want me to kill them? Can I kill them now? It wouldn't be a hassle!\" Once again, the Syrians deserve it at this moment, and Elisha has the authority and power to do just that. This is what Elisha chooses to do:"}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, let's recap. The Syrians are making camp in the land. Elisha gets wind of it and tells his king. The king goes and drives them out, and they learn that it's Elisha telling Israel’s king. So Syria sends an army to go and destroy the place in which Elisha is and kill Elisha in the process. They completely surround Elisha, get close to Elisha to kill him, and Elisha blinds them (by the power of God), brings them to Samaria, opens their eyes (by the power of God), and then... feeds them? He feeds them and then lets them go. What was stopping them from coming back to try and kill the Israelites again? What was stopping them from coming back to try and destroy Elisha specifically?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Nothing. The faith of the Lord is what was protecting Elisha, and he knew it. The only reason that they were not able to kill him this time was because God provided a means. Why would God not provide a means again? For a time, the Syrians went back home and just pondered what had taken place. These people were not looking to try and take camp in their land anymore because of this solution. Their lives were certainly changed. In what ways? Time will tell. But as of right now, they are still reeling from what just took place with their vision and provision. Honestly, this was a miracle that should have made them question a lot of things about their faith. Miracles happen all the time, though, and people don't stretch their faith far enough to turn to the Lord. They reel from it for a moment, and then they forget about it when the next trial comes."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":31,"title":"Troubled Times","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Syrian bands didn't come back in, but that was not going to stop Syria's king from gathering an army of his own to try and take Israel down. I'm sure Benhadad heard what had just taken place, but evil people don't care about the facts or truth. They can be easily swayed because they have no foundation to stand on."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hold the phone! This is just kind of thrown in there, but this needs to be studied a bit deeper. We cannot forget that Israel (and its king in the northern kingdom who lives in Samaria) was very much against the Lord. God protects His own, but the king (at this time) was not part of the Lord's family. So while Elisha is doing a work to help serve and provide for those that need it (and also helping the king for his own country), the Lord was still doing a work on His people too."},{"kind":"para","text":"He has allowed a pretty great famine to take place, and apparently a donkey's head was valuable, and a small piece of bird poop was valuable. How? I really don't know. They had so little in their possession that they are just trying to survive from random things they are able to own. It seems apocalyptic honestly. That is how God judges a nation though. We see that happen over and over again throughout scripture, and we see that still happen in this world today. If we are experiencing a famine, or inflation today it is because the Lord is judging us. We certainly deserve it. I'm thankful we are not at this point yet, where we are trying to buy bird poop for a price."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king had the ability to help his people. The reason the Lord (God of Israel) was not helping them was because of the king’s terrible leadership. He had no compassion for those under him. He had made this nation poor and wanting. This lady is so undone at this moment, and begs the king to help, and the king just says, \"What could I do to help you? If God won't help you, I won't help you.\" And this is her response:"}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's no mistaking what you just read. This is how bad the land has become. They are (sorry for the image here) literally eating their own children. They have so little in the way of provisions, and are so neglected by their leadership that they are forced to do whatever it takes to survive. This woman agreed with her neighbor to eat her child today, and then they would eat the neighbor’s child tomorrow, and this lady thinks it's unfair that the neighbor did not give her child to eat the next day. She went back on her word. By the way this is worded, we are under the impression that these people have no moral acuity. They are not conscious of the fact of what they just did. This is absolutely terrible. I can't think of anything worse in all honesty. We are just reading about it and it makes me sick..."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This bit of information makes the king see how undone his country has become. It finally opens his eyes to his poor leadership. He sees that he has caused these people to become so calloused in society that they are willing to do some unthinkable things without even feeling emotion about it. That is the society he has literally created for his people. It should not have taken him this long (and a happenstance) for him to finally see what he has done to his own people. This is so depraved, and we are not even getting the full picture. I think scripture leaves out even worse things than this that were happening. We can simply just imagine the kinds of things they were doing, and I guarantee it's still not as bad as it actually was. Think of the worst thing that a person can do... I guarantee it was worse there."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is what arrogant leaders do. I don't even want to call them leaders because they really aren't. Bad leaders place blame on everyone else. Good leaders shoulder the blame. Bad leaders look to attack those that are doing good. Good leaders work with those that are doing good. Bad leaders want to change things that are moving in the right direction. Good leaders want to grow things that are moving in the right direction. Bad leaders want to take the credit. Good leaders want to give credit. Measure a leader by how focused they are on themselves compared to other people. Is anything ever their own fault? If not, then they are a bad leader and you can count that every word they speak is going to be a lie."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":33,"title":"Confronting Elisha","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha knew the king was coming. He was going to stop the messenger at the door before he got in, but in the middle of preparing for this, the messenger arrived, and the king with him. Instead of the king addressing the fault within himself, he tries to go to Elisha and place blame on the Lord. His messenger is there before Elisha expressing his anger with God. Instead of turning to God and asking for forgiveness and mercy, he turns to Elisha and tells him to let God know how angry he is. In fact, he is there to try and ruin Elisha. He wants to blame anyone else but himself for what he just witnessed."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a symbol of a bad leader. I don't know of many good leaders who would see a problem and think to themselves, “Well, they need to be a better person.\" No, they see a problem and think, \"How have I failed them? What can I do to fix this? How can I walk with them and help them?\" You can recognize that in teachers as well. A genuine teacher doesn't see a failed student and think, “Boy, they are dumb. They will never understand.\" A genuine teacher will always shoulder the blame for when someone fails, especially if that student has tried really hard but simply doesn't understand."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoram doesn't see it this way. He is under the impression that God has failed him, not that he has failed God. And wouldn't you know it, we are left on a cliffhanger. We will have to wait and see what Elisha says about the king’s accusation next. It was good, though, for the king to come to Elisha. That is the only place he can come to at this point. He must turn to the Lord!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":33}]},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 07"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see Elisha and his students go to make a place where they can learn and grow by the Jordan river. The chapter quickly transitions to the continual war between Syria and Israel, and then transitions again to the king seeing how depraved his nation has become because of his terrible leadership. Instead of the king taking ownership of the problem, he tries to place the problem onto Elisha and sends a messenger about his anger towards Elisha. We are left on a cliffhanger, as Elisha tries to clean up the mess that the king is making."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we will see that Elisha gives his answer to the king about what the Lord is going to do for them. They are not a people that deserve God's provision, but God is going to give it and He is going to prove Himself here. They should all see this and realize that the Lord has been paying attention, but they seem to miss that every single time Elisha tells them about it. All they have to do is turn to God (or the man of God as they have done here and there)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Elisha's Answer","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a pretty huge jump from buying a donkey’s head and bird poop from the last chapter. The inflation will finally be over, and things will start to look up according to the Lord. I guess the Lord is seeing this, and because the king has taken notice (and this man has asked some questions), the Lord is finally ready to move the Israelites back towards Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"The best way to feel some alleviation is after a while of heavy pressure. Sometimes pressure in your life is a good sign. It means that God is preparing you for some peace. He is preparing a time of joy and repentance. And in the times of joy and repentance, we must remember the seasons of suffering. We must remember what it was like and do what it takes to not go back to that. Sadly, the world forgets fast, and so it won't take long for Israel to fall right back into their sin."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This man is going to see how real all of this is. He might not believe it at this moment, but he certainly will when he sees it and can't partake. I don't think he believes this is going to be real. I think he wants a sign right now. But he is going to have to wait on the Lord just like everyone else."},{"kind":"para","text":"The king has been placing blame on Elisha for the famine, let's not forget. He says that it's Elisha's fault that the people are living this way. We might recognize that Elisha has not shifted the blame BACK on the king (even though it was the king’s fault). I believe Elisha could have easily put the blame back on him, but he chooses to shoulder the blame. Elisha was a better man than the king. I also think that what God really wanted was the king to TURN to the man of God, and he is now doing that because he has no other choice. He has no one else to blame."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes that is the only way the Lord will get someone's attention. He will give you no other choice but to finally turn to Him and surrender. Whether the king surrenders to the Lord or not is still up in the air (and we will see what he chooses to do), but it's sufficient to say that he has run out of options."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":8,"title":"Four Lepers","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"While Elisha is giving his speech about the coming provision of the Lord, we have a group of four very poor men talking about trying to kill themselves. They think if they stay where they are, they are going to die; if they try and go into the city, they are going to die; if they try and ask for help from the king, they are going to die, so they might have a better chance just living with the enemy. They are going to try and go to Samaria to find new life. That is not the place to go, friend."},{"kind":"para","text":"You don't leave your home to find something better. You don't try and find a new comfort in the world. That is what seems like second nature to us. Our sinful flesh wants to just bury our burdens and sorrows and sins in something new, and we find out rather quickly that it doesn't suffice. The only thing that would suffice for these men was to search for the Lord. If they had gone to Elisha for help, Elisha would have helped them, and they would have known about the provision of the Lord (which will happen literally the very next day). But they are prideful and hasty, and they reject the goodness of God. Don't we all do this, though? I know I'm guilty of it. Paul struggled with it too:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the good that I would I do not: but the evil which I would not, that I do. Now if I do that I would not, it is no more I that do it, but sin that dwelleth in me. I find then a law, that, when I would do good, evil is present with me. For I delight in the law of God after the inward man: But I see another law in my members, warring against the law of my mind, and bringing me into captivity to the law of sin which is in my members. O wretched man that I am! who shall deliver me from the body of this death? I thank God through Jesus Christ our Lord. So then with the mind I myself serve the law of God; but with the flesh the law of sin.","ref":"Romans 7:19-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you struggle with rejecting God's goodness, you are in good company. If even the great Apostle Paul had this problem (and we could really look at all of the Apostles for the same example), then you shouldn't be surprised when you struggle with it as well."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a strange conclusion to draw in this moment. The Syrians had disappeared because the Lord (specifically) scared them. Honestly, it would seem as if the Lord did all of this simply for these four men to learn a lesson (that it's quite obvious they are missing). They should have turned to the Lord instead of the enemy. Satan is enticing, to say the least. He entices with seemingly good things. But if he is attached to it in some way, we can rest assured that it is not going to be good. It will be used for something sinful and ungodly. So we are best off to stay clear of Satan's enticements and surrender to the little that the Lord has already given to us. That will be worth more than your sinful pleasures anyway."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I mean it's one thing to steal food and water. Obviously, they were in great need of it. But to steal their silver and gold was over the line. They should have been sufficient to eat and head to the man of God's house. They have had plenty of opportunity and plenty of reason to turn and go to the Lord. They are so far gone from God that they don't see their need for Him. There had to be something in them that sparked some repentance. There had to be something in them that said there was more out there than just worldly provision. There had to be something that said, \"These gods that we have been serving are not providing for us. Maybe we are looking at the wrong gods. Maybe we should look for a God of gods?\" That just doesn't click to them though."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's interesting how easily people can give up the thought of the true God ever being present. We get a lot of debate from atheists today as to \"Which god we should serve.\" That's what they say. \"Why isn't the Greek gods sufficient? They were just as real to the Greeks as your God is to you.\" No, they weren't. The Greek gods never supplied for their needs. The true God gave them over to their own gods and completely disconnected Himself from them, and look at them now. They are a nation fallen. That is why I believe this verse in Jeremiah to be about ANY and ALL nations that turn to the Lord:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"At what instant I shall speak concerning a nation, and concerning a kingdom, to pluck up, and to pull down, and to destroy it; If that nation, against whom I have pronounced, turn from their evil, I will repent of the evil that I thought to do unto them. And at what instant I shall speak concerning a nation, and concerning a kingdom, to build and to plant it; If it do evil in my sight, that it obey not my voice, then I will repent of the good, wherewith I said I would benefit them.","ref":"Jeremiah 18:7-10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yes, it's about the Israelites, but it's also a prophecy. That means it can be used for the Church and the end times as well. God's creation must simply turn to Him if they want to be taken care of by Him. That has been historically proven time and time again, and it can be absolutely proven today."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":15,"title":"Checking the Camp","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are finally going to share the wealth of what they have found. They are afraid that if they just stay there and wait, something is going to happen and their good time will be over, and their lives (possibly) as well. So they want to go tell the king about it so that provisions can be made for the whole city. There must have been quite a bit in this camp if it can help support the city."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems well enough to lavish themselves on all that good food and provision that is literally just sitting there. It wouldn't be too difficult for the king and his army to simply go in and take it. These four poor lepers did it without hesitation; why couldn't others do it too? If they worked quickly, they likely wouldn't even be caught, and the city could really use the supplies."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, this would make a lot of sense. It would seem as if it's a trap that was set up to lure the Israelites in and take advantage of them while they are in their weakest state. It is quite likely that the Syrians know that Israel is not doing very well in this moment. The Israelites are falling apart because of their sins. They have given themselves completely over to it. Not everyone, but the majority has. So if the Syrians were smart enough, this is something they could surely use to get the Israelites into a position that would not benefit them. But the Syrians were just running away from a noise that the Lord made them hear. They thought they were being taken over, and so they left that camp. God has provided a means for them using these four poor lepers."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The soldiers didn't even take their clothes with them when they ran. It was evident to anyone that went that it was abandoned. The king didn't want to send a whole host, but he could spare a few people just in case it was a trap. They found that it didn’t look like a trap, and that it might be worth going and just taking what was left."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, before we continue forward, I think it's important that we remember Elisha's words at the beginning of this chapter:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Elisha said, Hear ye the word of the LORD; Thus saith the LORD, To morrow about this time shall a measure of fine flour be sold for a shekel, and two measures of barley for a shekel, in the gate of Samaria.","ref":"2 Kings 7:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"That was the promise that the Lord had given to this servant of the king. That was the promise that the king heard. Jehoram knows that God is going to provide, and this is (yet again) ANOTHER opportunity for the king to turn to the Lord. There have been so many already, and this is surely a sign that God is paying attention. The king’s moment needs to come!"}]}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":"Just As God Said","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just as God said. That is how we are going to view life and Scripture. It will all be JUST as God said. We might not believe what is going to happen in the end times, but you can bank on the fact that it will be JUST as God said. You might not believe that Jesus was the Christ, but everything He did was JUST as God said. You might not think that God can save today, but you can be saved JUST as God said. Everything WILL be done \"according to the word of the Lord.\" So, when uncertainty arises (and it always does), we would be wise to go and say \"what does God's Word say about this?\" and I promise you it will be \"according to the word of the Lord.\""}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This man who went to Elisha (on behalf of the king) saw the provision, but did not have a chance to take it. His own people unintentionally killed him. They were in a hurry to get as much from this camp as possible to jumpstart their economy, and they didn't care who they had to mow down to get it. I've seen people do stupid things like this for less. According to some online surveys over, one hundred people (roughly one hundred and seventeen) per year are injured Black Friday shopping. It is reported that three people die per year from it. That's just SHOPPING on Black Friday. That's from people pushing and trampling over others to try and get some sort of \"deal\" on a day we are supposed to celebrate what we are thankful for already having. That is a sad reality of our era and climate. In recent years, Black Friday hasn't seen very many actual deals on anything important, so I don't think it's ever worth going to things like that, but some people do, and to them it's cutthroat. These people in Israel have been waiting for a deal on necessities for a long time, and it has finally arrived. They are pushing in, and at LEAST one man died because of their hurry to get the supplies."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That's a sad ending, but it was also warned to him. The writer gives a recap of what we read at the beginning of this chapter. Why? Well, he wants it to be clear that it was the LORD working this. He wants the reader to understand that everything will always happen \"according to the word of the Lord.\" That is what we need to keep in our hearts and minds."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":20}]},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 08"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see Elisha reveal the blessing of the Lord for the land given to them by the negligence of the Syrians. The king of Israel was trying to blame Elisha for what was happening in the land, and Elisha proved the kings evil by revealing the providence of God. God's providence came, and the people were blessed because of it. It helped jumpstart the economy."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then they quickly turn away from the Lord (as revealed in this chapter) and everything falls back into famine and mischief again. We see the Shunammite woman again, and Elisha is sure to take care of her. There is new kings placed on the throne of the southern kingdom, and they have turned course from following the Lord. Everything seems to be falling apart quickly, and we seem to get a bit of a break from speaking about Elisha for a bit."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"The Return of the Shunammite Woman","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This lady had been good to Elisha. She has made him a place to lay, and has taken quite good care of the man of God. He has taken quite good care of her in return. Elisha does not want this woman to suffer the same fate as the rest of the sinners in this land. God had just provided some supplies for Samaria in our last chapter, but the land was not healed. There were still a lot of other places in Israel worshipping false gods, and doing heinous things, and so the sins of the people quickly rushed back into the land."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now God is going to turn it back over to a famine. He is taking away His providence again. They have not turned to Him, and so they have gained judgement again. We are seeing that come to life in our country today. But we will see that (as the famines come and go over and over again) God protects those that are dedicated to Him. At this moment, it was this woman who deserved the protection. She is warned about the famine, and is told to move her family out of the land and recommended some places to go. That could not be easy for her, but she also knows that Elisha is serious about this."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The land of the Philistines was not a good place for her to be either. But it was a better place for her than where she was before. That is sad, isn't it? Let's bring this down to the likeness of a church today. Churches are to be safe havens for God's children. They are to be places where a Christian can go and live with the Lord. We call it our \"church home\" for a reason. It's a place we can call our home with the Lord. There are many church \"homes\" that are falling apart at the seams. They have really poor leadership, and it is making the people suffer immensely. They are facing spiritual leprosy and spiritual famine."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's sufficient to look at leprosy as a judgement. It could be looked at as sin. We are unclean in our state without Christ. But I think the church can fall into sin if it has poor leadership. Many churches do, honestly. They experience spiritual famines where people are left to begging for some spiritual feeding. They, sadly, won't receive it there, and they are forced to live in a place that they shouldn’t, simply because it's better for the season. I have had this experience before."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe we are in a spiritual draught. We are in a spiritual depression and a spiritual famine. We are lacking basic spiritual needs. That leaves people with two options: They can either suffer with the church (as they have been) and fall apart with the church (or grow the wrong way with the church), or they can live outside of God's house for a time. This woman was living outside of God's land. She was living with the enemy. But that was the recommendation by THE MAN OF GOD to protect her (sadly)."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a bold and heartfelt woman. She loved her people. She loved her country. She knew that she did not belong in the land of the Philistines. She had been praying for her land, and now she was demanding an audience with the king to make some changes. She wanted her people to turn to the Lord! She had waited the seven years, and it had been long enough for this king to turn things around and do what was right. So she was ready to come back, and she wanted the king to know her heart on the matter."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Gehazi has been gone for a minute, right? He has Leprosy, unless it has been taken away? So the only things that Gehazi would be able to tell the king about were the situations that took place BEFORE that happened. This is an interesting look at the countenance of the king, though. He is not angry (or at least it doesn't seem he is) with Elisha at the moment. He says that Elisha has done \"great things.” Very uncharacteristic of the king."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the middle of telling the king about a boy that Elisha brought back to life, the mother of that boy shows up! That is fortuitous. Apparently, some people had been living on her land, and that had to be addressed as well as the spiritual nature of the land. So, he asks the king to help her with her dilemma, and Gehazi says, \"Hey! This is the woman I was just speaking about! My proof of one of Elisha's great things is here!\""}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She gets everything back just as she had found it. Once again, it seems a bit uncharacteristic of the king. Was he feeling okay? I suppose seven years of famine will surely change a man. I don't believe he's a man of God, but he certainly isn't the same man he was before."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":15,"title":"Benhadad's Sickness","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, seven years have an impact on the other leaders as well. This king (of Syria) was trying to capture Elisha not too long ago, and now he has no choice but to seek answers from him. There is no denying that the God in which Elisha was serving was evident and real. He was what true power looked like. The kings couldn't deny that the power of God was evident in the life of Elisha, and that is why he is the talk of all the leadership in these countries."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems to always be the case that people look for God in the last moments of their life. They wait. I don't know why they wait. I haven't been on that side of the aisle before (and I never will), but I can't imagine that lying in your deathbed and begging for an answer from the Lord is a good feeling. If it were me, I would feel guilty for living so long without Him. I'd feel guilty for wasting my entire life when I had the chance to serve Him while I had health. God gave me health, and I squandered it by rejecting Him. He is (of course) forgiving, even in those last moments, but I would still feel guilty for not coming to Him sooner, and with the ability to serve others with Him. So many wasted opportunities..."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It won't be from his sickness that he will die, but there will be a time that he will die. Obviously, there will be a time that EVERYONE will die. But there is another way in which this king of Syria will die. He knows what will happen. The Lord has given him a vision of it. The king of Syria will be of good comfort to know that he won't die from this sickness, but then he will live in discomfort knowing that he will die a different way. I don't know what sickness he had, but I assume it was worse than a cold. Of course, this is good news to Hazael, because if the king dies, then Hazael will become the new king."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I guess Hazael's face must have looked quite grim, and he noticed that Elisha noticed him. He changed his countenance quickly and felt ashamed for whatever he was thinking (which will be revealed in a moment). And ELISHA started to cry. Knowing what is coming is a very heavy burden for a man to bear. That is why God usually doesn't reveal the future to us yet. It would be a lot for us to bear."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hazael will be a very powerful ruler, but he will be a powerful ruler AGAINST Israel. The reason that Elisha is sad is not because this man will become evil. I think Elisha knows better than anyone else that every single person in the world has the ability to be evil. Rather, he's sad because this man will be torturing and killing his own people. The Lord had given Elisha a vision of the coming battles with Syria. At this moment, everything that has taken place with Syria and the northern kingdom of Israel has seemed like child's play compared to what Hazael was bound to do."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hazael is not a dog for the Lord. Dogs obey their masters. Hazael is not going to specifically \"obey\" the Lord. The Lord will move him though. This WILL come to pass. That's what Hazael wants. He wants the power of the throne. He will get it, but he will abuse it, and he will abuse the Israelites as well, and this was tearing Elisha apart. Though nearly ALL of Israel has been against the Lord for so many years now, Elisha still has a love and heart for them, and this is not the way in which he wants his people to end up. It will be brutal and cruel to say the least."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At least he tells Benhadad the truth. He then kills Benhadad with a cloth full of water. He didn't die from his sickness, though. And JUST like God said, Hazael now has the throne. This was a bad sign for Israel. We've already seen how far gone and depraved Israel has been lately, and we might think that things could not possibly get much worse... We would be wrong..."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":19,"title":"A Light In Judah","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So now we have Joram as the king of the northern kingdom, and Jehoram as the king of the southern kingdom. This might get a little confusing because they have nearly identical names. In fact, their names literally mean the same thing! \"Jehoram\" means \"Jehovah is exalted.” Joram means the same thing. They are similar names. When we were first introduced to “Joram,\" scripture calls him \"Jehoram.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So he died according to the word of the LORD which Elijah had spoken. And Jehoram reigned in his stead in the second year of Jehoram the son of Jehoshaphat king of Judah; because he had no son. Now the rest of the acts of Ahaziah which he did, are they not written in the book of the chronicles of the kings of Israel?","ref":"2 Kings 1:17-18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now the author is changing it up a bit. We have the northern kingdoms \"Joram\" and the southern kingdoms “Jehoram.\" Why did they change the name? I assume to help create less confusion. There have been two Jehoram kings, and two Ahaziah kings, and so it's quite possible to get them confused from what king was good and what king was bad, and which ruled which kingdom. But we will see that these Jorams are not so different... sadly… The southern kingdom has had some very good kings so far, but we will find this king straying away from that goodness. Why? Well, because he's married to Ahab and Jezebel's daughter:"}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Oh, how the mighty have fallen. This is why it's important for a Christian man to marry a Christian woman (vice versa). Jehoram has plenty going for him because his fathers were good kings. They were people pointed back to the Lord. His wife (Athaliah) will be someone that takes his eyes off of the God of his fathers, and that will be the ultimate downfall of Israel."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah was going to deserve destruction, but God made a promise to David. Because of this promise, God is going to make sure there is some sort of light present in this nation. Judah WILL survive. They WILL continue the lineage because God ALWAYS keeps His promise. It's the promise of salvation, by the way. That is what this is speaking about. The promise to David was a promise of a Savior. That is why God is not completely done with us today. That is why He has not completely destroyed us. He keeps His promise. His promise is the Savior."},{"kind":"para","text":"He will always give a light to this world while there is still hope left in it. But just like the northern kingdom, that light will stray away, and over time it WILL disappear, and the Lord will have no choice but to finish this creation. Does that mean He will take His light away? No. Those that are in eternity are His light. His light will always be shining for all of eternity."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the city had no need of the sun, neither of the moon, to shine in it: for the glory of God did lighten it, and the Lamb is the light thereof. And the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth do bring their glory and honour into it. And the gates of it shall not be shut at all by day: for there shall be no night there.","ref":"Revelation 21:23-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"It might seem as if the light of Judah will disappear, but the Lord will shine that light again. It comes back, and the Lord reveals it through Jesus. I have no doubt that the writer of 2 Kings is looking at this bleak account and thinking, \"Even I don't know how Israel is going to get through this. Yet, I know that God has promised a light, so I trust He is going to keep that promise. Perhaps I should write that here so the readers know that God WILL keep that promise.” Sure enough, we have other accounts in Scripture to prove that God has kept the promise."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":24,"title":"Ahaziah Reigns","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Edom is now against Judah. That took them by surprise, I think. We will see in Obadiah's account that the Lord is not thrilled with this decision."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Under Joram's evil rule, Judah made enemies with people that were once servants to them. That was a mistake. Both Libnah and Edom were no longer allies to Judah, and then Joram dies. We (obviously) can get more information about his spiritual state in Chronicles (as well as all the other kings of Israel)."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ahaziah\" means \"Jehovah holds.” Seems like a promising name. Yet, we have learned that their names don't mean much about their character at this time. So surely that is the same with Ahaziah coming from such evil parents..."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":29,"title":"Joram's Wound","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Joram was reigning in the northern kingdom for eight years when Ahaziah took the throne in the southern kingdom. The kingdoms were still split, let's not forget."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Athaliah\" means \"afflicted of the Lord.” Certainly she was, because she was so far against the Lord. You will always be afflicted when you fight against the God of the Bible. For a deity that is supposedly not real, he sure is able to hurt a lot of people."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, he doesn't live up to his name. That is an interesting take, though, isn't it? As we've been reading throughout Scripture, we have seen that names matter to the people. It shows the life they lead. Now it shows the life they SHOULD be leading, but they are not. That is the depravity of man. God has a life for us, but we do not follow it. We usually shun it. We reject it and try and fight against it. These kings will be given to their chosen lifestyle, but their names will always resemble the mark of a life they SHOULD be living. God is still going to hold this nation. He is going to hold the kingdom of Judah, but He will do it AGAINST their own wills."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It wasn't often that the northern and southern kingdoms allied together to battle, but I am guessing the battle was too much for Israel to face alone. So the southern kingdom joined forces with the northern to fight against Syria, and it sounds like Syria won. They wounded the king of Israel."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are left on a cliffhanger (of course). We will have to wait and see what happens to Joram. If only there were a way we could just get to the next chapter and read it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joram is inflicted with a wound that is bringing him near to death, and Ahaziah is caring enough to come visit him and see how he is doing and how the sickness can be treated. The dynamics have changed in these kingdoms for sure, but like motives reach like people, and these two kings had sin in common with each other. That is why they can ally. They couldn't ally before because one king was for the Lord and the other was against the Lord. Now they can ally because they are for the same cause. They are both against the Lord…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":29}]},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 09"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the reign of Ahaziah in the southern kingdom, and the wounding of Joram in the northern kingdom. Ahaziah seems to get along with Joram, and so he goes to make a visit to him while he's laying sick from his wound and waiting for his healing."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we pick back up with what happens in the house of Ahab and Jezebel. We finally get the fulfilled prophecy that Elijah spoke about (seemingly) so long ago. A young prophet comes to anoint a different man (Jehu) as the king of Israel, and this man takes the current kings down so that he can assume the throne as the Lord had promised him."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"A Young Prophets Call","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This right here... This is perhaps the funniest thing I've read so far, and we have read quite a few funny things that have happened. Elisha is giving a task to this young prophet about anointing a new king of the northern kingdom. He comes from the family of Jehoshaphat, which was a good king (for the southern kingdom). It was time to make some change in the leadership of the northern kingdom. Joram was making a mess of things and causing all sorts of drama and pain for the Israelites in his sinful and evil way of living. This was not going to stand with God, and so He has tasked Elisha to send a student of his to go and speak to Jehu, giving him the crown. But it's the way in which he is to do it that I find funny. He is to go in and look for Jehu, then pick him up and take him to a room, and then he was to take a box of oil and pour it on top of him, and say, \"I have anointed thee king over Israel\" and then bash open the door and run away. It's a simple task, yet it's done in such an abrupt and random way."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The young man listens and obliges. Honestly, this seemed pretty straightforward and simple enough. This was to fix the current leadership of Israel. This would (prayerfully) be a better pick for king, and it would make the Israelites turn back to the Lord. I do think it's strange that he sends this prophet instead of doing it himself. I have to assume that the Lord told him to send his student rather than telling Elisha to go specifically. Maybe He knew that Elisha would run into trouble if he went. Not as many people are looking out for this young man, but everyone knew about Elisha, the prophet. If Elisha went, he would certainly be noticed, and that would likely paint a big ol' target on his back. Either way, this young prophet seems to listen and does the job he is commissioned to do."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The prophet didn't actually \"carry\" him out, but just enticed him to come out. Elisha was pretty specific about carrying this man out, but I'll let it slide. I'm sure that was a minute detail, and this works just as well. He was told to entice him to come out anyway (it says \"make him arise up\"). They just needed to be separated from everyone else and alone, and that is what this young prophet did."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There were a few changes made HERE as well. He was told to just tell this guy that he was going to be the king and pour oil on his head. It seems there was more added to it here. I assume that Elisha had told him to say this, but we don't really get that picture from the conversation earlier. I mean, everything this young man said was absolutely true. Jezebel was a terribly evil human being who mistreated a lot of very innocent people for personal gain. Her own people even. God is not going to let that go. Jezreel will be the image of that (and if you don't remember, that is where Joram is lying sick right now, and Ahaziah is visiting with him)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I suppose the most disappointing part of this is the last part where it says, \"And he opened the door, and fled.\" I think it would have been a little more flashy if he jumped out the window. It would have been more action-packed and dramatic, but I am not the one in control of this. If I were, I suppose I would have stopped Joram from ever becoming king to begin with, but the Lord has a purpose for everything, and we will see that even the evil deeds of this king will serve a much greater intention than anyone knows and that is to prove the authority of the Lord, and how sinful and wicked this world is without Him."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":13,"title":"Jehu Anointed","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It sounds (to me anyway) that Jehu didn't think this young prophet was serious either. His friends call him a \"mad fellow,\" and Jehu simply says, \"He said what all mad men say. It's nothing important.\" That is how Christians are looked at by the world, by the way. We are just looked at as mad fellows. I don't assume that Jehu has cleaned up a bit since oil has been poured on him. I'm sure it was somewhat noticeable. I don't know how much oil was poured on him, but I'm sure it was enough to have to clean something up. So these friends of Jehu didn't fall for his casual entrance back into their chambers."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was either serious or it was a joke. I am going to lean more towards the serious note. They probably thought the prophet was speaking the truth. They agreed with the sentiment. If it was a joke, it would also make sense because they think of the prophets as \"mad fellows,\" let's not forget. But they probably didn't disagree with the idea that Jehu would make a good king for them. Better than Joram in their eyes."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":16,"title":"A Conspiracy Against Joram","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Jehu takes the prophets’ words and puts them into his own hands. He is going to TAKE the throne. If Jehu had waited, I guarantee the throne would have just been given to him. But the task given by the young prophet was to be king, and that is what Jehu is going to do. He is going to have the throne. Joram was near death anyway, and the people there would not be ready for a revolt of this kind."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahaziah was there too, and that was another king who would have to be dealt with. He is a bad king, too."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":20,"title":"Two Messengers","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They looked like their own people, but they didn't seem like they were coming to meet peacefully. So he is going to go out and see. If not... well, the whole city would know and start to defend itself, I guess."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The man that went out to ask about peace never returned. Jehu actually lets him go, and the man is able to join them or move out of the way. That, honestly, was more mercy than most of the leadership had given at this particular time in Israel."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They could tell (now) that this was Jehu. I guess people have a specific way of driving. I know growing up there were different methods of driving from everyone I was around. My aunt had said that her daughter drives like her. She drove with rage and yelling. She would talk to the other drivers and tell them how stupid they were from the seat of her car. My dad drove very specifically too. He was aggressive to say the least. I knew one particular young man (he was young to me even when I was young, for when I was in my twenties he was still a teenager) who drove like a mad man. He would just simply floor it everywhere he went. If he wasn't driving 90 he was driving too slow."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu seems to have a specific way he drives, and that peeked their attention. The fact that their messengers weren't returning started to worry them a bit. Why would Jehu be coming this way, and why aren't the messengers coming back? Obviously the first messenger could have been a fluke thing, but TWO messengers not returning means something far more diabolical is approaching."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":26,"title":"Confronting Jehu","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joram and Ahaziah knew there was more to this than Jehu just coming to make a visit, so they were going to confront him to his face. Joram was not doing too great still. He hadn’t fully recovered, and so fighting was not a great option at the moment. With the alliance of Ahaziah (though), it might have been a better opportunity for Joram to win. But Jehu was basically unstoppable because of the anointing of the Lord."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Dang, shots fired. It's one thing to attack a person face to face because of their depravity, but to bring their mother into the equation (and reference her as... well, you can read it) is a whole different version of insult. Once again, though, Jehu isn't wrong. Jezebel was a despicable person. Which is kind of funny to me that anyone today would call their child Jezebel. There is nothing good about that name. You'd be better off naming your child Lucifer at that point..."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joram knew it wasn't for peace. I wonder how he came to that conclusion?"}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, that was fast. There was no need for battle. The king was dead that quickly and easily. Jehu was a really good shot. I don't imagine that he was a good shot because of how much he practiced (though I'm sure that's part of it). I'm sure this shot was good, only because the Lord guided that arrow."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice that the name changes back to the \"Jehoram\" spelling. That is not to bring any confusion, except now we are confused. This goes to show that those two names are interchangeable, seeing as they mean the same thing and the names are closely related. Don't get hung up on it. Jehoram and Joram (here) are the same person. Why they switched it back and forth? I don't know. Perhaps the translator just wants to keep us on our toes."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu is insinuating that he is following the instructions of the Lord here. That is an interesting thing to note. He has been told by the Lord what he was anointed for, so he should be a good replacement on the throne... right? At this moment, he is thinking himself to be pretty close to the God of creation in his methods and living. I will not say he is or is not at this moment, but everything is being fulfilled according to the sovereign purpose of the Lord. Jehu is recalling something that the Lord had apparently said to him about taking down the house of Ahab, and so he commissions his captain to bury Joram in the field of Naboth. That's a befitting place, isn't it? Ahab and Jezebel stole this place from Naboth, remember? Ahab wanted it, so Jezebel had Naboth killed to get it. Now that is their burial place. That is their portion of this life and legacy. They wanted this vineyard, that is what they are going to get."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Did Naboth have children? That makes this even more sad if so. They didn't just kill Naboth; they completely robbed the future of his children as well. They didn't even give his children a chance for a future. That makes all of this even worse."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":28,"title":"Ahaziah Killed","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Two kings killed in one day. That's quite a bargain. What a sad ending to these kings. Ahaziah was not a part of the deal, but that was what Jehu took to himself. At least he was buried with his family line. That's nice. Let's not forget that BOTH of these kings were evil. They only had one thing in common, and that was their sin against the Lord of Israel. Their sinful and evil lifestyle let them die together. That is a picture of every one of us. Live for the world, die from the Lord. Live for the Lord, die FOR the Lord. If you are living for God (though), then you will never die for eternity."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"title":"Ahaziah's Lineage","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting verse. Why place this here? We are getting a bit of a recap of who Ahaziah is. Let's remember that Ahaziah came from the family of Ahab as well. His mother was Ahab’s daughter. That makes Ahaziah a benefactor of the Lord's judgement as well. He falls under the same jurisdiction as the rest of this family the prophets have said would be brutally gotten rid of. But he was also under the line of Judah's heritage. Jehoshaphat was a godly king, so Ahaziah will be buried in the city of David for THAT sake alone."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":37,"title":"Jezebel's Death","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think Jezebel cared at all. She had to act the part, really. She disfigured her face to make it seem as if she was just a lonely, innocent woman. It's a lot like this:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Moreover when ye fast, be not, as the hypocrites, of a sad countenance: for they disfigure their faces, that they may appear unto men to fast. Verily I say unto you, They have their reward. But thou, when thou fastest, anoint thine head, and wash thy face; That thou appear not unto men to fast, but unto thy Father which is in secret: and thy Father, which seeth in secret, shall reward thee openly.","ref":"Matthew 6:16-18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Religionists dress their faces up to make themselves look sad, but they aren't really sad. It's just a part of their tradition. That is what the Catholic Church does by and large today. They have their Ash Wednesday and season of Lent, and they just disfigure their faces and call that “fasting,\" and Jesus literally preaches against that. Why? Are they hurting anyone? No. But they are not genuine, and it robs people of a true heart of worship and gives them a fake persona of tradition. They prove they don't love the Lord. They just love themselves. That is the same lifestyle that Jezebel is living here. It's a fake personality that robs her of true, genuine character and compassion."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She thinks she can just talk her way out of this. That was not the plan for any of this. Jezebel earned herself a very slow and painful death. She was a cruel and evil woman."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He, nor his own men, had to do anything to take care of this evil old woman. The eunuchs that worked under her were the ones to do it. They killed her brutally, and the Lord fulfilled the prophecy that she (no doubt) knew was going to come to pass. She may not have ever believed in the Lord, but God will keep His promises whether you believe or not."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Eunuch\" means \"official.\" These were people who were likely around the throne of the royal family quite a bit. They were usually emasculated, but that is something we can talk about later in scripture."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Whether he liked the idea or not, she deserved a burial for being in the line of royalty. By the fact that he was able to eat and drink before burying her, it shows that her death meant very little to him. Honestly, it means very little to everyone. How sad that people can be so evil that no one cares if you live or die. I don't imagine she would have very many faithful members at her funeral."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is a gruesome scene. They really did a number on her. They threw her off the balcony of her home, and she hit the ground hard enough to literally burst. That is quite the picture."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was nothing left of Jezebel to even identify her. Jehu (masterfully) points out that the Lord promised this to happen through Elijah. Elijah did warn about this coming to pass quite some time ago."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt speak unto him, saying, Thus saith the LORD, Hast thou killed, and also taken possession? And thou shalt speak unto him, saying, Thus saith the LORD, In the place where dogs licked the blood of Naboth shall dogs lick thy blood, even thine...And of Jezebel also spake the LORD, saying, The dogs shall eat Jezebel by the wall of Jezreel. Him that dieth of Ahab in the city the dogs shall eat; and him that dieth in the field shall the fowls of the air eat. But there was none like unto Ahab, which did sell himself to work wickedness in the sight of the LORD, whom Jezebel his wife stirred up.","ref":"1 Kings 21:19;23-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"It was a rightful ending to someone so heinous. A lot of people read scripture (specifically Revelation) and think, \"How could God be so cruel to the world? Look at how He is going to treat His own creation. He obviously doesn't love us.\" Then we read stories like this and think, \"Yeah! The Lord is justified to do this to this terrible, disgusting woman!\" Well, that is how the whole world will be in the end. The world will live a life like Jezebel. That is the picture of the end times, and we will root for the downfall of that terrible lifestyle."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Notwithstanding I have a few things against thee, because thou sufferest that woman Jezebel, which calleth herself a prophetess, to teach and to seduce my servants to commit fornication, and to eat things sacrificed unto idols.","ref":"Revelation 2:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have quickly approached that lifestyle, and it is quite evident in our world today. There will be, however, nothing left of her when the Lord is finished."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":37}]},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the story progress with Ahaziah and Joram as they work together against Syria. In this time, Jehu is made king, and Jehu kills both kings and takes it upon himself to go after the entire family of Ahab."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Jehu take his responsibility of killing the line of Ahab very seriously. He gains the obedience of Samaria by requesting the heads of Ahabs sons, and then goes after the worshippers of Baal. We will see the beginning progress of the northern kingdom falling into captivity."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Identifying Allegiance","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Before I continue, did we read that correctly? SEVENTY sons in Samaria? Jehu has his work cut out for him if he's going to take out that entire family lineage. That's a lot of kids, isn't it? Jehu is writing a letter to the people in Samaria so they know what his intentions are. It would be wise of them not to stop him or get in the way. There is only one family line he is after, and he doesn't need casualties of the civilians there. We might remember that the people in Samaria are living in extreme poverty under the rule of this family line. They have made it nearly impossible to live in prosperity in Samaria. I don't think any care was given to the fact that they had just lost their king. The sons of the king might care, but the citizens of the city surely don't."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu seemed to have a power that no one could compete against. He was able to take down TWO KINGS in one day. So the sons of Ahab didn't think they stood a chance to take the throne. There are SEVENTY kids, let's not forget. You'd think at least ONE would have the boldness to fight for the throne, but none of them did. Jehu had a lot of power before the Lord anointed him. He was a captain of captains. He was a skilled war veteran. He knew exactly how to fight and how to win. But now with the authority of the Lord, there is literally nothing stopping him from assuming the throne."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people in the city (and the authorities there) were fully submissive to Jehu. They didn't want to get in his way. They were just hoping for some mercy from Jehu. Jehu is still not king (yet) technically. He is still fighting for the throne, but it seems it is just being given to him at this point. So the entire city of Samaria is willing to obey whatever Jehu wants."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu says, \"If you want to prove that you are loyal to me, kill all of the seventy sons of Ahab and come to me in Jezreel when it's finished.\" I believe the sons were hanging around the \"great men\" of the city because they were the strongest. They could protect them. Little did they know that these \"great men\" were willing to comply with Jehu as well. Nobody liked this family, and nobody wanted to deal with going against Jehu at this time either."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a weird gift! Jehu had seventy housewarming baskets sent to him in Jezreel. This was a symbol (to Jehu) that the throne was certainly his if he wanted it."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":11,"title":"Jehu Finishes the Job","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That had to be a gruesome scene. The city gate was a pretty busy path for people to take. People were going back and forth, in and out of it all the time. Everyone was going to see these piles of heads as they were going about their business. People would certainly wonder what was happening, and they would probably be in terror as they saw it. Even for them, this is pretty serious."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu gives authority to God here. It was not by the hand of Jehu that these seventy people died. It was by the hands of the city that they did. The people cannot say, “Well, it's because Jehu is trying to assert authority over all of us. He is a killer. He strong-armed his way onto the throne.\" Rather, it was told that the Lord was going to answer this way, and it was assured that the Lord answered exactly how He said He was going to. Now the people will have no reason to reject the obvious authority of the Lord in this matter."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God could have ended this lineage off in several ways. God could have taken care of this family lineage Himself. Jehu felt that the Lord was giving Him the responsibility to clean up this mess. And so he does. He takes care of this entire family, and anyone that showed any allegiance to them. The priests were an important touch. They were tainted. They would not be working for the God of Israel. They would be working for the fake gods of Ahab."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"Ahaziah's Sons Killed","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These men were going down to give their attendance to the king and queen’s children. Little did they know that they were all dead. And now they will die too because they came from the line of Ahaziah (who was still of the line of Ahab). That's not just a happenstance of a meeting. Obviously, that was an ordained collision."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So over seventy associates of Ahab were killed, and at least forty-two sons of Ahaziah were killed. That's a lot of men being slaughtered in a short time. Jehu was making quick work of this family."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":17,"title":"Jehonadab Joins Jehu","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Jehonadab wanted to support Jehu's mission of slaughtering the family lineage of Ahab. He thought this was a good idea, and Jehu was doing the Lord's work. \"Jehonadab\" means \"Jehovah is willing.\" And the Lord was willing to let Jehu take care of all these men. I want to highlight (again) that the Lord could have taken care of this about a million different ways. If Jehu doesn't do anything but assume the throne, the Lord would have taken care of the family of Ahab. But, of course, the Lord knows exactly what people are going to do and how things are going to play out. God knew that Jehu would take it upon himself to fulfill these promises of the Lord, and that is why God does this at this time."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's a very weird thing to think about, isn't it? God knew that Jehu was going to do this, and that is why Elijah prophesied about it. Jehu is under the impression that he is fulfilling God's prophecy. He's being a \"man of God.\" Yet it's Jehu that God prophesied about this happening to. God didn't say it would be Jehu who killed this family line. In fact, God just made a promise that this family line would be destroyed. God knew it would be this way, but Jehu thinks that he is somehow speeding up the progress and doing the Lord's work. God did not commission Jehu to do this. But Jehu took it upon himself to assume the commission of the Lord. Yet God KNEW that Jehu would do this, and that is why He prophesied about it so long ago. It can really make our brains hurt if we think too long about it."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":24,"title":"Gathering the False Worshippers","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I guess it's an appropriate time to define Jehu. \"Jehu\" means \"Jehovah is He.\" He is serving the Lord by fulfilling these prophecies. We think, \"Boy, Jehu is a man after God's heart. He will be a good king.\" Then we get to this verse and everything turns around... at least that's how it seems. Jehu is playing into their desires of idolatry. They loved their Baal worship here. He is weeding out the Baal idolaters."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a sad moment in Israel. The whole church of Baal was full. If you see a church full like this today, it is likely that they are not worshipping the Lord. It's not impossible for a church to be this full for God, but it's certainly not common. I think prosperity preachers are just worshippers of Baal. They are fake worshippers. They pretend like they worship God, but they really just want to rob their congregants and take advantage of them. They want to play on people's emotions, rather than fill them with the Spirit of God. There are some churches that are full today because people genuinely want to worship God, but not many. Be wary of mega churches, or pastors who speak a lot about numbers. That is a sure sign that there is something off."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is something about the authenticity of God that keeps phony \"Christians\" from coming into a genuine church. They don't like the truth. They like to feel better when they leave the church. That's not what the church is for. There should be moments of this, but that is not the main focus of the church. Jesus Christ will bring joy to a genuine Christian, and terror to a phony one. That is how you can know if the church is legitimate. Who do they preach?"}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu is luring them into a false sense of security. This is quite the thing to watch. If Jehu were so serious about the Lord of Israel, why would all these worshippers of Baal think that he has suddenly changed his mind? I think it's because there have been a lot of fake \"Christians\" in charge over the years. They were once following the Lord too at one point, and it was easy for them to turn away."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It would be possible that there would be some actual worshippers of God in the assembly. Jehu did not want to kill them. He just wanted to focus on the idolaters. He wanted to make sure that the evil Baal worshippers were the only ones getting this treatment. Why would a genuine Christian be in the house of a false god? Well, I don't know. Perhaps they just wanted to see what Jehu was doing. Maybe they got word about Jehu's act here and thought, \"I knew he wasn't serious about the Lord! How could he? Let's go see what kind of man Jehu really is.\" So they had to weed out the assembly AGAIN to make sure there was no one innocent attending today."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu is going to order a slaughter of every single one of these people. None of them were allowed to leave. If a guard allowed one to leave, he would be treated as a betrayer, and his life would be taken for the one that he let go."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":28,"title":"Baal Destroyed","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we might read this and think, “Man, this guy has no mercy! He just killed a whole group of false worshippers without a single care.\" These people were awful. They were the worst kinds of religionists. They put the Satanist cults to shame today. Satanists talk about worshipping Satan with sacrifices, and drinking blood, and all sorts of weird, evil thoughts towards Satan. But compared to Baal, they are just children. The people of Baal did all that and MORE. They were murderers of the worst kind. They were also rapists, and cannibals, and terrible, forceful religionists. Jehu was actually doing the Israelites a favor by getting rid of these terrible people."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Finally (because of the acts of Jehu), Baal was completely taken care of and gotten rid of in Israel (at least for this time). They should have turned away from Baal a long time ago, but they don't ever seem to learn their lessons. We are here thinking, \"Jehu is a good king after all. He is doing everything right for the Lord! The throne is restored to Israel!\" But the story isn't over yet, sadly..."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":31,"title":"Jehu's Sin","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehu didn't turn to God. He turned to the golden calves of Jeroboam. It was no better than worshipping Baal in the eyes of the Lord. Nothing was different. God sees all false worship as the worst kind of worship. Whether you worship Satan, or you worship Mary, to the Lord it's the same."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God is going to grant Jehu four generations on the throne because of his ability to take care of the lineage of Ahab and destroy the people of Baal. But God will eventually have to deal with Jehu's false worship, and the sins of that will be something that is passed down to the next generations."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":36,"title":"Jehoahaz's Reign","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has planned this moment for a long time now. The Lord is beginning to \"cut Israel short,\" and they are being led into captivity by Assyria. The northern kingdom will become desolate because they are not turning from their sins, and they will not turn to the Lord. God is giving them over to what they want (ultimately)."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This has been a long time coming. We knew that it would lead to this. It's been warned about for generations. In fact, it is Moses that warns about captivity if they turn away from the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When thou shalt beget children, and children's children, and ye shall have remained long in the land, and shall corrupt yourselves, and make a graven image, or the likeness of any thing, and shall do evil in the sight of the LORD thy God, to provoke him to anger: I call heaven and earth to witness against you this day, that ye shall soon utterly perish from off the land whereunto ye go over Jordan to possess it; ye shall not prolong your days upon it, but shall utterly be destroyed. And the LORD shall scatter you among the nations, and ye shall be left few in number among the heathen, whither the LORD shall lead you. And there ye shall serve gods, the work of men's hands, wood and stone, which neither see, nor hear, nor eat, nor smell. But if from thence thou shalt seek the LORD thy God, thou shalt find him, if thou seek him with all thy heart and with all thy soul. When thou art in tribulation, and all these things are come upon thee, even in the latter days, if thou turn to the LORD thy God, and shalt be obedient unto his voice;","ref":"Deuteronomy 4:25-30"},{"kind":"para","text":"They have had chance after chance from the Lord for generations now, and the Lord has sustained them for this long. Now they were so far gone that the Lord had to let them go here. He will still get their attention eventually, but it will be after such a brutal and terrible captivity."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the entrance to the captivity of the northern kingdom. \"Jehoahaz\" means \"Jehovah has seized.\" Fitting name for a specific time. We might also notice that scripture doesn't outright say if Jehu was a good or bad king. That's because he did a lot of good things, but he still didn't turn to the Lord. Personally, I believe that to mean he was a bad king. The spiritual nature of a leader is what determines if they are good or bad."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Twenty-eight years is a decent chunk of time. I think that is a great run personally. Some of these kings are just terrible, and so their time is cut extremely short. Jehu was not terrible, but he also wasn't a saint. But his lineage will continue the kingdom, even though the kingdom is falling apart as we progress."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":36}]},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we followed the conquest of Jehu. He had taken it upon himself to kill the entire line of Ahab. He did just that by killing all the direct decendents of Ahab in the northern kingdom. A few decendents came from the southern kingdom that he was able to reach as well, but there was still plenty for him to kill."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Athaliah will help Jehu kill the remaining decendents. Obviously, she is not helping him kill them for the sake of Jehu, but she wants complete authority over the throne. If Jehu and Athaliah are successful with their mission, they will fully iliminate the lineage of Christ and God will not be able to keep His promise like He is supposed to. But God ALWAYS keeps His promise."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"One Survivor","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The mother of Ahaziah would insinuate that this is the daughter of Ahab and Jezebel. This lady doesn't seem to be any better (or different) than her own mother. Jezebel was a cruel and mean person, but Athaliah seems to be worse. She is upset about her son being killed, and while Jehu is killing the seed of Ahab in the northern kingdom, Athaliah is going to kill the seed of her son in the southern kingdom. You might wonder why? Well, so do I! I assume she is killing her own grandchildren for a few reasons."},{"kind":"para","text":"The one prominent reason is because she is afraid of a grandchild coming in and taking authority away from her. While Ahaziah was on the throne, she still had influence. If there was another on the throne, that influence would be gone. The further the distant relative to her, the less influence she would have. So to avoid that, she killed the entire seed of Ahaziah so she could stay in charge."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was one child that escaped. It's important to note this because he is the only reason that the lineage of Christ succeeded. If Joash had died, Christ would not have come through the lineage of Judah. That was the promise that God made to David. That line needed ONE child to survive for the promise to be kept. \"Joash\" means \"given by the Lord.” You'd think with the influence of his mother and father, he would be a terror to the people. You'd also think that because he lived in such a terrible time in the history of Israel, he would become a hardened person. That will not be the case with Joash."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This boy was hidden for a long time. Athaliah would be looking for anyone related to Ahaziah for the rest of her life to make sure the throne remained hers. She was going to be the sole power over Judah. The kingdom was still divided. As long as she was on the throne, she would have protection from Jehu ever getting to her."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":9,"title":"Protecting the King","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that no one was happy with the reign of the queen. They are making pacts and covenants with each other behind the queen’s back, and they want to turn this around on her. They want her evil reign to end. They know there is a rightful line for the throne, and Athaliah couldn't stop the promise from being kept."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had to make sure this boy was kept safe. There would be a company of men and soldiers guarding him with their lives. The queen would not have a chance to get to him (or anyone else for that matter). They REALLY needed the queen to be dethroned, and so they are going to do whatever it takes to make sure that happens, and this seven-year-old boy becomes the king instead. Yes, you read that right. He is going to be king of the land at seven years old."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, they did it right. They weren't going to mess this up because it would mean the doom of the future of all Israel. Of course, the Lord was going to protect this line anyway. Again, that was His promise. They knew it was the promise. They felt relief when they saw that there was one surviving member of the king. I believe they had lost all hope that God was going to keep His end of the deal. They felt away from THEIR end of the deal, so why wouldn't God fall away from His? Well, God doesn't work based on the loyalty of His children. He has His own mind, and He can never lie. He only speaks the truth, and what He says comes to pass will surely come to pass. So, He has made a promise of the Savior through the line of David, and He is going to keep that promise of the Savior through the line of David even if all of His children are completely against Him."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":12,"title":"God Save the King","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were in this room for one thing: They are going to make this child their king. This will be the most powerful seven-year-old boy in all of existence. It's interesting that they want this seven-year-old to be king over a full-grown lady. They trust the authority of a seven-year-old over an evil old woman (that should tell her something). The protection that this boy has is like nothing you have ever seen before. It puts our Secret Service protection to shame today, honestly."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yes. GOD save the king. God save the world, honestly. God save the people that were around the king too. God must save everyone because everyone seems to be messing everything up, and they can't tell which way’s up or down. They don't know the difference between their left hand and right!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?","ref":"Jonah 4:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"How far gone we have gotten from this chant. Have we heard this in a long time? I think the last time we heard the children of Israel say this was with Saul, if I'm not mistaken."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Samuel said to all the people, See ye him whom the LORD hath chosen, that there is none like him among all the people? And all the people shouted, and said, God save the king.","ref":"1 Samuel 10:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think they truly meant it with Saul. I think after they saw the reign of terror Saul had presented later on, they regretted ever asking for a king. Then David comes along, and there is civil war between these kingdoms, but he eventually unites them. Now we are here, and the tribe of Judah is almost left without a king. They really needed the intervention of God at this time. It was due for them to remind themselves of who the real Savior actually is."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"Athaliah Killed","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It must have been quite the celebration as they shouted \"God save the king.” Athaliah hears it and wonders what that could possibly mean. To her knowledge, there was no one left that could become a king. She had killed them all. So she comes to the temple to see what they are talking about:"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She sees the boy; her mind is reeling. She can't figure out how she could have missed one, and she doesn't believe that this boy can be a part of the royal line. How could this be so? How could she have missed one? So she does the only sane thing a person of her age can possibly do. She tears her clothes, cries, throws a tantrum, and shouts \"Treason!\" over and over. Let's see how that idea works out for her."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They still had respect for the house of God. Even if this kid was not a part of the royal line, they didn't want Athaliah to be their ruler anymore. They needed her gone. She could shout treason all she wants, but at the end of the day, she is going to be taken care of, and the people are going to have a new ruler. Thankfully, they could change rulership in the right way, and they didn't have to commit any sort of treason."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"And this ends the terrible reign of the throne of Athaliah. I don't think it could have ended quickly enough. They were waiting for this boy to become coherent before putting him on the throne. They wanted him to know and understand what it was that they were doing. I'm sure he still didn't grasp the full gravity of the situation, but as long as he was under good Godly authority, he would not repeat the same mistakes as his parents."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":21,"title":"A True Revival","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is what the people needed this entire time. This is what will make the people prosper. It made them prosper before, so there was no reason that it wouldn't make them prosper now. All they needed to do was dedicate their devotion to God alone. While Joash is the king, Jehoiada is the brains behind him, and that is a good thing for Joash. Joash wouldn't understand the reasoning behind making God the primary focus, so Jehoiada is going to train him up to understand that. What a great teacher for Joash."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jehoiada\" means \"Jehovah knows.” What a fitting name isn't it? Jehovah certainly knows everything. He knows what is best for His children. He knows what is best for the leadership of His people, Israel. He knows that there is a great burden to these people, and the burden is going to become greater. He knows what He is doing by allowing evil people like Athaliah to reign for a time. He knows about your heartaches and pains, and your trials and temptations. He knows about your victories and defeats. He knows everything there is to know about you, and about those that don't WANT Him to know. He knows it all. That is such a great name."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have seen kings come in and the false altars broken down before. We have seen Jehu take out the people of Baal. But this (I believe) is the first time that the whole of Judah has cohesively done it together. They are in unity to clean up their land. They are done with false gods, and done with their burdens carried by worshipping these fake images. They are done with bad leadership, and phony judgement. They want God and God alone. They want to put things back in their rightful place and start prospering again. They want to be on fire for the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a revival in the land of Judah that's taking place here. I think this is one of the only times that revival comes. I think there are glimpses of it throughout our times reading from Saul to Joash, but this is the most genuine spark of revival fire I have recognized this entire time. They had to REALLY be beaten down to get here though. They have learned a lot of lessons. If only this revival lasted forever. If only ALL revivals lasted forever."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have heard about a lot of different \"revivals\" taking place throughout history. I will just identify six at the least, here in America. We can look at the \"Great Awakening\" in 1730. It lasted for 10 years. Roughly 30,000 people were saved at that time. Then we had a SECOND Great Awakening in 1805. We have just recorded that \"tens of thousands\" were saved. But that only lasted ONE year. There was a \"Prayer Revival\" where ten thousand members would meet for a year straight every single day for prayer. That was in 1857. A revival took place under the preaching of D.L. Moody in 1905. Roughly 100,000 people dedicated their lives to Christ. Another revival in 1949 with Billy Graham. The Jesus Movement in 1970. There have been some acclamations of revivals happening today as well (whether true or not I could not tell)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know there is genuineness in some of these revivals happening for generations, but they are like the revivals before Joash. They are small flames that burn out quickly. We need a Joash revival to happen today. We may not get that, but one could pray. All it took was for one man to turn the people back to God. This priest was serious about the motive to turn to God, and because of it, the land will flourish for a time."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine (to this king) the throne was giant, the crown was giant, his house was giant. He probably didn't know what luxury living even looked like. It was not that long ago that he had to live in hiding, and away from the evil hands of his grandmother. Now he gets to sit on the throne and be the face of the kingdom’s leadership. That is quite a turnaround."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will see that Jehoash and Joash are (for some reason) interchangeable. The writer of Kings does not make keeping up with names very easy. Either way, Judah has a rightful king, and he's only seven years old. Now, anyone that reads this account will likely think like me, \"Surely he is going to mess this up.” We will actually be surprised. Thankfully, he has a lot of level-headed people around him to point his leadership in the right direction. He's a prodigy (of sorts) for the kingdom, and he will do well by it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Joash did that which was right in the sight of the LORD all the days of Jehoiada the priest.","ref":"2 Chronicles 24:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoiada will be his compass. Eventually, though, Jehoiada will die. Then Joash will be left to figure out the leadership of the country on his own."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":21}]},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw the evil reign of Athaliah and her desire to kill the line of Azakiah (her son) so that she can remain as the power of Judah. She misses one little boy named Joash, and when Joash was old enough (seven years old) he would be placed on the throne. Obviously, Athaliah finds out and it does not sit well with her. She throws a tantrum and they end up killing her so Joash can take his rightful seat. Joash is known as a good king, but he does not take down the high places and that creates some problems for the kingdom still in the future."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter we will see the type of man Joash is, and the great influence Jehoiada has over the king. The northern kingdom is facing some battles and difficulties too that is not being addressed at this moment because we are focused on the situation down south with Judah. Joash wants to take some time to repair the Lord's temple, and in the midst of it the king of Syria comes to try and overthrown the kingdom of Judah."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Jehoash Reigns","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joash (or Jehoash as the writer is now calling him), reigned for forty years. That's a pretty good stretch of time for one king. I'm thankful that our nation has a four-year limit for our leaders. Some leaders are just so profoundly dull and incompetent to deal with them any longer. If we had to be under the reign of our current president (2024) any longer than this, we would be doomed for complete and utter destruction. Perhaps if the kings had term limits, things would have been a little easier for them as well. That's not how a monarchy works, though. The throne is passed down. If it's not passed down, it's stolen, and the throne becomes less valuable, and the position of leader becomes more tainted. That's what we have been seeing in the northern kingdom. That is why they will be taken into captivity first. But I'm getting ahead of myself."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He did what was right in the sight of the Lord, and that is a good thing for God to write about you in His book. However, there is a key part to this verse which reads \"all his days wherein Jehoiada the priest instructed him.” As I mentioned in the last chapter, Jehoiada would die eventually. Jehoash would do what was right only because he had direction to do it. That is (sadly) how a lot of leaders work. Even in the churches, we see that many pastors will only be led by other pastors’ advice. They don't have a mind of their own. They will do what they are told by the traditions and lineages of the people around them, rather than looking to the Lord themselves and receiving instruction."},{"kind":"para","text":"By the way, that is the type of relationship the Lord wants from all of His creation. He wants to be able to personally direct you. He doesn't want to go through any other mediator other than Jesus Christ and the Holy Spirit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus;","ref":"1 Timothy 2:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"So if we are getting our direction through any other means besides God directly, we are going to ruin our own testimony. That is why we should be wary when we see a church specifically controlled and geared by its staff. If people are the ones making the decisions, you can bet that they will always make the wrong ones."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The high places… I have mentioned this before, but I believe the high places are a symbol of lustful sin. The people had a hard time taking away \"high places.” I think there are thousands of Christians who have a hard time with that still today. Does this mean God is completely done with you if you struggle with lust? No. I think the Lord extends mercy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Moreover the law entered, that the offence might abound. But where sin abounded, grace did much more abound:","ref":"Romans 5:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Sin (of any sort) should be offensive to you. So when you sin, and you don't feel even a slight inclination of guilt or conviction, then you are likely not a saved man. If you are convicted by your sin, that is proof that the Lord has your heart and is working on you. If you cannot get rid of the high places in your life, you are obviously not alone. Even in the greatest revivals of Israel, they couldn't quit this sin. But there is victory. You must chip away at it every single day. The more you chip away at it with the help of the Lord, the lighter the burden becomes. The more you chip away at it, the more restful you are in the Lord."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":8,"title":"A Desire to Rebuild the Temple","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a fascinating thing for Jehoash to do. He wants the house of God to be in good condition. It has been neglected for quite some time. They have had plenty of other gods to worship, but Jehoash is commissioned to make sure the God of creation is at the forefront. Jehoiada was an obvious influence on that. Jehoash is doing a good thing here. But he had bad people in charge of taking care of it."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know how old Jehoash was when he asked for this. But he is twenty-three years old now, and the priests had not fixed anything in the Lord's temple. It was still in shambles and hanging by a thread."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We could assume that the priests that were involved with gathering the money were simply pocketing it. They would rather keep the money for themselves than give it to the Lord. David wanted to glorify God with His own house, and Solomon completed that desire, and the people left it in ruins. If that is not a symbol for today, I don't know what is."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have a lot of well-meaning men who want to dedicate their works and times to the Lord’s house, but the people that are actually in charge of it are not using it for the Lord. The priests were tasked with gathering money for the Lord. They did that through the people of Judah, and those people thought that the priests were putting it to good use. They weren't doing that though. We are not told to what use they were putting it to. We can just assume it was probably nothing better than taking care of God's house."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are dedicating something to the Lord, it is wise of you not to take it away from Him. It's not that God can somehow use the money. It's about how much love and heart you really have for the Lord. That's what He wants. The temple was abused by mankind. God will have a better temple built in Christ. But even that temple will be abused by mankind..."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":16,"title":"Repairing the Temple","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoiada created a lockbox for the money to be kept in so that no one knew how much money was there, and that way it would be fair to take what was in it to repair the temple. There were only a couple of people who would be allowed in that box, and Jehoiada would focus on the spiritual nature of those people rather than their title or status. I think a lot of churches have problems with people placement today. They look to their resume rather than their zeal. If you want to put any person in leadership, you should see if that person is saved rather than if that person is savvy."},{"kind":"para","text":"You can have a business mogul in charge of the money at your church, but that doesn't make him an honest man. You can have a motivational speaker at the pulpit, but that does not make him a man of God. You can have a politician at the desk of your school, but that does not make him a truthful teacher. You can have the best construction company at your disposal to fix the holes in your walls, but that does not make them an honest and inexpensive option. The spirit (alone) is what should determine whether or not a person is capable for ministry. It should not be by their resume. I can't stress that enough."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They finally could give the money to the proper people to do the proper work. Even still, the most prominent motive for this work should be the spiritual zeal of the workers. I'm sure the people working on this project were not Godly people. They probably had a few in there, but these people were just good at their jobs more than anything else. We know it's possible for saved men to do the work of these people. We saw that with Elisha not that long ago. How I miss the ministry of Elisha. That was refreshing. We haven't had very many refreshing moments in the history of Israel lately. In fact, Elisha seems to be a forgotten figure at the moment. But the point is, Godly people were just as good for the work as ungodly ones. They just needed the work to be done."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It would be slow going for the rebuilding process, but it would get done over time. There was a lot to fix up. Things haven't looked good for this temple basically since Solomon's son came to the throne. He let everything be taken from this temple, and now everything had to be replaced. To replace all of this would take a lot of time and wisdom. Something that this kingdom doesn't really have. They never WILL have it again, because it seems to have ended with Solomon."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The priests doing the accounting were faithful men. They were giving the money to the right people. Jehoiada carefully chose people who could be trusted rather than the whole assembly of priests. It's sad that there were priests in the Lord's temple who were not doing their jobs like they were supposed to."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The priests were still getting payment for their faithfulness. They had a portion of funding still being provided to them. If the other priests had been wise stewards of the money given to them, they would be taken care of as well, but they ruined it by not dedicating the money properly."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much: and he that is unjust in the least is unjust also in much.","ref":"Luke 16:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord tests His people with little before He gives us any more to handle. Honestly, that is a good way to test anyone that you are preparing for leadership. If they have a desire to be a leader in the church, give them a little ministry and see how they fare. I've seen people become offended by their pastor for having such a small ministry. The pastor gives them something little to take care of and instead of them being grateful for being considered at all, they become offended and reject the ministry. They want something big or they don't want anything at all. That is not the way the Lord works, and it should not be the way in which a pastor works."},{"kind":"para","text":"On the contrary, I've seen pastors give BIG ministries to small-minded people. They just give them what they want, and the ministry swallows them whole. They are no good for it. Sometimes they just neglect the ministry they have been given. I'd rather a person show their heart through a small ministry than neglect and fail a large one. But what do I know? I'm not a pastor. And what a shame that anyone would look at ministry in a godly way."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"title":"Hazael's Attack","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Syria was doing a number on the northern kingdom at this time. They were starting to lose territory, and now Hazael wants to go after the southern kingdom."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He paid off Hazael. To be fair, these things wouldn't be worth much if they were all dead and the kingdom was in disarray. He bought himself some time to build up Judah and prepare it for another potential future takeover. The Lord (as we will see) is going to take care of Judah for a time. He has reason to protect them at the moment. There are still some things that are not addressed, and the Lord will deal with that in time, but because of Jehoiada's movement to turn the people to God, there is still protection and movement."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":21,"title":"Joash Killed","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might read this and think, \"But why?” Well, Chronicles will address this a little deeper, but the premise of it is that evil people do evil things. Joash is a man that makes some mistakes. One of those mistakes was in killing Jehoiada's sons. The people didn't like this. But we can look deeper into that when we get to that book."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Amaziah\" means \"Jehovah is mighty.” Thankfully, this will be a good king too. The tribe of Judah will have a good longevity before captivity, but captivity WILL be inevitable. They will not stay focused on the Lord, and that is the reason the Lord hands them over. They will be completely against God. They will have no remorse or care, and so they will dwell under the authority of people who are just like them. Babylon will be their captors."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":21}]},
    "13": {"chapter":13,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we focused mainly on the reign of Joash in the southern kingdom. We have taken a slight break from the problems up north to focus on the happenings of the kings in the south. Joash was a good king, but he had made some dumb mistakes later in his life. He desired to help repair the temple of God, but it came with some difficulties that made it take longer than he has expected. He eventually finds a solution to the problem and fixes the temple back up. He is later killed and his son Amaziah takes the throne."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we are heading back up north to the kings difficulties there. We get to see the ending of Elisha and his final actions before he is taken home. Elisha's prophecy allows Israel a few more years of victory, but they run out of that in due time because of their kings lack of faith, and their nations lack of repentance. Isaiah will display the issues happening in the land, and why the captivity is necessary."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"The North's Defeat","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are heading back to the Northern Kingdom. We have spent a good amount of time talking about the Northern Kingdom through these chapters, honestly, but I think there is a lot more to talk about because of how terrible the kings have been. Not even Jehu is technically considered a good king, and he cleaned up quite a bit. During the reign of Joash, Jehu's son takes the throne, and he is another bad king. Of course, this will lead into a series of troubles for the northern kingdom."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jeroboam was keen on calf-worship. That was the same thing that Jehu fell for as well. He cleaned up the prophets of Baal, but he didn’t seem to touch Jeroboam’s false gods. Because of this, he set his son up for failure on the throne. Jehu should have known better than to allow any sort of idolatry slip by, but I don't think Jehu actually knew the Lord specifically. I think he was just under the mindset that he was doing God's business. What God? Well, that wasn't up for Jehu to decide. He just knew that what he was doing was pleasing to one of the gods, and that god was blessing him for his deeds."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Syria was a really powerful nation at this time. The northern kingdom has gone up against Syria several times now, and it seems that every time they do, it begins to get worse and worse. They are not holding their own here, and the northern kingdom is really starting to feel the pain of not having the Lord deliver them. It feels (at least to Jehoahaz) that the Lord is on the side of the Syrians rather than the Israelites. And, honestly, it SHOULD feel this way. They have literally brought this onto themselves."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't see how they cannot see the issue at this point. They have seemingly completely forgotten about the Lord's work on their behalf for generations. They don't even know what Scripture is at this point. Judah will fall under that same problem later on. We will see (in Nehemiah) that the Bible is taken out and read before everyone, and everyone is acting as if this is the first time they are hearing this. That is a sad take for society. America is not too far behind."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoahaz has nowhere else to turn to. I'm sure he tried to appease every god possible, but nothing was working. So he has no choice but to turn to the God of the ark. He has to turn to the God of his ancestors past. Perhaps that God will listen. Here's a secret that seems to be missing in society today: God is always listening. He listens to your hurts and prayers. He is always watching. He WANTS to intervene, but He will not if you choose to reject Him for it. He will only intervene if there is a particular path that needs to be taken; otherwise, He often gives us what we want, and we find out it's not really a good thing."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God hears the prayer of the king and sends a savior instantly. Yet the king and all the people still turn away from the Lord as if God didn't just rescue them. They find reason to ignore God and turn to their own false gods and find favor in them instead. It truly boggles my mind. I suppose I was that way when I was younger. I grew up believing there was a God. Whether it was the God of the Bible or not is not clear. But I believed there was something, and I would pray for mercy and help, and then when mercy and help came I would instantly forget about the prayer I had made. Perhaps that is why I have so much emotional and spiritual baggage? I've addressed those things with the Lord now that I'm saved, but just because the Lord forgives me does not mean I forgive myself."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Syrian king was brutally taking out the northern kingdom. Jehoahaz doesn't seem to be learning anything from this defeat either. He thinks it's all fine, but as we will see, things are slowly decaying for the northern kingdom."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now you might read that and think, \"Hey! I thought that was the king of the southern kingdom?” Yes, but this is a different Joash. I believe that is why they changed Joash of the southern kingdom to Jehoash in our last chapter. They were preparing us to know the difference. From now on, the southern Joash will simply be known as Joash, and the northern Joash will be known as Jehoash. They are the \"same\" name in a sense, but just different spellings so as not to confuse you (if you aren't already confused enough)."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":13,"title":"Jehoash's Reign","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Can you guess (before reading ahead) if he was a good king or a bad king? I bet you could never guess what kind of man Jehoash was..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I am starting to think that these kings in the northern kingdom like living in defeat and filth. They are either completely blind to what is really happening right under their noses, or they are purposely ignorant of it. I suppose we will actually never really know. I cater more to the thought that they are just ignorant. They know what's happening, but they don't want to address it. If they address it, they have to admit their faults and actually do something to change the situation. If they address it, they will have to give up some of the sins they enjoy so well. Well, actually, they don’t. Joash in the south was still struggling with sin, but he was also focused on the Lord. That is why God considers him to be a good king."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joash left the same legacy as the rest of the kings in the land. Nothing was changing except for the power of their enemies. God did promise to allow four generations of the throne to be taken in northern Israel, but that doesn't mean they had to live long. I feel they could have had longer lives if they had simply turned to the Lord. Can you guess if Jeroboam will be a good king? I'm sure at this point the king will not disappoint us..."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":19,"title":"Elisha's Last Prophecy","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha will be known as the chariot of Israel. That's quite interesting that Jehoash is coming to visit him. It's also interesting that Elisha has been missing in action for this long, and he is suddenly making a reappearance. Obviously, there has been plenty of work for him behind the scenes, but this is the only moment we see Jehoash come to visit him. I'm assuming this was the time he turned to the Lord and asked for favor. Even MORE interesting is that Elisha is sick and he is going to die from this sickness. That was how the Lord saw fit to take Elisha out of this world. Why? Well, I don't really know."},{"kind":"para","text":"I've known preachers and pastors who have died from a sickness or cancer, and it has been something that has baffled me. Why would God choose THIS way to take someone's life? Why wouldn't He allow this preacher to die in a blaze of glory like Elijah? Why couldn't Elisha be translated too? It's a mystery for all of us, honestly. But the Lord makes the choices of life and death, and the way we die is to be just as respected as the way we are born."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For whether we live, we live unto the Lord; and whether we die, we die unto the Lord: whether we live therefore, or die, we are the Lord's.","ref":"Romans 14:8"}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Elisha still has enough strength in him to give one last illustration before his passing. He wants to prepare the king for what is to come. This is an interesting look at Elisha. He was a prophet until death. He didn't try to run from his commission. He took it with compassion and care, and loved even the hardest people to love (which was the kings of the northern kingdom at that time)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have heard that there are just some preachers you cannot put down. The Lord had made Charles Spurgeon sick in his older years, and even then he would not keep from behind the pulpit. It is said that he had deacons carry him up on stage and place him to lean on the pulpit to continue to preach until his last breath. Whether bold or stupid I can't say. But Elisha is in his deathbed, and the only thing he is concerned about is equipping the king."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was good news for the king. Elisha's hand symbolized the leading of the Lord on the king. That DOES take place (if you don't remember). Syria was a powerhouse that was really pressing against Jehoash at this time. So I'm sure there was worry in the king’s eyes as Elisha was giving this prophecy. That arrow signified their battle and victory. So I'm sure some pressure was relieved from the king’s shoulders."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Three is a strange number of times to do this. I'm sure there was some angst and anger stored up in Jehoash's heart to hit that ground far more times. Elisha doesn't say how many times to hit the ground; he just tells him to hit it. Jehoash wants to stop at three."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king was the one in charge of the outcome. Because the king doesn't have faith in the Lord, Syria is going to come back against the northern kingdom in due time. They will win for a season, but after the third battle it will be over… I'm sure Jehoash is thinking, \"Elisha has really lost it here. He must not know what he is talking about. Surely we will be victorious based on our own strengths, not based on a fortune teller.” He has learned nothing over the years… Nothing that the kings have done so far has been from the consideration of the Lord's Will. They have not asked the Lord what it is they should do. David set the example, but none of the kings following has taken it. He would pray before (mostly) every major decision, and the Lord would answer accordingly. We have not seen a king do that in quite some time."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"title":"Elisha's Last Miracle","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even in Elisha's death, he was still bringing miracles from his bones. This man was dead, and the bones of Elisha revived him. That had to be a very confusing moment for this man. We don't get any more information about what happens to the man after this here. This is all we are left with, strangely enough. It just goes to show the power that Elisha had in his legacy. He had the power and presence of God even down to his bones."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":25,"title":"Jehoash's Victories","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God was in favor with the Israelites only because of the promises made to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. He was in favor with Judah simply because of the promise made to David. God KEEPS His promises. Forever. He never goes back on them. He never says, “Well, this promise is finished, so therefore I no longer have to keep it.” No. When God makes a promise, it is not like how man makes promises. We make promises and keep them until they are fulfilled. God makes eternal promises. Those promises are kept until the Lord dies. Seeing as He is eternal, I don't think those promises will ever be forgotten."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Three times was all he could do, because that is as many as he was willing to do. He made that decision with Elisha and the arrows. He could have beaten them until they were completely gone, but he chose not to. I'm sure after the first victory, Jehoash had forgotten what Elisha had promised. I'm sure he was not aware of the other two victories that took place. He just thought it was his own strength at that point. That's just the sad reality of prideful people."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":25}]},
    "14": {"chapter":14,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we looked at the continual battle of the north country over the Syrians, and their defeat as they tried to stand up for their territory. Elisha makes a final appearance and it's towards the end of his life. Jehoash (of the northern kingdom) comes to pay tribute to him in his last moments, and Elisha gives one last prophecy about the next three battles that the northern kingdom will face with Syria. They will win (even with little)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see the prophecy of Elisha come true, but the southern kingdom tries to take a stand against the north. The northern kingdom doesn't want to wage war at this time, and so Jehoash tries to talk Amaziah out of it, but he simply won't listen. It would seem as if all hope is lost for the north, but God is moving His children in specific ways for a purpose that needs to be fulfilled."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Amaziah Reigns","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now (in this verse) the writer is using the same spelling for both names… He doesn't seem to want to make it easy for anyone. I will just call the southern Joash \"Joash\" and the northern Joash “Jehoash.\" I feel that it's just easier to separate them this way. At the time that Jehoash is reigning, Judah has Amaziah. Joash was killed if you don't remember from a couple of chapters back."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the rest of the acts of Joash, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the chronicles of the kings of Judah? And his servants arose, and made a conspiracy, and slew Joash in the house of Millo, which goeth down to Silla.","ref":"2 Kings 12:19-20"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joash had some problems in his worship. He didn't take down the high places, if you don't remember. Because of this, Amaziah is left with the same enticements."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, Amaziah is referenced as a good king. How is it possible for him to worship false idols and still be a good king, and yet the northern kingdom worships false idols and their king is “bad?” It's all based on the spirit of the individual. 1&2 Chronicles will illustrate this better, but these kings were not just doing bad things. They were completely rejecting God altogether. The northern kingdoms’ \"bad\" kings don't consider the Lord at all. They don't try and take His salvation. Whereas the southern kingdoms’ \"good\" kings are trying to follow God specifically, but they have enticements all around them pulling them away."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see this same theme in our politicians today. Most politicians play the role of religion quite well. They say, \"Oh yes, I am a Christian\" or \"I am a Catholic.\" but they have no relationship with the Lord or His church in any shape or form. Abraham Lincoln went to church. George Washington often held meetings in churches. Dwight Eisenhower was a frequent attendee at church. They could easily pull the \"I'm a Christian\" card if they chose to, because they had proof to back them up. These other politicians have no leg to stand on. They are religious to appease people, not because they actually care. That is how it is in the northern kingdom."},{"kind":"para","text":"They don't believe in the God of Israel, they just say they do to appease people. In the southern kingdom, the kings were genuinely serious about following the Lord, even if the people around them or under them chose not to. If Amaziah wants to reign properly, though, he is going to have to get rid of those high places. They will just be an enticement to him and his people until he does. Worse yet, it will be an enticement to his children if he does nothing about it."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":7,"title":"Sins of the Father","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As should have been the case. These people did a heinous thing, whether it was justified or not. They killed their own king because of something that he did that they didn't like. David wasn't as calloused as this with Saul, and I believe David had more of a right to kill him. So Amaziah takes care of the problem quickly, just in case they might be a problem for him in the future. What was stopping them from giving him the same treatment?"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He did this according to the command of the Lord. The sins of the individual will be addressed by the individual alone. That is a law of Moses. I believe that is why Joshua doesn't kill the entire family of Achan. But that is besides the point; we have already spoken about that in Joshua."},{"kind":"para","text":"I find it fascinating that Amaziah knows this law though. He has learned it from something, and I assume the people that Jehoiada set up to be around the king were people who rehearsed scripture to the king. They kept him in a good line. That is another way you can know the genuine spirit of a leader. Does he keep to God's word above everything else? If not, then you can pretty much rightfully assume they are not real Christians like they say they are."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Edom had been a problem for the southern kingdom. Remember, Esau is Edom. They were to be working together, but Edom was a continual enemy to the Israelites. So Amaziah takes care of them and takes one of their cities and possesses it."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"title":"Amaziah Continues to Fight","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is still conflict between the northern and southern kingdoms, let's not forget. They were still in civil war with each other. Amaziah wants to end things with this king in a battle for the kingdom. If Amaziah wins, he can successfully unite the kingdom back together under one authority. If Jehoash wins... well it wouldn't be good for anyone. So Jehoash sends back a letter:"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoash has no room for battle at this particular point. The Syrians have killed most of his men. He was left with about ten thousand foot soldiers according to our last chapter. Whereas Judah has plenty of men to spare. The letter that Jehoash sends back is a letter of precaution. He is telling Amaziah to simply \"stay home.” He doesn't want to escalate a battle at this time. It would be a loss of innocent casualties over nothing at the moment. Amaziah would just be causing more harm than good if he chooses to battle here. If Amaziah loses, it will look very bad for Judah. And if they win, it will look tyrannical. Nobody will come out on top here."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":14,"title":"Judah's Defeat","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoash didn't want to battle. In all honesty, Jehoash was the voice of reason here. He was making the wiser decision to refrain from battle. But Amaziah was too prideful and headstrong to listen to anything that Jehoash had to say. He was on a victory high, so he thought it was wise to take down the northern kingdom while the iron was hot."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"May we not forget that these are their own people. They are not in agreement with each other, but they are all blood relatives. That is such a sad scene. Amaziah's desire for blood put him and his kingdom in a bind. Their defenses were destroyed, and their wealth was stolen by the northern kingdom. They ended up losing far more than they were going to gain."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"title":"Jeroboam Reigns","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are catching back up to where we had left off in our last chapter. Of course, if you want to get into the deeper story of these battles, 2 Chronicles is where you will want to go (specifically around chapter 25). But 2 Kings needs to move along; otherwise, we won't get to the downfall of Israel."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":22,"title":"Amaziah's Death","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's funny that this is written over and over again. It's a question that keeps coming up. \"Are they not written in the book of the Chronicles of the Kings?” The writer of Kings tasks us with answering a question. Are they written there? Well, you have to read Chronicles to find out. Spoiler alert; Yes, they are written there in great detail. They are there for you to fill in the blanks and gaps of things that you may not have known if you didn't have the information at your disposal. But again, I am keeping that information separate, so we can go through that at an individual setting instead of trying to bash our way through both books to get them over with."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Lachish was a place that was to protect runaways. They were obviously not doing that anymore. It just goes to show how far gone they have become from the law of Moses."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Azariah\" means \"Jahovah has helped.” \"Elath\" means \"grove of lofty trees.” The Lord has helped them with a grove of lofty trees. I'm assuming this was a good means to help fortify their city a bit more (seeing as the northern kingdom had seriously destroyed their fortifications)."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":27,"title":"Victory and Defeat","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we go back a little bit to get some story about Jeroboam. We cannot forget some of the things he did. We questioned if Jeroboam would be a good king or not. Of course, he isn't."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jonah makes an appearance! Some people have said that Jonah is a fictitious story about a minor prophet who doesn't seem to care about the Lord's will. Yet, here is proof that he was a valuable member of the Lord, and he had helped this king take back territory that they didn't have before. He gave prophecy to his kingdom at this time, and that was a huge help to them as they were trying to maintain their land. Sadly, that will not last forever."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jeroboam was against the Lord, but the Lord allowed prosperity. Not because they deserved it, but only because He had compassion on them. They were going to turn back to Him eventually. He knew that. He saw that in the future. He is living it at the moment they are going through this. I know that to be true because Isaiah points out how directly recent Christ's death is. In Isaiah 53, he states that Christ is (currently) bruised for Israel's iniquities. We can talk about that more in Isaiah."},{"kind":"para","text":"The point is, God has compassion for these weak, sinful children, and He does not want them to be completely wiped out, but He also doesn't want them to continually reject Him. So He has balanced victory and defeat to get them weaker and weaker until there is nothing left for them to do but to turn to the Lord and request help and mercy. Sometimes that is what the Lord needs to do with us. He needs to dwindle down our pride with defeat, but give us hope with victory until we have whittled away at our sin long enough to turn to Him and request His help."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":29,"title":"Jeroboam's Death","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Are they not written there? Yes, they are. \"Zachariah\" means \"Jehovah remembers.” Yes, what a faithful name. Jehovah ALWAYS remembers. However, Jehovah's creation often forgets…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":29}]},
    "15": {"chapter":15,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: If you are starting to feel a bit weary from the same story happening over and over again with these kings of Israel, then you are getting just a small glimpse at how the Lord felt. It is a weary thing that they continue to repeat the same mistakes over and over again and they don't seem to learn any lessons from their failures and faults. You'd think something would click at some point, but it just doesn't, and that is why the Lord is wearied by it. We are getting this information in pages that takes us only a couple hours to read. God was dealing with this for literal YEARS. I couldn't imagine having to watch the pain of this for so long and refraining from doing anything. They have literally kept the Lord back from helping and serving them."},{"kind":"para","text":"In our last chapter, we got a deeper look at the northern kingdom and the political strategies of that time. The southern kingdom was doing quite well, and the king thought it would be a good idea to attack the northern kingdom. The southern kingdom ends up losing because of their pride. We get the look at the continual lineage, and change of positions in the kingdoms."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we continue that look and change of the thrones. It seems the northern kingdoms throne and leadership changes on a dime and in rapid sucession. They will not have a solid leader really ever again. This will, also, ultimately lead them into their captivity. Judah has been sitting well for a while though, and the leadership has been solid enough to look to the Lord. However, things are going to change once the northern kingdom (Samaria) is taken into captivity."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Azariah's Reign","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might be thinking, “Wasn’t there already a Jeroboam?” Yes, there was. It seems the kings were running out of names, so it's time to recycle. He was the first king of the Northern Kingdom once the kingdom was divided, and he was a bad king. This king, here, is… also an evil king, but he did a few good things during his reign. But we also look to Azariah as the next king of the southern kingdom. He will be the particular focus here."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we had a seven-year-old as the king once before, and now we have a sixteen-year-old reigning as king, and this king reigned for fifty-two years! That's a long time to reign as king. And if it matters, his mother’s name is Jecholiah. \"Jecholiah\" means \"Jehovah is able.” I believe his mother was a good influence on him. His father was probably a pretty good influence as well. That is likely why he will be a good king for the southern kingdom:"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, the high places stayed. You might say, \"Why do you keep defining the name? They don't always end up being what their name implies anyway.” Well, first of all, I find it fun. Secondly, that is an interesting thought, nevertheless, isn't it? I believe these people COULD end up like their name implies, but they have the choice (like we all do) to reject it. They could have been people that lived a life that honored their God-given name. One can only hope that Jecholiah lived a life that represented the ability of God. That would leave Azariah with the capability to flourish. \"Azariah\" means \"Jehovah has helped.” That is the hopeful legacy that this king will leave behind. That's the example he should be showing to the people that are following underneath him."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might be thinking, “Well, that's extreme, isn't it? Is it because of the high places? The other kings didn't receive this kind of punishment for it.” Well, I'm sure that has partly to do with it. But no, we miss some of the story because of what the next verse says:"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you want to know what happens to Azariah (or Uzziah as he's called there), you will have to read in Chronicles (chapter 26 specifically). The basis of it was that he went into a place he was not supposed to, and it went directly against the Lord, so the Lord gave him a punishment for it, and he KNEW he wasn't supposed to do it. That's as much as I will spoil."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They bury royalty. I want to point that out because I've heard a lot of people say they want to be cremated when they die. I think that's fine. I don't have a problem with cremation, but when you look at scripture, every person of power is buried. Every person that is hated is thrown to the wayside or burned. They disrespected the dead bodies if you were not a liked person. I think you can honor a person with cremation, but there is nothing more honoring (according to God's Word) than a burial."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jotham\" means \"Jehovah is perfect.” Another name that should be simple enough to live up to. Jehovah certainly is perfect. His ways are perfect. His direction is perfect. His judgement is perfect. His leading is perfect. It would be a perfect idea to follow the mind of our perfect Lord."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":12,"title":"A Stolen Throne","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, in the northern kingdom, we have Zachariah coming into the picture. We got a glimpse of him in our last chapter, and we are hoping that this is the one, right? But of course, nothing has changed, and it won't change for the foreseeable future."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, \"Zachariah\" means \"Jehovah remembers.” This name plays a double role. Not only does Jehovah remember you and your pains, He also remembers your sins when you are against Him. He will not forget your trespasses if you have turned away. That is ultimately what makes Zachariah an evil king. He goes against God. He might have been smart, but he was evil. That is for every leader in every position in every place on this Earth."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might be good at your job, and you might be a smart leader, but that does not negate the fact that you can be evil. If you are against the Lord, you are evil. Period. There's no debate about it. Our actions (without the consideration of the Lord) are always only evil."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually.","ref":"Genesis 6:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"That statement is true throughout the rest of history. We see that we always lead ourselves to evil things when we place ourselves in charge of anything. We run things into the ground."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yet again, the throne was stolen. It's not a surprise up there. The throne has been stolen over and over again, and there is no way to keep it from happening anymore. The northern kingdom is just anarchy at this point, really. I know there are a lot of people who hate government because of the evil that spews out of it. They are right to think that, but they also have to understand that the people fall if the leader is not strong. If there is no solid truth to walk on, people will scatter, and it just becomes perversion and sadistic quite quickly. We are seeing that play out over and over again in the northern kingdom of Israel. It would not have been a fun time to live there at the moment."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The writer isn't asking the question anymore. He says, \"They are there. If you haven't answered the question for yourself yet, I'm just going to tell you, they are there.” If you want deeper information, that is the place to go."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Four generations. It didn't last long, and the last generation was literally killed in front of everyone, and the throne was stolen. But God kept His promise nevertheless. Every generation was worse and worse, and we will see that Shallum is... Not going to last long, actually."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"The Throne Stolen Again","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shallum\" means “Retribution.\" \"Retribution\" means \"punishment inflicted on someone as vengeance for a wrong or criminal act.” He certainly lived up to his name by killing the king and stealing the throne. Sadly, he will get what he has given. Actually, it's not that sad because this man would have been no better for the throne. He would have been evil too. It's already a pretty evil thing to kill the king and steal his seat."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"After one month, Shallum was killed. That was a quick transition. Now we have Menahem on the throne. \"Menahem\" means “Comforter.\" That sounds very hopeful, doesn't it? We cringe at the thought that he will not live up to his name..."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You'd think, \"How much could a man really do with one month on the throne?” Well, apparently, it was enough to give further detail of it in Chronicles. It seems he was working on conspiring against some people (likely in his own land). He was just a man out for blood, I presume."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, doesn't this just bring comfort to your eyes… This man was even meaner than the previous king. Shallum conspired but didn't act. Menahem simply acted on impulse. He believed he deserved authority, and when it wasn't given to him, he slaughtered everyone. He even killed pregnant women who had no way of defending themselves. He had no morality or care for his power. He just did whatever he wanted because he had the army to do it. What a terrible man."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think there is nothing weaker than for a person (or people) to attack a woman while she is pregnant. That is her most vulnerable state. If you are the type of person to hurt a pregnant woman (physically or emotionally) on purpose, you deserve the worst kinds of punishment both here on Earth and in the pits of hell. And honestly, that is what you will get. That is what Menahem will get too."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":22,"title":"Menahem's Reign","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"TEN years of this kind of torment. That is too long in my opinion."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, of course. Is any of us surprised by this? I assume he would have done even worse than Jeroboam did, honestly. He was no different, but he also killed and destroyed innocent victims because he wanted to throw a temper tantrum. Ten years is too long for this kind of reign. But then again, there are kings already that have been just as terrible (though I don't know if there is anything worse than killing pregnant women)."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king of Israel robbed his own people to pay off the Assyrians. This won't last for long, because eventually they will be taken captive by Assyria because the nation of Israel will not turn to the Lord. We can see that it's all leading up to that point right here. We have seen that coming to fruition for a while now. They, honestly, deserve it judging by the way they are living and the callousness of their nature."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Pekahiah\" means \"Jehovah sees.” Great reminder to the king. God is watching. He is seeing their actions. He is paying close attention to their evil. There is no sin that can be left as a secret."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thou hast set our iniquities before thee, our secret sins in the light of thy countenance.","ref":"Psalm 90:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Neither is there any creature that is not manifest in his sight: but all things are naked and opened unto the eyes of him with whom we have to do.","ref":"Hebrews 4:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"This would also be a good reminder to those that are NOT against the Lord in this time. There were some living in the land that were actually followers of the Lord. They were few, but they still existed. It would remind them that God sees them. Among the thorns, God sees the lilies. He sees your pain and suffering, and He is addressing His remembrance on you. Even through an evil king, God gives a reminder to His people that He is watching. None of this is lost on Him."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":26,"title":"The Throne Stolen Again Again","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The northern kingdom is going through kings like they are going out of style. While Judah has had one king for a while now, the northern kingdom has had three or four kings popping up and replacing these men. That is how you know that the northern kingdom is not doing too well at the moment."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shocker. I'm shocked. Are you as shocked by this as I am? I am shocked."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Shocker. I'm shocked. Are you as shocked by this as I am? I am shocked… We get yet ANOTHER stealing of the throne. It's just a continual repetition of history at this point. Pekah (not to be confused with Pekahiah) has decided that he would be a better fit as king, and seeing as the throne can be taken so easily, he simply goes to the king and kills him with his soldiers, and takes the throne. \"Pekah\" means “Opened.\" The names are similar, but they represent different things. I'm willing to bet that you can probably guess the type of king Pekah will be..."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, it doesn't really matter what the things that Pekahiah did were. He was a terrible king, and his father was a terrible king, and it just keeps getting worse and worse through their leadership, and I'm not sure anyone really WANTS to read about the cruel and disgusting things that they did. Perhaps you are intrigued by it. It's a good source of historical accuracy, no doubt, but it's not something that is fun to read. Reading about the slaughtering of pregnant women is grotesque enough. I really miss the stories of David and Solomon..."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":31,"title":"The Captivity","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When you place people that have been under the same rule and traditions as all the rest of the land in charge of the kingdom, of course they are not going to change their stance on sin. The only reason we have any president right now that has the desire to get rid of abortion is because we have not fully given ourselves over to that sin. Eventually we will though, and there will not be a single moral president running for office that will be anti-abortion. Every single candidate will be PRO-abortion, and that will just become the law of the land. In fact, it might become (sadly) a requirement. My family and I will not submit to that rule. I'd rather suffer the terrible consequences of a bad leader than the consequences of disobeying the Lord and sinning against Him."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So now they have become captives in Assyria. They will be slaves to them. Assyria is not a nation that was for the Lord of Israel. They were very much against the God of Israel. They will be converted, and they have been still largely converted today! It's kind of fascinating to think about, and it gives more leverage to the accuracy of scripture and its history. Either way, in the middle of their captivity, Hoshea thinks it's a good time to take control, so he does what others have already done and kills the king and takes the throne."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This would be the only moment in Chronicles that should intrigue us. This is a very important moment in Israel's history. It's something that the prophets warn about, and it's a picture of the way in which the world is turning. The whole world will eventually face this same problem before the Lord's return. It will become a lost cause (or so it seems)."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":38,"title":"Jotham's Reign","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are heading back to the southern kingdom during the time of Israel's captivity. Judah is protected from the captivity at the moment, but they won't be protected forever."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jotham did some good things, but he still hasn't taken care of the high places. They have been there for years at this point. That will be their ultimate downfall. If one of the kings had taken care of that for the Lord, Judah would have been protected forever. But, of course, the Lord knows what they will do and how they will do it, so He has this all planned out and is going to use it for the future of Judah."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Are they? I'm glad the writer has asked this question again. It was stale without it."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The southern kingdom was starting to feel pressure. You might say, \"But if Jotham was a good king, why would God allow fightings with Syria and the northern kingdom?” Well, it's because of the next king, Ahaz. \"Ahaz\" means \"he has grasped.” We have lost the refined meaning of names to the world. Though Judah has had a good track record of kings at this point, they have not gotten rid of the high places and idol worship. That will cause the main problem here with this king. Ahaz will not be a good king…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":38}]},
    "16": {"chapter":16,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we continued to follow the line of kings coming through the north and south kingdoms. The north kingdom kept getting the throne taken by a different person frequently. They couldn't keep a king on the throne solidly. I believe that is what ultimately helps their captivity to commense. The south kingdom has had good kings over and over again, and the Lord has made them prosperous, except they haven't taken down the high places which goes against the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we will see that the high places will become a problem for the leadership of Judah. Ahaz is on the throne, and he is an evil king before the Lord. We will see why and how he is evil in this chapter, and it will seem as if all hope is potentially lost for the southern kingdom as well (because the northern kingdom is being taken into captivity from Ahaz's payment to the Assyrians)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Judah's Evil Idolatry","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The southern kingdom has had a good run. I wonder what happened with Ahaz? There was no reason for him to turn from the ways of his father. Jotham was a good king. What changed? Well, I believe that the sins of idol worship have finally set into the leadership of Judah. They did not do anything about it for years, and the reason that God has told them to get rid of it far beforehand was for this cause right here. He was protecting His children. They have rejected the protection, and now it will turn and bite them for it."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Where did he even learn this? He killed his own son for a false god. There are so many terrible things a person can do, but this is the worst one. Killing your own child for a god that isn't even real. That's unimaginable."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahaz seems to be enjoying going against the Lord as much as possible. Jotham didn't seem to train him up in the way he should go. He seems to have neglected his leadership for his son, and now Ahaz (as the leader) is muddying up the waters of his lineage’s legacy. He had examples, but it seems he wasn't paying any attention. I do still blame the fact that the high places were not taken down. The reason that the writer of Kings keeps pointing it out is because he knew that it would ultimately become a problem for the future of Judah."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"title":"Israel and Syria","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At this time, Northern Israel and Syria are confederate with each other. They are working together to try and destroy their brothers in the south. We can read more about that in Isaiah, but this is certainly a poor decision on behalf of the northern kingdom. This is, of course, just one of MANY poor decisions. We have read about all the poor decisions, and we have seen that it all leads up to the north’s ultimate captivity. Being confederate with Syria was not going to be the kind of prosperity they were hoping it would be. Even against Ahaz (who was a bad king). God is not done working on the southern kingdom yet, so he doesn't allow the Syrians and Israel to win this battle. Once again, we are told the back story of all of this in Isaiah."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":9,"title":"Judah and Assyria","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the captivity of the north kingdom ultimately takes place by the decision of the king of the south kingdom. They sold their brothers into slavery. Of course, the Lord has warned that this would take place far before any of this happened. Moses was the one that told them it would happen. The north kingdom was no better than trying to kill their brothers with the help of an enemy. Syria was not doing any of this for Israel’s best interest. Assyria isn't helping the southern kingdom for Judah's best interest. Everyone is just looking out for themselves. I imagine that this isn't a fun thing for the Lord to watch at this time. He wanted His people to be united, and at this moment, they are extremely vengeful and terribly disunited. They downright hate each other."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":15,"title":"A New False Worship","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahaz found a false altar from the Assyrians and thought it would be a good idea to add it to his collection of false worship. He couldn't be a dumber king if he tried. That's the sad part, honestly. I don't think he was trying to be so dumb, but that is what he is being, whether we like to call it that or not."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You think, “Well, it's no surprise; this king does not love the Lord in the slightest.” And you'd probably think that it can't get much worse. But it does..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ahaz takes an altar dedicated to God and puts it as a dedicated altar to Assyria's false god. Worse yet, when we read in Isaiah, we find that Isaiah tells him about the Lord providing him with victory. It's the God of Israel that Isaiah points to. He also warns him about the trouble with idol worship. Ahaz doesn't want to listen, though, and this is the fruit of his labor now."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is causing this priest to sin with him. He is making the same sacrifices you would usually make to the Lord (according to Levitical law) but to a false god instead. This is Ahaz's way of spiritually spitting in the face of God. That's a bold strategy if I've ever seen one. He wants to play with fire (literally and spiritually). Let's see how that works out for him:"}]}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":"Ahaz's Terrible Defiance","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Urijah doesn't seem to put up a fight about it. He simply complies, even against his better judgement. There had to be something in the conscience of Urijah that said, \"Maybe I shouldn't do this. This sounds like a bad idea.” But he seems to ignore it and go on with the king’s decision anyway."},{"kind":"para","text":"SIDE NOTE: This is a message for everyone reading this today. It is okay to question the ethics of a leader if it goes against the Lord's authority. In fact, it's important that we make sure we are not going against the Lord through following our leaders’ decisions. Line everything up with scripture, and if what you are being told to do does not line up, you should bring it to the leader’s attention, even if it means you will die for it. Better you die because your leader is a sinner, than that you should die for your own sins."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is just awful. He is completely dismantling the Lord's temple so that he can use it for his false worship on this new altar. Ahaz is directly defying God, like Satan has tried to do. I believe Ahaz was a puppet for Satan. Satan had a good time with this one, because he was able to use God's own people to demonstrate God's weakness. You might be saying, \"God has no weakness.” You are right. But He certainly has a soft spot, and it's for those that reject Him. He loves those that hate Him. Even in this, He is not looking at the sins of Ahaz and saying, \"How could you? You ungrateful, good-for-nothing swine! I'll show you! I can’t wait to pour my wrath and judgement on you!” No. He's looking at this sin and thinking, \"Child. If only you knew the love I have for you. If only you accepted the proof of the future Savior. There is coming a love you don't understand here. He will show my love in the face of your evil acts. If only you turned to me. You would be saved. You would have true freedom, and you would have a means of worship far greater than these false gods. You have no idea what you are missing.” God is hurt by it. That is why His wrath builds up. He stores it, because the rejection of Him from His creation HURTS Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.","ref":"2 Peter 3:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is patient with us. He suffers our sins for us. He does it for a long time as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD is longsuffering, and of great mercy, forgiving iniquity and transgression, and by no means clearing the guilty, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation.","ref":"Numbers 14:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Ahaz still has a chance to repent, but he won’t. That is what ultimately pains the Lord. He is pained by these people's failure to turn to Him, even though He gives them every single reason to do so."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth.\" 3 John 4"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a verse that embodies the Lord. So the opposite would be true. \"There is no greater pain than to hear that my children have rejected me.\""}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might have read all this and think, \"The southern kingdom is lost.” Thankfully, it won't be (yet). Hezekiah will now take the throne. \"Hezekiah\" means \"Jehovah is my strength.” Strangely enough, Hezekiah will be a good king. How? How does he reject the lifestyle change of his father? Well, I don't know. It's a mystery that the Lord was able to get the attention of Hezekiah at all, knowing that Ahaz was such a terrible man. I (honestly) believe it was because Hezekiah was a reader. I think he read a great deal of books growing up in Judah. He came across one of them written by an ancestor of his, and it dumbfounded him. He probably read it and thought, \"I don't know what to make of this. There is so much richness in these words, I don't know what to do with it!” I am, of course, talking about Proverbs. We know he read the proverbs because he is seen putting some more in that we wouldn't have been able to read had he not found them locked away somewhere."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"These are also proverbs of Solomon, which the men of Hezekiah king of Judah copied out.","ref":"Proverbs 25:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah was inspired by these words and came to know the Lord because of it. Solomon's children didn't find the value, but thankfully God preserved these words, and it reached the eyes and ears of Hezekiah to give the southern kingdom a final push for victory. Sadly, it is also through this man that the southern kingdom will be taken into captivity. But I won't end this chapter off on a sour note; we have time to talk about that later. Hezekiah will be a good king for Judah, and he will restore a lot of what the kingdom has been missing."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":20}]},
    "17": {"chapter":17,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, We saw the relationship building of the northern kingdom with Syria, and the southern kingdom with Assyria. During this time Ahaz was king of the southern kingdom, and he made an entrance for Assyria to take over the northern kingdom (as the northern kingdom and Syria were going up to fight against their brothers in the south). We ended the chapter with a terrible sight from the king of Judah as he disassembles the temple of God to create a new false worship for a god that Assyria had made. It was a terrible end, and we aren't going to get much conclusion to it in this chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we are focusing on the northern kingdoms captivity. We have been building up to this moment for a long time now, and we got the beginnings of the pain in our last chapter. Assyria is going to come in and completely take the people by droves, and then steal the land of Israel for themselves and their allies. It is a terrible time to be an Israelite."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Hoshea Reigns(ish)","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So while Ahaz was on the throne, the northern kingdom (surprisingly) received a new king. \"Hoshea\" means “salvation.\" Obviously, this man is not the man to save Israel. This is the man that will lead the northern kingdom into captivity. But, this must come to pass for quite a few reasons. Most of the reasons being that the Israelites were asking for it by doing everything in their power against the Lord. They wanted a life of captives, so the Lord is giving them that life."},{"kind":"para","text":"The other reason (and more importantly at that) is it will ultimately bring the Savior. The Savior needed to come to this world (and specifically Israel) at one of the most difficult moments in their lives. That would give them a perfect reason to love the Savior as much as possible. Of course, there are a great deal of people who don't love the Savior, but we can talk about that later. I do believe that is why we go through so much trouble today. There are people who need the Savior in our world and in our country. They would be wise to take the warnings of trouble to turn from the way THEY want to do things, and start pursuing the Lord's way of doing things."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Assyria was receiving gifts from the northern kingdom, but they forgot a year, I guess. Instead, they gave a gift to Egypt, and that didn't sit right with Shalmaneser of Assyria. So it was time to take the northern kingdom captive. Obviously, there was a lot more happening behind the scenes as we have read through the last few chapters. Ahaz sent the Assyrians some gifts from the southern kingdom to take care of the northern kingdom, and so they began the captivity that way. It seems the next king was trying to pay Assyria off to keep their distance, but it didn't last forever. Assyria is now taking them to their own territory, and the land of Israel will begin to become desolate just like the Lord promises:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I will scatter you among the heathen, and will draw out a sword after you: and your land shall be desolate, and your cities waste.","ref":"Leviticus 26:33"},{"kind":"para","text":"But the Lord WILL bring them back:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thou shalt no more be termed Forsaken; neither shall thy land any more be termed Desolate: but thou shalt be called Hephzibah, and thy land Beulah: for the LORD delighteth in thee, and thy land shall be married.","ref":"Isaiah 62:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is all predetermined by the Lord. He knew exactly where they would end up, and He has given them warning after warning about following this path. They have not listened, and now they are reaping the fruit of their own labor. It's a bitter fruit at that."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It didn't take too long for Assyria to fully take over Israel. Israel was a flourishing country at one time. We saw at the beginning of 1 Kings that Israel was powerful and wealthy. That seems almost like a dream now that we have gotten to this point. We would assume (if we were living in Israel at this time) that Solomon was never a real person, and they never had the value that was once claimed in that land. We wouldn't be able to believe it. The only reason we would believe it today is because God's Word speaks about it and declares it. If it weren’t for Gods Word, we would not believe Israel was ever a prosperous nation today."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There were millions going into captivity at this point. The land was vast and full of people. There was plenty for the Assyrians to take to their own advantage. They scattered these people into these areas in Assyria and would use them as slave labor. When we see them come back into their land, we will find that their number has greatly dwindled from millions to a few thousand. Still a lot, but not nearly as much."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":18,"title":"The Sins of Northern Israel","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So this is part of the reason that God allows this captivity. It was that the Israelites were bowing down to false gods that they said they would NEVER bow down to. The Lord had driven these false worshippers out of the land, and the Israelites allowed them back in with open arms. They completely rejected the promises they made to the Lord, and they went absolutely against everything He wanted for them. That is a partial reason for the captivity:"}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They also did terrible things in the name of their false worship. They did things openly, and this verse says they did things “secretly.” They were murdering people, even innocent people and children, and they were doing it with a smile on their faces thinking it was the right thing to do. Murder is never an okay thing to do. It's never right. Yet they had convinced themselves through their fake gods that this is the way of life. The Israelites were not living like that when Joshua helped them take the land. They didn't live like that for quite some time. They fell for some false worship here and there, but never to this degree. They were so far gone from the Lord that they didn't even recognize who or where God was. He was somewhere in the background. Even the priests were too far gone. They would do their sacrifices for the Lord, and they would pray and read, but it was all for personal gain, and they would ADD this to their false worship as well. They worshipped the Lord, AND other gods. They had no love or compassion for the things of God alone. That is what happens when you place your trust into anything other than the Lord. You place your trust into false things, and they slowly rob you of your relationship with the one and ONLY God of the universe. You do that to yourself when you fall in love with worldly things."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They did all of this on PURPOSE. It wasn't like they accidentally stumbled into idol worship. They didn't just happen to be in the wrong place at the wrong time. They set all of these false altars up themselves and started robbing God of His due worship. They rightfully knew what they were supposed to do with their worship, and they went against it, knowing that it was displeasing to the Lord, and started doing the exact opposite of what God wanted them to do. How relevant a testimony this is to all of mankind."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are all guilty of that lifestyle. Thankfully, the Lord has gotten my heart; otherwise, I would need to answer for that in eternity. I certainly pity the people who reject the Lord today. I feel genuinely sorry for them because they have no idea how terrible of a decision they are making. The Israelites didn't seem to care about their terrible decisions either, and this is just a small picture of how the Lord has given them up and over to the enemy."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I've said this before, but God never lets His children do something evil without a warning against it first. He still warns us today. It's through His Word that we receive warnings. He will tell us what is good and bad, and it would be wise of us to listen. Sadly, we do as the Israelites, and we turn away from Him. We stiffen our necks in the opposite direction of where the Lord leads. We are proud, arrogant, and dumb people. We should learn these lessons from the Israelites in the Old Testament here, but we never seem to understand. That is what sin does for us. It keeps us from facing God."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I somewhat blame Solomon. He gave them a lavish lifestyle when he left. He didn't destroy the false altars during his time, and it became a problem from then on out. It never went out of the land. He set them up for prosperity, while simultaneously setting them up for a fall. Because they were prosperous with the idols near them, they assumed the Lord didn't care and was still going to take care of them anyway, so they never got rid of the idols, and they quickly turned away from everything the Lord had promised. It's an interesting note to make. God has made promises to these people (and He's still making promises to them; Isaiah gives the account), and they have decided to reject His promises and follow after lies. Yet, the Lord will still keep His promises even if they choose to reject Him. And He will use their captivity to prove it."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is just a small glimpse at some of the things they were doing. Obviously, there is more depth to each one of these idol worships. Like passing their sons and daughters through the fire was not just having them \"run through fire.” No. They were burning their children alive as an act of worship to fake gods. The divination and enchantments were not just \"playing with magic.” No. They were giving people false hope about futures, and healing that would never actually come to pass because they don't carry the power to heal people like that. They were also killing people with medications and ointments that they sold to \"heal\" people, and really it was just toxic and poisonous. Even the \"evil in the sight of the Lord\" line is downplayed."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you don't remember, they got to a point where they were eating their own children. They were murdering and robbing each other too. It was complete anarchy and chaos. There was no law or direction for these people. They were completely undone and lost. They had all chosen that for themselves though. We can't forget that. They were warned about how things would be, and the Lord had sent prophets and priests to them to give them warning, but they have all rejected it."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The only tribe left was Judah. It should be blatantly obvious to us (reading) and to the Israelites being taken captive that the Lord was with Judah. Why is He with them? Well, they have had nearly all good kings. There have been a couple (and Ahaz at this time was a bad king), but the Lord found favor with them because they had made it a point to turn to the Lord and keep Him first. They had their idols, sure, but they didn't put the idols above God. Well, they didn't until Ahaz. Ahaz ruins everything for Judah, and we will see what God does with Ahaz's evil. Judah won't be left for long..."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":23,"title":"Israel's Lost","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was Ahaz that gets them there quicker. It's something that has been growing for some time; it was just growing slower than the rest of Israel. But Ahaz completely turns them, and we will see that Ahaz's son has a lot of things to deal with when he takes the throne."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The writer is writing this from a future point. Israel was currently in captivity when the writer wrote this. So he is giving the account of past events to help the reader understand the history of how they got to this point. You, perhaps, didn't know that this was coming to God's children when you first started reading this book. You might not have known that Israel was going to be so far gone. You might have thought (like me) that the Lord took care of His children, and it was just one prosperous story after the other. No, we are mistaken."},{"kind":"para","text":"The sins of the land are very evident, and they are penned down to prove how depraved mankind really is. It is a lesson for all of us as readers. We will ALWAYS make the wrong decisions when we are left to our own devices. We will ALWAYS choose evil. We are evil people. You might also be thinking, \"But you said the Lord ALWAYS keeps His promises. He isn't keeping a promise by letting His children be captured. He is robbing them of the future promise of David's line being taken care of.” Actually, the Lord is still keeping that promise (as we have the gospels to prove that). Furthermore, He is keeping ANOTHER promise as well. The promise that they would be led into captivity if they chose to do evil before Him. That promise is not a happy one, but it was a promise of the Lord nevertheless, and they are seeing that be fulfilled as well. He can keep ALL promises, and He WILL keep the promise of the line of David."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"title":"Lions Sent","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"All of this territory was now Assyrian territory. Babylon is an important people to take note of. It will be by those people that Judah will be taken. They are owning a portion of the northern kingdom now, and it makes it much easier for them to reach the southern kingdom when the time comes."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord is still taking care of the land. He still wants the people there to be very clear that this land is owned by HIM. They should know this through the prophets, but of course they are going to reject God's Word on the matter. They won't listen to the prophets of God because they have their own idea of who god is and what He is capable of doing. They have their own gods. So the Lord sends lions and gives them a reminder that this isn't their land. They can't get too comfortable. He will send a lion to Judah too."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And one of the elders saith unto me, Weep not: behold, the Lion of the tribe of Juda, the Root of David, hath prevailed to open the book, and to loose the seven seals thereof.","ref":"Revelation 5:5"}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that these tribes recognize that it was GOD who sends these lions. God could get their attention better and quicker than He could His own children. That's a sad thought..."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":33,"title":"A False Fear","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is amazing. The priests were not teaching this for quite some time. They had altogether given up on everything related to the Lord. Now he is tasked with letting them know about the God of their country. I imagine that this priest is teaching them about the God of Israel and how important it is to fear God, and it is reminding himself of how foolish all of his people were to reject God before. They could have lived prosperously if they had just turned to the Lord. They could have lived freely in this land if they had paid attention to God's commands and feared Him. They lost it all, and that is something that is being slowly realized now that they are taken out of the land and treated like slaves somewhere else."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The high places were never taken down. So the new tribes living in the land were just going to use what was already there to put their own gods in place. They don't seem to believe what the priest is telling them. Why would they? The Israelites didn't even live the way the priest claims they should live, and that was evidenced by the fact that they had high places with false altars..."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Succothbenoth\" means \"the daughter's booth.” This could have a twofold purpose. One, it's likely that this was made for the women of the people to worship. They had separate places for them to worship. Or (second), it is made for some sort of lustful purpose. The second is more likely, but I don't know for sure. We do know that Babylon will be known for their acts of sex and violence, so that is likely what this altar was made for."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And upon her forehead was a name written, MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT, THE MOTHER OF HARLOTS AND ABOMINATIONS OF THE EARTH.","ref":"Revelation 17:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Nergal\" means “hero.\" I'm assuming this altar was where they believed they would get their strength. They probably praised this altar when they successfully took Israel over."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ashima\" means “guiltiness.\" It was a god of desolation. Another god they believed gave them victory for the land."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Nibhaz\" means \"the barker.” \"Tartak\" means \"prince of darkness.” It would have been better if \"Tartak\" meant \"the bite,\" but I'm not in charge of naming. Having the prince of darkness was an interesting choice by these people, though."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Adrammelech\" means \"honor of the king.” \"Anammelech\" means \"image of the king.” They used these altars to sacrifice their children. That is not too different from the false worship of Moloch, which the children of Israel also dabbled in."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But king Solomon loved many strange women, together with the daughter of Pharaoh, women of the Moabites, Ammonites, Edomites, Zidonians, and Hittites; Of the nations concerning which the LORD said unto the children of Israel, Ye shall not go in to them, neither shall they come in unto you: for surely they will turn away your heart after their gods: Solomon clave unto these in love. And he had seven hundred wives, princesses, and three hundred concubines: and his wives turned away his heart. For it came to pass, when Solomon was old, that his wives turned away his heart after other gods: and his heart was not perfect with the LORD his God, as was the heart of David his father. For Solomon went after Ashtoreth the goddess of the Zidonians, and after Milcom the abomination of the Ammonites. And Solomon did evil in the sight of the LORD, and went not fully after the LORD, as did David his father. Then did Solomon build an high place for Chemosh, the abomination of Moab, in the hill that is before Jerusalem, and for Molech, the abomination of the children of Ammon. And likewise did he for all his strange wives, which burnt incense and sacrificed unto their gods.","ref":"1 Kings 11:1-8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they built the high places of Baal, which are in the valley of the son of Hinnom, to cause their sons and their daughters to pass through the fire unto Molech; which I commanded them not, neither came it into my mind, that they should do this abomination, to cause Judah to sin.","ref":"Jeremiah 32:35"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I polluted them in their own gifts, in that they caused to pass through the fire all that openeth the womb, that I might make them desolate, to the end that they might know that I am the LORD.","ref":"Ezekiel 20:26"}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn't really seem that they \"feared\" the Lord. They gave Him a place, perhaps, but they didn't \"fear\" Him. They were wise in putting themselves low, but unwise to put the priests high. The priests were tasked with continuing in their false worship. They had no problem with it before. Now, I'm thinking that this worship has lost its taste and value. Now they are doing this worship for somebody else. They probably didn't like doing it at this point."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You can't fear the Lord and serve other gods."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"No man can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve God and mammon.","ref":"Matthew 6:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"It could be argued that the false gods were Mammon. They certainly worshipped these gods for a profit to themselves. It wasn't because these gods actually did anything for them. They had tricked themselves into believing they did. I think \"fear\" is a strong word that's being used here. \"Accepted\" maybe, but \"feared\" likely not."}]}]},{"start":34,"end":41,"title":"A Reminder We Miss","entries":[{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Their captors \"feared\" the Lord according to the writer, but the Israelites didn’t. They had wholeheartedly turned away from their God, and He had an easier time getting to know these strangers in the land. That should not be the case. Of course, the Lord should not just be seclusive to one person or people group, but He certainly shouldn't be inclusive to evil people who don't care for Him. He is getting the attention of the strangers at this point. That's a good thing. But it won't last forever. Just like the Israelites, they will turn from the Lord as well, and that \"fear\" they claim to have will become \"fear not\" towards Him."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The writer is giving a continual reminder to the reader here. This is written TO the Israelites. It reminds them that the Lord has been good to them and has guided them to prosperity. The law and statutes given to them were not to bog them down; they were there to BENEFIT them. It was given to them to help them be guided and directed by the Lord and His goodness. They mistakenly thought that though. They associated God's law with being bound, when in truth it was there to liberate them. It was there to see their need for a Savior and love the Lord. That is why I reference Deuteronomy as the book where God's law meets God's love."},{"kind":"para","text":"It has been evident since the beginning that the Lord has shown and proven His love to His people, and they have done nothing but reject it and attack the Lord for it. We don't seem to understand the love of God. We think God's love is restrictive and hurtful. We view it as unaccepting and limited. That is simply not true. His love is righteous and always seeks for YOUR good. We might not see it as good, but we cannot deny that the love of the Lord is only pointed to good and right. The world thinks that acceptance of any sort is what genuine love is. The Lord looks at His creation and says, \"I love you and want what's BEST for you.” Rather, what WE think is best is usually not best. If we have learned anything from 1&2 Kings, it should be that we do NOT know what is best for us. The Lord says what is best, and it is through His love that we can trust what He says is best to actually be the best. We might have to give up some things we really enjoy and think we love in order for us to live in His love, but it's always worth it, and so much more comforting."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Don't forget this; Do NOT fear other gods. Do not serve anything other than the Lord. That is written for the Israelites, and the church. We have more trust and value in our money than we do in the Lord of creation. That is a grave mistake."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Fear God, and you have nothing to worry about. Fear God and keep His commandments. It has been the theme through all of scripture. Solomon understood it:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is the whole duty of man.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 12:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet it's lost at this point. Wisdom is fearing the Lord;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom: and the knowledge of the holy is understanding.","ref":"Proverbs 9:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Wisdom is Christ; and you can't even know Christ unless you first have a fear of God. You cannot serve God properly if you do not fear Him. If you are not in awe and reliance on the Lord, you cannot expect the Lord to ever work with you. He does not want to work with your unbelief. He wants to work with your faith."}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The new nations come in, and they show them that fearing the Lord is something simple. Again, that won't last long. As long as \"and served their graven images\" is in the sentence, God is not going to honor that. It will eventually fall apart unless that part of the sentence is gotten rid of. False worship will always win over genuine worship. That's just the sad reality of our sinful nature."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":41}]},
    "18": {"chapter":18,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the northern kingdom finally be taken into captivity completely and entirely. Everything so far has been leading to that point, and we have been waiting a while for that to come to pass. The Lord cannot do anything any longer to protect these people who have rejecting every sort of presence and protection from the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we come back to the southern kingdom in the midst of Israel's captivity. We see the kind of man Hezekiah is in his reign for Judah, and the types of problems that Assyria brings there way while he is on the throne. It is a trying time for Israel to be sure."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Hezekiah's Reign","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are heading back to the southern kingdom. It's usually more refreshing when we get to this kingdom. Obviously, we look to the northern kingdom a lot more through these books because that is where all the action is taking place. There are a lot of terrible things happening up there. The southern kingdom has been mostly living in the right way for the Lord. At least until Ahaz. Ahaz was awful. But now we are going to look at his son, Hezekiah. \"Hezekiah\" means \"Jehovah is my strength,\" if you don't remember. It's quite a strange thing to think that Ahaz would name his child this. Perhaps it was Hezekiah's mother who had more influence on him:"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Abi\" means \"my father.” There's no indication that she was a godly woman, but I cannot see how Hezekiah would be a godly man without some sort of influence in his life."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah was a king as good as David. None of the other kings have had the privilege of doing \"according to all that David\" had done. There might have been good kings, but they were not according to the goodness of David. What a fascinating thought. His father was the complete opposite of him. This goes to prove that you can be raised in a terrible environment and still break the mold of your culture. Some people (today) are under the impression that your environment makes you who you are. Ultimately (and truthfully) it is your own mind that determines that. You can make up your mind to be different. The Lord can certainly change a person. The Lord changed the course of Hezekiah. What a blessing!"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a sigh of relief. It can almost make you cry. These high places have been a problem for a long time, and Hezekiah knew it. He saw them and said, \"It's time to destroy these things. It's time to move ourselves back to the Lord. We see how the northern kingdom faired by rejecting God. We need to avoid that.” They even went as far as to worship the brazen serpent that Moses made. That was meant to be used as a symbol of salvation. Just as Jesus would be put on the cross, the Israelites would look to the serpent on the pole!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up:","ref":"John 3:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah did a good thing by destroying it. “Nehushtan” means “a thing of brass.” There is a certain religious group (I won't name them today) who touts the idea that they have a fragment of the original burning bush that you can pray to, to remove some of your time from purgatory. You have to pay money to pray to it, but it helps you with your time for eternity. They also claim to have a piece of a thorn from the crown of Jesus, as well as the linen cloth that covered His face if you'd like to pay to pray to that. They claim to have the staff of Moses as well. In my opinion, someone should do what Hezekiah did and break them. That is idol worship. That is exactly what the Israelites did to get themselves into trouble. They took the good things of God and worshipped THEM instead of God alone. God is the only thing worthy to be worshipped. If you have to have beads, or cloths, or spend money, you are dabbling in idol worship, friend. Take a lesson from Hezekiah here."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He did what Solomon didn’t. He destroyed the high places. What an astounding king!"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a sentence! He didn't just follow the Lord. This says he \"clave to the Lord.” He held onto everything the Lord wanted as tightly as possible. He wasn't going to let go. He wanted the Lord's blessing. He was like Jacob requesting that the Lord bless him. He wasn't going to let go even if the Lord broke his hip."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He didn't waver against his enemies either. Assyria was certainly a powerhouse. They had taken over the northern kingdom, and Judah was surrounded by enemies at this point. The only reason that they could remain prosperous was because the Lord was providing for them. Hezekiah gave them the chance to prosper by his faith in the Lord. That is something we must remember, because that will be their downfall when they forget."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You would think at this point, \"What a nation now! There is no way for them to fail.” But we all fall short..."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":12,"title":"They Obeyed Not","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The writer is giving us another reminder. He's also giving us a time frame. Ahaz started the siege of Israel, but the complete captivity didn't take place until Hezekiah had reigned for four years. This had to be concerning to Hezekiah. I am certain it probably hurt him to see his brothers being taken (even if they are so far gone from the Lord). He also knows that they were worshipping false gods. The writer makes that clear as well right here. The reader should not forget that the reason for this captivity is ultimately because they turned away from the Lord. They didn't want God as their strength, so they didn't get God as their strength. They got what they asked for."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":18,"title":"Assyria's Strength","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah tried to be smart and rebel against Assyria, but it was not going well. The Lord had given some prosperity to Assyria, and Judah would not have a chance to win in this battle. You might be thinking, \"But why? Doesn't the Lord love Hezekiah's faith?” Yes. But the Lord is also working out a captivity for Judah too. All of Israel is going to fall together. Hezekiah is great, but the sins of his ancestors are going to catch up with the land. God will have to allow captivity. They had messed everything up for themselves already."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a ploy to aggravate Judah. The goal here is for Assyria to show its strength against Judah. Judah can do nothing to overthrow Assyria. But Assyria can do nothing to progress the Lord's work any faster either. It all boils down to the Lord's moving at this point. They are trying to drain the wealth and courage of Judah by bringing this whole army over to Jerusalem and just simply standing there watching. It's a scare tactic."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king sends out trusted men, and they are going to have a small conversation with each other. Hezekiah must be feeling anxious at the moment. He doesn't want war, but he will be prepared for whatever way this might go."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":25,"title":"Assyria's Threat","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rabshakeh was the mouthpiece of Assyria. He wanted to try and scare Hezekiah. He thought that Hezekiah had no strength. Not compared to Assyria. He was right."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah cannot trust his allies. He cannot trust the Egyptians. Once again, Rabshakeh was right. Those are failing strengths. They will not help Judah win in any battle against Assyria. They are a \"bruised reed.” They will just hurt Judah rather than help it."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rabshakeh seems to know a lot about what's happening in Judah. He knows that Hezekiah has taken the false altars down. He knows that they are focused on the strength of the Lord. The one thing he has mistaken (however) is what the high places symbolized. They weren't places to worship the Lord. That is left for the temple alone. No, Hezekiah cleaned up the city, and that proved (in that sentence alone) that Assyria is not as strong as they think they are. Hezekiah would hear this and think, \"That was a foolish revealing of their hand. They think we have lost our strength from the Lord, and yet it's the Lord's doing that we are still standing today.\""}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's trying to convince them that they have the greater power. He insults them by saying that they don't even have enough people to help them, even if they gave them horses to prosper. It is a tactic of Satan, honestly. He says, \"I will give you this and that if you just submit to me.” People, sadly, do submit to him, and they are left with less than what was promised every time. Satan is not going to give you anything worthwhile. He will just rob you."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is claiming (now) that it was Israel's God that has given Judah into his hands. Hezekiah can't be fooled by this. He claims he is doing the will of God to come over here and destroy the land of Judah. This will either shake Hezekiah's faith, or it will strengthen him. Hezekiah is not a fool. Hezekiah has been seeking the Lord this entire time, and he has done a good job at following the Lord's commands and purpose. If the Lord was on anyone's side, it will always be on the side of the righteous."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":35,"title":"No gods To Save","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rabshakeh was speaking to them in Hebrew, but I'm assuming he was not very good at it. This was Eliakim's stab back at him. It's a bit weak compared to all the claims of Rabshakeh so far."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Man, this guy is cruel. He's certainly convincing. He just throws insult after insult back at the Israelites here, and they are tasked with just taking it."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people of Judah have seen that the Lord has been gracious to them since Hezekiah has started pointing them back to Him. Rabshakeh may have had a strong chance to convince them before Hezekiah, but I think he has lost that lately. They have been in good standing since they have turned to God, and they would be foolish to turn away now. There might have been some standing on the wall that day thinking, \"I really hope Hezekiah knows what he is doing, because this guy seems really convincing.” Hezekiah has to stand his ground and be assured that the Lord is not speaking through this man. It's evident that he doesn't know that the high places were for pagan worship. That's a pretty important clue to Hezekiah that God is not with him."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's making promises to the people of Judah that they will have more luxuries if they just turn themselves over. They will live the good life in the land of Assyria. They will be treated like royalty. Obviously, that is not true. The northern kingdom is not living the good life at the moment. They, perhaps, don't know what kind of life north Israel is living, but they can be assured that it wasn't fun or worthwhile. They WILL be treated like slaves."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"No, because they are fake gods. Judah has turned over to the one and only Lord that can actually do something to save them. None of the other gods could save them because they don't actually exist."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Those gods are dead. He is correct. They are useless. They cannot do anything to serve and save anybody. But he does not know the Lord. He does not know the God of creation. That is his biggest weakness."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He thinks that Assyria has more power than any other god. Including the God of Israel. He thinks he is being clever by mentioning these other gods, but they cannot be swayed by the weakness of false gods at this moment. The only strength and trust they have is in the Lord God of Israel. They will have to depend on Him if they are going to see a victory."}]}]},{"start":36,"end":37,"title":"Hold Your Ground","entries":[{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were commanded to listen and not respond. I'm sure there was a lot they wanted to say, but they were given the command to hold their ground. That will be a problem for the people of Assyria, no doubt. They want a reaction or they want submission. They aren't going to have either one."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They believed what Rabshakeh said. They tore their clothes because they believed it was all over. They had no trust in the Lord, and no trust in their king. Hezekiah was doing everything exactly right, and the next step for him was to go to the Lord about this battle. He should pray to the Lord. He should speak to the priests, and receive wise counsel. We are left on a cliffhanger though, and we will have to read the next chapter to see what happens. At this moment, however, it seems as if all hope is lost."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":37}]},
    "19": {"chapter":19,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we are given a brief look at the problems that Hezekiah has addressed in the start of his reign. We see that Assyria comes with some messengers to threaten Judah and get them to comply with Assyria and become slaves with them. The messengers tell the people of Judah that it was their God that tells Rabshakeh (the messenger for Assyria) that Judah will be taken over and God is not with them. The people of Judah believe it and tell Hezekiah."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we will see what Hezekiah does with this information. He makes wise decisions here, and the Lord reveals His power to Hezekiah. It's an impressive chapter, and it shows God's moving for the king, which is something we haven't seen in quite some time."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Hezekiah to Isaiah","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Hezekiah is equally as worried about the threatenings of Rabshakeh and Assyria as well. He does the smart thing though, and he goes to the house of the Lord. The best place to turn when you feel like you are hurt and conflicted is to God. So many kings before this have tried to take the situations into their own hands, and they have let it all go to waste because of it. They should have just gone to the Lord to begin with to resolve the problem and get solutions, and Hezekiah wouldn't have been in this mess right now. Hezekiah is tasked with cleaning up his family’s mess. That's a good reminder to us; Whatever we do today will be a mess or a blessing to the generation following us. May we not set them up for failure."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Isaiah\" means \"Jehovah has saved.” Isaiah lives that testimony too. He lives as if God has already saved His people. Yet when we look to the Lord's people during the time of Isaiah, they are in complete destruction and turmoil. It would seem as if God has completely forgotten about them, and there would be no chance for them to be saved. That's not how Isaiah will see it though."},{"kind":"para","text":"Isaiah is an interesting prophet. Elisha was interesting, but I think Isaiah has a lot more hope and promise to give to the land than even Elisha did. He will preach the gospel to the people. He will tell them about the coming Savior, while simultaneously telling them the truth about their current dilemma. He has a whole sixty-six-chapter book dedicated to his prophecies for a reason. I truly believe God set Isaiah up to be the central connection between the Old Testament prophets and the New Testament promise."},{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah, yet again, was wise to go to this man. He needed to know the truth from Isaiah, and he knew that Isaiah would tell him the flat-out truth no matter what. Isaiah had a deep connection with God."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"It may be…\" God has already heard it. Hezekiah should have known this. He should have understood that God was listening and God was moving. He wants to be sure though, through Isaiah. It makes you wonder why Hezekiah didn't just go directly to the Lord himself with these questions. Why is he relying on the prophets? We see David go straight to God over and over again when he faces a battle or a tough situation. Why doesn't Hezekiah do that too?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah also calls the Lord \"...thy God…\" He is assuming (it seems) that God is unreachable. Maybe he feels as if the Lord is not on his side at the moment, and that is why he words it like this? Maybe he feels like if he gets to the Lord, he will mess something up, so he wants a man of God to intercede for him. It seems he has not learned too much from his ancestors."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":7,"title":"Isaiah Assures","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's quite a bit to unpack here. Firstly, it doesn't seem as if Isaiah spends too much time praying to the Lord about it. It seems he might have already been ready with the answers before the men even showed up. God was pretty good about giving information before the fact to His prophets so they can just deal with the problem and move forward. This particular problem is going to be dealt with quite easily. Isaiah says that the Lord is going to deal with it because Assyria is not a kind people towards the Lord. They have \"blasphemed\" Him. They have lied and made the Lord of lesser value because of it. The Lord is going to send a \"blast upon him\" which simply means that a word is going to come into his ears. \"Blast\" means \"mind\" or “spirit.\" He's going to hear a rumor and return to Assyria to see if it's true. When he gets back, he will die. That's how quickly and easily the Lord will deal with him. Hezekiah won't have to worry about battle or fight; he can just let Rabshakeh die in his own land from his own blasphemy. You'd think this would astound the Assyrians, but it won't change much for them."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":13,"title":"Rabshakeh's Letter","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rabshakeh has his hands full now. He was waiting for word from Hezekiah, but now he's going to have to deal with this first."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So he's in the middle of this, and while he's assessing his enemies, he wants to send word to Hezekiah about his next plans of attack on them. He wants to continue to threaten Hezekiah while he deals with a different fight by Libnah."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If there is a person who starts their debate off with that assertion, you can rightfully assume from the get-go they are lying and deceived themselves. Right from the start, Rabshakeh has said that the people of Judah should not listen to the Lord or trust what the prophets of the Lord say about victory of any kind. He has been trying to get them to be as alone as possible and reject the leading of the Lord. Thankfully, Isaiah was a very trustworthy prophet, and could be listened to better than Rabshakeh (who isn’t a prophet, mind you)."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This letter just makes the victory they face all the more sweet. The people delivering the letter will think they have the upper hand, but Hezekiah will know that the Lord is going to deal with Rabshakeh and his lies. Rabshakeh seems to be running out of scare tactics, though. He already used this one. It's just a reminder for Hezekiah to think before he acts in war with Assyria."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":19,"title":"Hezekiah's Prayer","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah's prayer is something special. It's one to be examined. He is assured that he will win and won't have to worry about it, but he is still coming to the Lord for assurance. He still wants to know for sure that the Lord is really on his side. He starts off by giving honor and position to the Lord. God is above all, and He is central to their power and righteous leader."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God HAS been listening. Hezekiah doesn't need to worry about that. It's an amazing thing to think that God would see and hear our hurts and woes. It's a very surreal thing for us to understand that God is watching and moving. The fact that He would pay attention to an individual’s problems is quite the testament of our Lord, isn't it? You'd think He would be too high and out of reach to ever access our domain of influence. Though He may be high above and far out of sight, He is near to the brokenhearted."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD is nigh unto them that are of a broken heart; and saveth such as be of a contrite spirit.","ref":"Psalm 34:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a beautiful truth of God's sovereign presence and purpose for mankind. He knows us ALL individually and pays attention to EVERYONE'S (no matter how small) inclinations and heartache. He sees and He hears it all and He assesses based on the individual situation for an overarching purpose of salvation and glory. It's so simple and so complex all at the same time. There might not be many people who you can reach here (everyone (including in the church) is on a high horse), but the Lord is always reachable."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rabshakeh was telling the truth about the way in which they took over all of the other places. That was evident. It would seem to Judah that they are the only ones left not taken over and destroyed. Hezekiah doesn't want to be taken by Assyria. He wants his people to remain in the land and be free to worship the Lord alone. What a difficult time for Hezekiah to be king."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has already planned to save them in several ways. He has planned to save them from this Rabshakeh character far before any of this anyway. He will save them from their sins as well, but that proof will take place later on. This is a great prayer though. It's a prayer of reliance towards the Lord. It's a prayer that bleeds hope and longing. It's one that showcases the hurts and worries of man, while simultaneously relying on the Lord. That is real prayer."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":24,"title":"Isaiah's Letter","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God is always listening. Isaiah sends him a message right away. As quickly as Hezekiah prayed is likely how quickly the message has come to him. He probably had just finished praying, and suddenly there was a messenger with a letter at his door about the prayer. If the information from Isaiah didn't convince Hezekiah before, this letter certainly should."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God wants Hezekiah to send a letter back to Assyria with some important information from the Lord Himself. God laughs at their assumption and threats. He thinks they are silly tricks and failed attempts at pride and arrogance. It is one thing to hear a message from a king that you are up against, and a very different thing to receive a message from the God of that king. It's like hearing from the boss’s boss. There's no one higher than God, and He will assess this situation and completely humiliate Assyria because of it."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"By messengers or by his own mouth, it doesn't matter. The only person that has the final say is God alone. He is the final authority on every matter. They might have looked strong in their taking of land, but the Lord sees it and thinks, \"How little these people really are.” The only reason they were able to have the strength to take over any other land and cut down any other trees from those lands was simply because the Lord allowed it. He could have lit them all on fire if He really wanted to. It wouldn't have taken much for Him to just drop down giant hailstones on top of them."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"All Assyria was (to the Lord) is a rue to the children of Israel. They were there to prove to Israel what they were missing. He allowed Assyria strength for the ultimate purpose of bringing His children BACK into submission and faith. They had strayed so far away from it for so long. Now He's addressing Assyria with Judah. At this time, Hezekiah was doing everything perfectly right, and there was no reason for Assyria to try and take advantage of Judah for anything. They were too power-hungry, and they needed to be put in check. God has the greater power, is what He is saying here. The mountains, nor the trees, nor the waters or rivers will stop the Lord from His mode of battle either. He's above and beyond all that."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":28,"title":"God's Proof","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Without God, Assyria would not have any strength at all. Without the Lord (in whom they are blaspheming against, let's not forget), Assyria would be a small land of nothing. Actually, Assyria (without the Lord's Will on the matter) would be slaves to all the lands around them. It was God that put them into their position of power, and it will be God that takes that power away from them. It was God that gives them victory, and it will be God who gives them defeat. That is the ultimate rule of thumb in all of life's questions and purposes. In the end, God will ALWAYS have the final say."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Assyrians were purposely going against the Lord. They knew about the God of Israel, but instead of trusting in that God, they had turned against Him and blasphemed Him, and made Him as the lesser of all their other gods that they served. God's not going to just let that slip by unattended. He is going to deal with it. Hezekiah receives this letter about what God is going to do, and Hezekiah is to simply give them this message. It's a bold message to say the least. Hezekiah is reading it through with hopes and assurance, I would assume at this point. This was to give some boldness to the situation for Hezekiah as well as put Assyria in its place."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":34,"title":"God Has Control","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is towards Hezekiah now. The first part of the letter addressed Assyria, but Hezekiah was the focal point of the prayer, let's not forget. God says there are enough helpers for the people of Judah to keep them fed and well sustained. You don't grow crops and reap the harvest in the middle of war. Yet, they will have at least three years of promised peace in the land to live as protected people."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There will be a people of Judah who will stand to continue the land. God is not going to fully give this land over to Assyria. He is not going to let Assyria win this battle. Judah has a fighting chance from the Lord because of their faithfulness (and because of the Lord's promise to bring in the Savior). The Lord will make sure to keep His promises."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Assyria has no power here. That should be an assurance for Hezekiah. That should comfort him. Whether he believes the words of Isaiah or not, God will keep to this promise. I would assume that Hezekiah can find plenty of reason to trust the prophecies of Isaiah. Of course, a lot of things that Isaiah prophecies about won't become reality until quite some time later, but they were all very true and evidential prophecies from the Lord. I think Hezekiah (or anyone for that matter) can see that God was using this man Isaiah."}]}]},{"start":35,"end":37,"title":"Assyria Taken Care Of","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That equates to one hundred and eighty-five thousand soldiers. The Israelites didn't have to lift a finger for this. The Lord took care of these people Himself. No body even heard fighting happening. They just woke up the next morning and found a bunch of dead bodies everywhere. That will certainly wake up the king of Assyria."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That all ended quickly and quietly. It was taken care of, just as the Lord had said. This is quite the astounding bit of history we are reading here. It's impossible to even think. Anyone else that reads that would assume that this has to be wrong. The information has to be muddied. Of course, there's not much of a way to prove that this was right, but seeing as everything else in scripture can be proven, I'm sure there will be proof of even this someday. Even if there is not, we can rightly put our trust in scripture to being 100% accurate, and this is what happened. The death of Sennacherib would be the place to start. I'm sure there is some evidence about his death out there that we could easily find. Either way, the Lord takes care of the Assyrians for Judah just as He had promised through Isaiah, and Hezekiah can rest in peace and ease for the time being."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":37}]},
    "20": {"chapter":20,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see Hezekiah try and deal with the situation of the Assyrians threats of takeover. He goes to Isaiah to find out what God thinks about it, and then he goes to the Lords house to pray directly to God. God answers both prayers, and gives Hezekiah assurance about what is going to take place. Then God takes care of the Assyrians and kills a whole lot of them without lifting a single sword."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Hezekiah will become very sick, and it will lead him to his deathbed. He will pray for more life and healing, and the Lord is going to grant him that. However, it might have been best if the Hezekiah just rejected the healing and suffered the fate of his sickness instead."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Hezekiah's Sickness","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah was going to be missing a good king when Hezekiah dies. He was a man that brought prosperity back to that land for a time. They should learn their lesson at this point about what the Lord truly wants and how that will ultimately affect their future as well as their purpose in the land. They should learn lessons from the northern kingdom as well (which is gone at this point). Hezekiah has proven to everyone in the world that following the Lord is what brings lasting and ultimate prosperity."},{"kind":"para","text":"Isaiah is looking at this as a mix of good news and bad news. Bad news because the king is going to die from this sickness, but good news because the king gets to be with the Lord (which is what every child of God should want). I look forward to my death someday. Whenever the Lord decides to take me home is sufficient for me. I can't wait to be with him. Hezekiah, on the other hand… He won't like the idea of dying so soon:"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah is bringing up an important point. He wants more life. He wants the Lord to bless him with longer years. He doesn't want to die from being sick. He wants to die from being old. He wants to die a noble death. Perhaps from something more righteous. This situation is tough for us to read about. On the one hand, we can sympathize with Hezekiah because we should all desire longer life (especially if the Lord is making us prosperous). On the other hand, we also know that there is a good chance for us to mess everything up if we have more time..."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the Lord changed His mind on the matter. He is going to give Hezekiah fifteen more years of life. You might say, \"Can God just change His mind like that?” Well, yes and no. Yes, because He can do whatever He wants with whomever He wants as much as He wants. And no, because He already knew exactly what Hezekiah was going to ask for before he even asked for it. God waited for Hezekiah to get in this space of thinking on purpose. It would do Hezekiah well to remember this moment and think about this prayer. Fifteen extra years of life is a lot for someone to have."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was some sort of boil that was causing him this sickness. The lump of figs was an interesting method of resolving this problem. No one would assume that figs would fix this. I don't believe the figs actually do fix this sickness. I think the figs are a lot more of a representation of the people of Judah more than anything else. The figs were to represent a united people, over a very sick kingdom. That is the view that will be given to these people in the years to come... sadly… Receiving healing here may have been a grand mistake for Hezekiah, though he means well by it."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":11,"title":"The Shadows Proof","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God is going to prove His healing to Hezekiah. The health of Hezekiah should have been a sign enough, honestly, but he wanted a little more proof so he wouldn’t have to worry about it again. The sign was of a shadow. Does Hezekiah want the shadow of the Lord to go backwards or forwards?"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If the shadow goes forwards, obviously that could just be because the sun is moving forwards. But for the sun to move backwards is an impossible thing. So he wants the shadow to go backwards. That is a grand problem with all of us, isn't it? It would be far easier to just believe the shadow moving forward than it would to see it moving backwards. It would be a miracle to see it move backwards. Moving backwards, however, is the wrong direction for anyone to go in. This speaks a lot about Hezekiah's next fifteen years of life."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The shadow went backwards. Judah will be heading the same direction..."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"title":"Hezekiah and Babylon","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon was a confederate with Assyria, let's not forget. Babylon owned a portion of the northern kingdom. His goal, here, is not to try and make friends with Judah. His goal is to rub some salt on the wound of Hezekiah while he was sick. He's playing friendly, but we will learn that he isn't."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a strange thing for Hezekiah to do. He opened up all his worldly treasures to them. Instead of bringing them to the Lord, he brought them to his material possessions. He was more proud of the things he owned than he was of the Lord healing him. I see pastors fall for this same thing. Instead of telling other pastors about the spiritual growth of their church, they all want to talk about numbers. That is a terrible field to be in, and we will see that it brings Hezekiah into some trouble as well because of it."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"title":"Hezekiah's Flaw","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah doesn't seem to think there is anything wrong with sharing the ins and outs of his treasures and home. Now the people of Babylon know exactly where all the weak points are in Judah, and they can easily attack and take everything from them. Hezekiah wasn't thinking of that, though. For some reason, he forgot that there are a bunch of surrounding cities trying to take Judah over. He seems to forget that he has really no confederacy with any of the other nations around them. And now he has opened up his border walls for a takeover:"}]}]},{"start":16,"end":19,"title":"Captivity to Babylon","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon is going to own them now. They will be taken into captivity. It seems that Hezekiah would have made the better choice of just dying from his disease than having an extra fifteen years. Now he will live in captivity, and the people that are under him will join him. His sons will become “eunuchs.\" We have spoken about them before. Most were emasculated. That means his sons will probably not have any more children once they become owned by Babylon. I have no doubt they have children already, but that will be the last of them. A great view of Babylon is in the book of Daniel. You can see how the Israelites were treated through that book and what they had to deal with. Obviously, there will be a line that doesn’t become emasculated because the lineage of Christ comes through Hezekiah."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah doesn't see this as a bad thing. Babylon was living in prosperity! They had a lot going for them! Judah would be good in the hands of Babylon. He seems to forget about the hands of the Lord altogether, so Isaiah reminds him, \"Isn't it good for you to live in peace and truth?” Of course, it's better to live in peace and truth, but Hezekiah didn't see the captivity as robbing them of that. He saw it as God giving them some prosperity. That is not what was going to take place, though. They were going to become slaves to Babylon."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"title":"Hezekiah's Death","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah had a great legacy, and he brought the people of Judah into a very prominent place of power and worship. He messed up at this time, though. That extra fifteen years wasn't worth it. It just got them into trouble because Hezekiah trusted the enemy rather than the Lord for this matter. He should have let the Babylonians go back home instead of inviting them in. Good men are still flawed men. That is important to note. That is why we should not put our trust in man. We should only put our trust in the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"He has a son that will take his place. His son's name is Manasseh. \"Manasseh\" means \"Causing to forget.” That will be Manasseh's goal, by the way. His ultimate purpose and goal as king is to cause Judah to forget the Lord. That is what will bring Judah into captivity. It's this terrible king’s leadership. He has learned nothing from his father or the lineage that was before him. He will do things completely differently and against the Lord as much as possible. I think it was Hezekiah's last years that caused his son to take the throne with such disgrace and pride."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But Hezekiah rendered not again according to the benefit done unto him; for his heart was lifted up: therefore there was wrath upon him, and upon Judah and Jerusalem.","ref":"2 Chronicles 32:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Hezekiah fell away fast with his new life. He used the extra life he was given to squander it and turn away from God. That is not a promising thought. Manasseh will make it worse, but we will see that in the chapters to come."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21}]},
    "21": {"chapter":21,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Hezekiah was near death when he chooses to pray to the Lord for extended life. God actually grants the king fifteen more years of life, but it certainly won't be great for Hezekiah because he makes friends with Babylon (the people that will come in and capture them in future). In fact, I believe it's because of this friendship that most of the things we will see happen from this point forward comes to pass."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that the next couple kings are not great. They lead the nation further and further away from the Lord, and they bring back all the false gods, and terrible altars that Hezekiah spent so much time destroying and getting rid of. They are spiraling out of control quickly, and I think they are getting a lot of influence from the lands around them."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"Manasseh's Reign","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Manasseh was a child when he took the throne. He obviously didn't learn anything from his father. I assume his father didn't pour much time and energy into his son. David did the same thing with his own kids. I don't understand why they couldn't seem to find the value they needed in their children. Whatever the reason, they let this slide, and the next generation suffered for it. Manasseh is a twelve-year-old monster who will not do very well to be the leader of the kingdom. He doesn't have the proper mentors to lead him and guide him into a proper truth and direction that will bring honor and glory to the Lord. Because of this, he will do evil."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He goes back to the old idolatry ways of living. He had some bad influences around him. I think there were people in the kingdom who said they missed worshipping false gods, and he thought, “Well, why don't we put them back up then? Have some fun!\""}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He even brings back Baal worship. It's as if he did everything humanly possible (on purpose) as the opposite of what his father had done. He didn't like any part of his father's kingdom. He didn't like the idea that the Lord was the number one priority. He felt like it was too restrictive. He wanted to LIVE! That's how it seems with this world. They see the following of the Lord as a hassle and far too restrictive. They look at it like rules and laws, and that is what you are bound to if you follow God. They don't realize that Jesus completed the law for us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Think not that I am come to destroy the law, or the prophets: I am not come to destroy, but to fulfil. For verily I say unto you, Till heaven and earth pass, one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law, till all be fulfilled. Whosoever therefore shall break one of these least commandments, and shall teach men so, he shall be called the least in the kingdom of heaven: but whosoever shall do and teach them, the same shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven. For I say unto you, That except your righteousness shall exceed the righteousness of the scribes and Pharisees, ye shall in no case enter into the kingdom of heaven.","ref":"Matthew 5:17-20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Because of this, when we put our trust in Him, we are no longer under the law; we are under grace. It is grace that controls us, not rules."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under the law, but under grace.","ref":"Romans 6:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should understand that the law was nothing more than to show us our sin. The law was to bring us to the Lord and say, \"I can't do it.” Manasseh couldn't keep the law, and instead of going to the Lord in repentance, he just started setting up other gods that would be \"easier\" to serve. He wanted gods that would bow to his will and ways, and that is what these false gods will do. Therefore, he won't have to worry about trying to keep the law, or trying to plead with the Lord. He can just find forgiveness in whatever god he makes up. The world is good at doing that. They find something they don't like about God, and they drop Him for something else in a heartbeat. It makes you wonder how long their marriages will last too."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Nothing was out of reach. He put false altars in God's house. I think there are a lot of churches who have false altars today. They hide them though. They act as if the altars are safe and secure, and they are there to worship God specifically, but they are not. I know there are a lot of altars that simply worship the god of self. It's about your own personal feelings, and hurts that you take to these altars and you sacrifice your feelings there, and then walk to your seat \"feeling\" refreshed. It's about making a show for the rest of the people in the church to think that you are something spiritual. There are altars made for money too. You go there and place your money on the altar and sacrifice your cash as reverence to the church and to a different false god. The church collects the money and then disperses it between all the staff members and themselves, and they live a high life simply based on church income. That's a false altar."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many altars and faces a church will have, but the real altar of the Lord is one of sacrifice, repentance, and grace. Sacrificing your sin, repenting towards the Lord, and walking in His grace. Where are those altars in America's churches? Where are those altars in the world today? I don't see them, sadly. Perhaps you should keep those altars at home, because you aren't going to find much more in the church today than false altars of false worship."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Man, he dabbled into everything imaginable against the Lord. Some of these things weren't even spoken about in previous kings. How did he get these ideas? I assume there were some people coming in and living with them that thought this was normal. Maybe he read God's Word and said, \"Alright, whatever this word says, I'm going to do the exact opposite of it.” He may have done it thinking there must be a reason that it's not okay, and he wanted to see what that reason was."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The writer is giving a reminder to the reader here. This house was made (also) as a reminder to the people in Judah. It was there to remind them that the Lord would be with them if they were serious about following Him. He would protect and guide them if they were wanting it from the Lord. This moment, though, is what changes the course for that. By putting these graven images, and idols, and false altars, they are openly agreeing that they do not want the Lord's help anymore. They want the help of false gods. They want to put the power into their own hands. If that is what they want, that is what they will have."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord will give them their desire, and in so doing, He will stop protecting them. He, of course, has a promise to preserve Judah for the sake of David. David's lineage is secure, but He does not have to let them live a life going in the opposite direction of where they are supposed to be going. If God continues to allow this to happen with them, they will fail before the Savior even comes through this line. They will never allow the chance for the Savior if God protects their sin. So He is going to let them have what they want and their sin will swallow them whole."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everything that Manasseh was doing was worse than anything that the land was doing before. All those people and tribes and cities that Joshua helped destroy and take over in the book of Joshua were all involved with a pretty high level of idolatry. That is why the Lord had rejected them. They were not willing to give up their idols to follow the Lord of Israel. Now we have the mix of all of those cultures in Israel, and they are doing even worse than they were before. They are mixing the idol worship from all over the land, and they are adding to those modes of worship to make them as flagrant and obvious as possible. They are literally \"provoking\" God to become angry. They want to see how much it takes to make the Lord move against them, on purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is crazy for me to think that there are people who are literally like this. Most of them claim that they don't believe in God, yet they try and do everything in their power to provoke His anger. They try and get God angry enough to hurt them. And you know what? God usually gives them what they want. It might not happen right away, but you can bet any money that these same people will go through a very difficult season or trial that the Lord has prepared for them because they aren't listening. The Lord WILL deal with them in His time, and they will have no choice but to either cave under the pressure of the trial, or turn to the Lord. That is what will happen with Judah. The northern kingdom caved under the pressure. Judah will cave under it too."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":15,"title":"The Prophets Warning","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Notice it says \"prophet(s)\" here. That's plural. We have an account of many prophets in the Old Testament. Isaiah was a big one. But we have Jeremiah too, and minor prophets who are experiencing the downfall of the land. I have no doubt that they were hurt and worried about what was happening in their land. They also had assurance of promises and hopes. What would they be experiencing while the world is falling apart around them? Will they be mistreated in captivity too? May we not forget, the Lord knows how to take care of His own children."},{"kind":"para","text":"He will take care of the prophets; however, the people will not think very highly of them, and the people (their own people) will mistreat them. Some would even go as far as to kill the prophets because of their words. To be a prophet at this time of Israel would be a very difficult and terrible thing. To be a Christian in the darkest era of the world is also not a fun idea. Yet you can't just get rid of Christianity when you are one. Once a Christian, you are ALWAYS a Christian. Once a prophet, you are always a prophet. But again, we know that God will not let this world do something without a warning. He has given warning after warning to the northern kingdom, and now he will give warning to the southern kingdom:"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just \"hearing\" about the evil that God is going to allow will make someone's ears tingle. We can just read it today and know that it was terrible. It should leave us with a pit in our stomach reading about all the evil that the kings have been doing. It should not make us feel victorious when we read about the captivities that are taking place either."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Israelites had the literal God of the universe at their side, and they had done nothing but reject Him, turn from Him, abuse Him, and use Him until there was nothing left. Then they would go to other gods and act like those fake gods they had just made up were far better than the God of all creation. These are people that knew about the Lord, mind you. They had seen the Lord do amazing things, and they still had reason to turn away from Him and worship other gods. They could watch the Lord provide a giant pillar of smoke and fire, and then split a whole SEA so they could walk across it on dry ground, and then drown an entire army that was pursuing after them, and then they turned around and worshiped a golden calf they had just made… Does that confuse you as much as it confuses me? They could see food literally fall from the sky, and they found reason to forsake the Lord. Water from a rock, and they still didn’t want to trust God. They were freely given land to take for themselves, and they STILL didn’t want to trust God. I just don't get it."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great reminder as well. It is not up to the leader to get God's grace. It's up to the individual. Over and over again, God had warned the NATION, not just the king. We might have missed that as we read. But if you go back and look at all the times the prophets spoke, it was always to address the PEOPLE of the king, and not just the king himself. The reason the king was addressed (at all) was because he was responsible for leading the people. But the people could easily just follow the Lord over the king, and the Lord would be pleased with the people and rebuke the king. However, the people hardly ever did that. They always just liked to follow the leadership of the king, and that is what usually got them into trouble. That is what gets us into trouble as well. We should not just blindly follow a leader. We should follow the Lord, and if that leader is not following Him, we should probably get out from under his leadership."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":18,"title":"Manasseh's Death","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These idols were not just about saying prayers and giving reverence to fake gods. No. They used them to burn children and sacrifice humans. They were likely eating humans as well. They killed innocent people who could not defend themselves in the name of these fake gods. That is the depths of idol worship. God gives limits to worship for Him. He says that we must have joy in doing it, but we must do it in holiness and as a people who are set apart. Self-worship doesn't care about what is proper. Nothing is off-limits when you are able to worship yourself."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Manasseh rested with his false worship. It was a terrible time for Judah, and now we have a new man coming in to reign in his stead. With everything that Manasseh did, you would guess that Amon would not be any better. And you know what? You would be right. Amon is a terrible king."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":26,"title":"Amon's Reign & Death","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Amon\" means \"master workman.” Maybe he was good with his hands. The Lord can use a \"master workman.” However, these hands won't be used to do any sort of work for the Lord."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Nothing changed with his leadership. He was leading them further into the trap of idolatry, and he was making it harder to live in the land of Judah. In the northern kingdom, this stuff took time. It took time to get the Lord completely out of the land. But these two sons (thus far) have been making good progress at removing God entirely from the picture."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, there were a few that didn't like this idea of false worship either. So, they killed the king. This is a serious crime. Whether the king is good or bad doesn't matter. The Lord does not condone the murder of the man on the throne. That has been clear for a long time. Not even David would stoop that low, and he had two opportunities to kill the king."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people of Judah didn't like the fact that there were conspirators in the kingdom, so they killed those that killed Amon. I assume that most of the land actually liked the way in which Amon led. They liked the idea that they could worship whatever god they so desired. They liked having the freedom to completely reject the Lord and live in their own appetites and sins. They liked killing innocent people, and they liked sacrificing their own families to try and get what they wanted. What a strange lot."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Amon was buried near his father. They were buried in an idol’s grave. That is the legacy they left, and that is how they will always be remembered. They are idol worshipers through and through. Judah is spiraling out of control rather quickly. We have had a lot of great kings on the throne in Judah, and now it seems as if they can't catch a break. They are falling away from the Lord quickly, and headed towards total destruction. Even if they have a good king next, there is so much that needs to be cleaned up and fixed that it would be too much for him to deal with. We will see what becomes of Judah in the next chapter."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":26}]},
    "22": {"chapter":22,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 22"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw the lineages of two kings of Judah, and it did not go well for them. They were evil towards the Lord and towards their own people, and they set Judah up for a major fall. Because of this they are quickly rejecting the Lord and going against everything the Lord has commanded."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Josiah come onto the throne, and he will do what is right before the Lord. There is no way for him to turn the kingdom around, however, and so God will still have to deal with the people of Judah, and Josiah has to figure out what to do with his rulership in the land."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Josiah's Reign","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have (yet again) another child coming onto the throne. \"Josiah\" means \"Whom Jehovah heals.\" That sounds like a promising name. We have been fooled by the northern kingdom though multiple times. However, it seems that Josiah has learned something that his father did not learn. How he learned it? I don't really know."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting that he didn't fall for the same traps as his father and grandfather. He has learned from their mistakes. This will not be an easy time of reign for him, though. This whole city has been given over to all sorts of terrible idolatry, and they are completely dedicated to it. There is a lot for this young king to fix up."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":7,"title":"Fixing the Temple","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If this sounds familiar, it's because we have actually seen this happen before. Joash originally tried to fix up the temple, and the priests were gathering the money, but then pocketing it and not putting it to work. Just like our churches today!"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The difference this time was that Josiah had faithful members dealing with the money. He didn't have to worry about them pocketing it. These people wanted to build the Lord's house back up and get it back into proper shape. I assume it was used as storage for all the false gods and things they were worshipping across the land. The Lord's house wasn't being used for what it was supposed to be used for. Just like our churches today!"},{"kind":"para","text":"I have made direct connections (multiple times) to the churches today, and you might read this and say \"Boy, Jonathan sure doesn't like the modern church, my church is great, I'm sure there are other churches in this world that are just as good as mine!\" Perhaps you are right. But even in your church, I guarantee there are politics taking place that are anti-biblical. People always fall for their own methods and traditions, and it seeps into the church leadership, and causes all sorts of questions and dramas. Even with something as simple as when the church takes breaks (and yes, I've had to deal with that problem on several occasions now). Actually I've had to deal with that problem on both ends. I've been accused by one church of taking too many breaks. They said I was hardly ever at the church (I didn't even have a day off, so I don't see how they could assume that, plus I have hundreds of pictures of me emptying out trashcans to prove that I am there throughout the week, plus pictures of events with the teens, and events with the church body, and singing, and leading worship, and preaching to the retirement home, and going soulwinning (three times a week on most occasions) yada, yada, yada). Then I have had the great privilege of being accused (by my next church) of not taking ENOUGH breaks. There is no winning. You know what the problem is between both of these accusations? Church politics. It's church tradition that they are complaining about not being met. They want to hold onto their own methods of worship, when that is not what the Lord is looking for. Honestly, I could argue that God finds the breaks we take (or don't take) as a very little thing in comparison to the rest of ministry. Yet the church makes it such a big deal."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our churches today are in disarray. They are storage units for our stuff. They serve no other purpose than to get us to the next big event on our church calendar, and pretend like we are worshipping God in one body on Sundays. That is not the means that God intended for the church. That is how the church falls apart and turns into what we are seeing in the books of Kings. Josiah wants to put God's house back into working order so it's not just used for storage for false god worship. May we have the tenacity and desire to do the same for our churches."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":13,"title":"The Law of God","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a HUGE deal. God's Word was buried under all the rubble. It was there the whole time, and they have no idea what they have been missing out on. His Word WILL change lives."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He realizes how far away from God's Word they have become. It is truly a wonder why he has any interest in following the Lord before this book was read. I assume he just wanted to clear out this temple and see what was in it, but this will certainly get his faith deeper than it ever could have been before. Every word that was being read to him from the scribe was like a knife to the heart. I'm sure he thought about what his father had done, and I'm sure he thought about how the kingdom was worshipping and starts to realize how much work needs to happen to go back to the Lord. They will not live in prosperity and security from the Lord if they are not willing to clean up the land."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a serious matter. He needs the Lord on their side. He knows that (in their current standing) the Lord cannot even touch them. They are unclean and completely unattached. So he sends the priests to go before the Lord and see what they can do to fix their current standing with God. And of course, this brings a flutter to the Lord's heart."},{"kind":"para","text":"God loves His children. He loves it when His creation turns to Him in repentance and pursues Him with their hearts and desires. That is why He is so forgiving so quickly. He has been sitting there watching their continual downfall, and just hoping that something would catch their eyes. He has given warning after warning, and has tried every which way to let them know that they are doing evil, but they just didn't want to listen. Now Josiah is turning everything around to face the Lord, and I know for a certain that this is pleasing to God. What a great testimony from a king."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":20,"title":"The Wrath and Mercy of God","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They seem to know of a prophetess who will give them a clear-cut response from the Lord. She will be honest (no matter how brutal it will seem)."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people were very far into their idolatry. They would not turn from it, and because of this, God would have to deal with them. That sounds like a very terrible message if it just ended there. Josiah wanted to turn to the Lord, and he wanted the land to prosper. He didn’t want to face the wrath of God. With a message like that, it would seem that all hope is lost. But there is hope for a repentant spirit:"}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Josiah will be protected from God's wrath. So will you if you have the Lord today. God does not pour His wrath down on His own children. It's those that have turned from Him and are not serving Him that will suffer from His wrath. The whole world can be falling apart around you because the Lord has led it to be this way, but YOU will be protected. The Lord will not let His own children (that is those that have trusted in Him and are saved) be a part of his outpouring of anger. He is not angry with His children. He loves His children."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is angry with the creation that does not love Him in return. He is angry with those that have purposely gone against Him. He's angry with those that seemingly hate Him. Josiah is a picture of how God loves us in spite of the whole world being against Him. He loves those that seek Him and are willing to be “tenderhearted.\" That is how we should prepare our hearts. They should be tender."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":20}]},
    "23": {"chapter":23,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we got the beginning reign of Josiah. He came from a lineage of terribly evil kings, and his fathers set up some difficult situations that Josiah would have to face while on the throne. Josiah finds the law of God in the temple of God, and when he has it read, it goes on a cleaning spree of the land."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Josiah set the land up for success by taking down all the problems that his fathers had put up and didn't take care of. They were abusing the Lord's temple, and they were defiling it by placing their own modes of worship in there instead of worshipping the Lord. Because of this, the wrath of God has been stored up for quite some time, but He is waiting to pour that out when the time is right."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"A Judah Revival","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king owes a lot of gratitude towards the Lord in this instance. God has promised to take care of him because of his desire to please the Lord and turn towards Him. So he's gathering all the elders in Judah to set up a proclamation to the people."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"All these people were without excuse now. There was no reason for them to turn away from what they had just heard. The king heard it and fell apart. The people will hear it, but it will seem as if it doesn't affect them quite as much. I believe it's because they loved their sin. They loved their false worship. If they were going to follow the Lord, they would have to give up some of the things they thought were worth their life. But Josiah did the right thing here and gave the Word of God to all these people so they knew what the consequences were for following after false gods. They have the choice now. Josiah will be on the side of the Lord, and the people under him can also follow suit if they so choose."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great example for all of us. When you hear the truth, you cannot help but tell everyone else about it. This is serious business, and the king of Judah believed it wholeheartedly. He wanted everyone else to know what was going to happen. He wanted the whole city to understand that they were progressing God's wrath by living the way they were. They had the chance to turn from their evil ways and look to the Lord now. When we proclaim the gospel, that is the choice we are giving to everyone else in this world as well. It is, sadly, still up to the individual people to make their own choices on the matter. But your job is to simply give them the gospel truth and let them accept or reject it."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king holds power, of course. So when the king proclaims something, the people will desire to listen. Notice it says that they \"stood to the covenant.” In other words, they are agreeing with the promises the king is making towards the Lord. The promise was that THEY would \"keep\" to His commandments, \"keep to His testimonies and statutes\" and they would do it with \"all their heart and all their soul\" and will \"perform the words of this covenant that were written in this book.” That's quite the promise to make. The king means it. I think the people meant it too after hearing what was going to take place. And I think it sparks a time of revival in the land in all honesty (as we will see in a moment). But that revival won't last forever. Actually, it won't last very long at all."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":14,"title":"Idol Hunting","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Josiah was cleaning up the temple once again, and he was doing it himself this time. There were things in the temple that should have never been there to begin with. He wanted to personally make sure that he wasn't going to have to ever deal with it again while he was on the throne. He takes the ashes and carries them completely out of town so that people can't even worship the ashes. He doesn't trust that people will not easily turn back to the Lord."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He then \"put down\" the idolatrous priests. They were sacrificing for the people. They treated these priests as a sort of mediator. They would do the work for the people, and the people would just watch as if it was a show at a theater. This way the people (personally) would not have to get their hands dirty. They could just say what they wanted done, and the priests would simply do it for them (at a price of course). It sounds a lot easier than it actually was."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is all sorts of messed up if you think about it. Instead of killing your own son, with your own hands, to sacrifice to a false god of your own choosing, they hand them off to a man wearing a costume to do the job for you, and you just stand and watch."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He's putting the ashes of these groves into the graves of those that made them. This was in equal parts a bit of a stab towards those that sacrificed their false gods, and it was a bit of a respect shown to those that were sacrificed BECAUSE of these false gods. These were children's graves that the ashes were being poured onto. That itself is a sad thought."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They even brought back the acts of sodomy. Those acts were sexual by nature. They were people who would rape and abuse others with acts that were completely against the design of God. Specifically, homosexuality. They had allowed that to be acceptable in the Lord's house for a time because they had completely rejected the Lord. To put it in God's house was an interesting decision. It's still an interesting decision to make today, and it happens all the time."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church has become more and more tolerant of the acts of sin in the church house today, and there are a lot of people who are continually living in sin who are in charge of so many aspects of the church. That usually wouldn't be too much of a bad thing if they were struggling with the sin. But they don't struggle, and what they do is entice others to dabble in the same sins until the whole church is stuck in a false mode of worship. That is why you cannot be unresponsive to sin. You must deal with it when it comes up."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are a pastor or leader in the church, your responsibility is to help those struggling OUT of the sin, not encourage them to stay in it. If you are struggling with sin, your responsibility is to surround yourself with people who can DAILY encourage you to overcome, not people who will tell you how normal your sin is."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"We then that are strong ought to bear the infirmities of the weak, and not to please ourselves.","ref":"Romans 15:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"God has given every person a level of strength in their spiritual walks, and it would be wise of us to use those strengths to strengthen others with their sins and hurts as well."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is destroying and taking down all the places of false worship, and he's doing it right in front of the priests that were working those areas. They enjoyed their work in false worship. They were (no doubt) being paid handsomely for it. They were doing all sorts of grotesque things and then getting money, food, shelter, and necessities from the people on top of that for their services. So instead of Josiah killing these men (which I think he should have done honestly), he takes them outside of their places of worship, and then starts defiling those places and destroying them right before their eyes. He wanted them to watch what becomes of their gross places of worship."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even after watching their places of business be destroyed, they would still not come to worship the Lord. They wanted their old gods back. They wanted their human sacrifices back. You say, \"How do you know that? How do you know they ENJOYED sacrificing humans?” Well, the next verse tells us:"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was not just human sacrifice. It was CHILD sacrifice. How perverted of a mind you must have to ever accept the killing of a child, no matter what the situation calls for. I've heard a lot (lately) about abortion being deemed necessary if the person was a rape victim. Firstly, if it was okay for this to happen, the statistics of abortion would drop WAY down, because it is hardly ever the case that someone is pregnant due to rape. It's not impossible, but it's not as probable. I'd say most (if not all) the people who get an abortion today are people who simply didn't protect themselves. That means that most of the people getting abortions are just killing children simply because they can and it's available. But we will say that the majority is due to rape for this particular situation. That is still a child in the womb."},{"kind":"para","text":"You kill a child because you don't want it. It's as simple as that. They don't want a man's child who they did not approve of. So they become a victim of rape, which is an awful thing for someone to experience, and then they turn around and become a perpetrator of murder by killing a child. They cause TWO victims in this story now. At the beginning, the person who was raped was the victim. The child in the womb is not the problem. The child in the womb (if I can be so bold) is a gift. The child is an arrow for the mother. The child can be brought up to become a person that can STOP rape from happening any further. The mother doesn't see it like this though, and so the best case (as governed by the state) is to kill the child and never give it a chance."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this situation in Judah, these were children that the parents got to see and hold and touch and feel. They were children that they may have even fed and raised for a short time. Then they were placed on an altar by a priest and lit on fire for a god that won't even meet their needs. The priests didn't care, though. They would get paid either way, and so they did what they had to do, and over time they became calloused to it. They found ways to enjoy the occasion. They made it fun for the whole family to be a part of. I can't fathom how done away a nation would have to become to be okay with this, but I don't imagine that our nation is too far behind, in all honesty. I know some states allow abortion up to birth. They were even talking about it for thirty days AFTER birth in OUR nation. That's extremely miserable to think about. And they don't even do it as a sacrifice to a god. They do it as a sacrifice for themselves. They just don't like being mothers, or the baby cries too much, or they become too scared to take care of the child, so I guess killing them is the best option. That sounds insane for me to even type out here..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ironically, they died and were destroyed in the same fashion as what they represented and were given too. They were given to the sun. It fascinates me that these people could worship planets. I think we still have that problem today, but I'm not going to get into that right here."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The brook Kidron keeps coming up. It seems to be the place that all idols go to die! It's constantly been a symbol of that throughout our time in Scripture. \"Kidron\" means “dark.\" One could argue that the darkest thing a people could ever be a part of is idolatry. Yet it's in this \"dark\" place that idols do not come back from."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It could be argued that this all stemmed from the lack of care that Solomon shows in his leadership. He should have taken care of these places when he was king. That was what his wisdom would have told him, but he seems to reject that part of his wisdom, and now (generations later) they are paying the price. That's a stark warning for all of us reading today."},{"kind":"para","text":"The sins you are dealing with (or aren't dealing with rather) are going to be a problem at some point. They will either be a problem for you later on unless you deal with it, or it might be brought down from generation to generation until much later in the future when your great-grandchildren are stuck with the problem and they are suffering from something YOU should have dealt with when you knew it was good for you. Thankfully, it's being dealt with now, but it can't just stop at this generation..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These false places of worship were now displaying what they really were. This whole time they were places of death. They were gravesites from the get-go. They were not going to be places that provided life to the people. They could only be places that provided death, and now they will be places of death and men's bones. If these false priests wanted to build these places up again, they would have to dig up all the bones and get rid of them. That would be a lot of work on their part. Either that or they would have to defile their worship over the dead bones. That, to them, was bad luck. It was a bad omen. It's still considered a bad omen to defile a grave today."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"title":"More Altars","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that it is never-ending. There was so much set up in such a short amount of time, and he kept turning every which way and destroying everything he was seeing. He is trying to make it impossible for the next generation to come in and reinstate these false places of worship. One right after the other. Thankfully, there were graves near this place that could be used to pollute these altars, but in the other instances, I have no doubt he had to do a bit of traveling to get bones and graves. It was not an easy task, but for Josiah, it was an important task, so he will use every ounce of energy possible to make sure that God's Word is supreme here."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":20,"title":"Preserving Godly Graves","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These graves were sacred because they were people of God. They were people that worshipped the Lord properly and didn't fall for the traps of idol worship like everyone else. They were people who went against the grain of culture, and were able to stand strong and firm in their faith even though they could face death, punishment, or become exiled. They deserved (even in their graves) to be honored."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He had taken care of everything he possibly could. I don't imagine he did all this in one day. I think this took him some time. It probably took him a week, maybe even a few weeks. Possibly even a few months to take care of all this. This really is a lot of work being summed up in just a few verses of scripture. It might seem as if it was quick and painless, but this certainly wasn’t. Just the traveling from place to place alone would have taken a few days, let alone the burning and digging up graves and tearing down altars and killing false priests."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":23,"title":"Observing the Passover","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Woah! This has been missing for some time. Honestly, I can't seem to remember the last time they had even thought about this or mentioned it. The sad reality is, even the best kings didn't observe the Passover. They didn't really even think about it. They probably didn't read much of the Word of God at all. It seems it had been lost in the temple for some time. I assume the priests knew about it, but they just used it as a display piece. That is what most American families do today in their homes. They put their Bibles on the mantle and display them like a piece of art. They never break the binding. If they would pick it up sometimes, they would learn how undone their family is and would know how to lead and control their homes. Sadly, that is lost knowledge in most of the world today."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was a Passover for the ages. It was better than anything they had ever held before. The Passover was a celebration, and the people (I believe based on this verse) really enjoyed it! They didn't know how wholesome and good the Passover could be. They probably knew it was a law, but they didn't know that it was good."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the problem with religion, by the way. They keep God's commands (sometimes) and they try to live by the Word, but they don't do it as something good. They just do it as something needful. The Passover may have been performed during the times of the kings, but it was never done in joy and faith. It was done in a religious tradition. It was done because they HAD to do it, not because they actually WANTED to do it. Josiah brought back the heat and desire that the people needed to observe the Passover, and it became a serious occasion for everyone."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice he observes the Passover AFTER he has cleansed the land. The land was no good to observe the Passover before, and it would have lost its meaning if he did nothing to fix up the place before this solemn assembly. He had to get rid of the idols before he could rightfully follow the Lord's commands. Same goes for our hearts."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":25,"title":"Josiah Fixes The Land","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We hear a lot about Solomon. Rightfully so. We hear a lot about David. Rightfully so. We probably hear a lot about Saul as well. Rightfully so. But hardly does anyone talk about this king, Josiah. He had (seemingly) more spirit than Solomon did. Solomon didn't even turn to the Lord like this. David didn't either. Josiah was the only king to do this and be successful. He did a lot to please the Lord. The Lord certainly would be pleased. I think that's important to consider before going into these next verses. But things were still too far gone. The Lord HAS to exact punishment for the sins of the people..."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":27,"title":"God's Anger","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God's anger was still with Judah. Why? Was He not a forgiving God? The churches say that God forgives, so what's the big idea here? God is a forgiver, I want that to be clear. He has forgiven Josiah. And for now He has forgiven the land. But the land WILL have to be dealt with. He does not forget the unrepentant sinners’ unrighteous acts. He will serve justice for what Manasseh and his lineage has done. That was already a guaranteed promise. Even if the next king was absolutely righteous, God still has to keep His promise."},{"kind":"para","text":"The fact of the matter is, God knows that Judah will go back to her ways, and this whole cycle will repeat itself again. Next time, though, they will have to undo everything that Josiah just did. They will have to work very hard to go back to idol worship. The people will have to determine if worshipping false gods is worth the effort and energy to go against the truth of God's Word, and the work that Josiah had put in to make it difficult for them to pop these altars up again."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":30,"title":"Josiah's Death","entries":[{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God allowed Josiah to prosper immensely. Josiah was a man greatly used by God because he was a man greatly influenced by God. If you want to be a man that is greatly used by God, then you must be a man greatly influenced by God. Sadly, Josiah made the wrong choice to go to battle this day. He should have stayed home. If he did, he wouldn't have died from this fight. God promised him that he would die in peace. Perhaps he still did. Maybe it was a quick death for Josiah. Whatever it was, this was the legacy of Josiah's death."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Josiah had an honorable death. The people will surely remember Josiah, right? They are going to place his son \"Jehoahaz\" to reign over the land now. That was how it had to work in the kingdom. That was the order in which the Lord blessed David. He will carry this out until Christ."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":35,"title":"Jehoahaz's Reign","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, Josiah left quite the legacy behind. You'd think that Jehoahaz was watching and paying attention to what his father did, so that he could repeat those same patterns. This would mean that Jehoahaz would be quite the good king! And the Lord would be pleased with him too, right?"}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"And, here, you face palm because you wonder what went wrong. I think Jehoahaz was just a spoiled brat; that's what I think went wrong. He had everything handed to him on a silver platter, and now he's abusing the good of the land and trying to bring back everything his father had worked so hard to take down..."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Egypt was quick to take Jehoahaz off the throne and take him to Egypt. He would become a slave to them."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh made Eliakim king in Judah. I guess he liked this guy more than Jehoahaz. \"Eliakim\" means \"God sets up.” That seems strange, doesn't it? Egypt shouldn't have this kind of authority. God certainly allows this to happen. It's not lost on Him. David's lineage was still being kept on the throne. Pharaoh changes the name of Eliakim to Jehoiakim. \"Jehoiakim\" means \"Jehovah raises up.” Pharaoh was being used by God more than the kings of Judah were, it seems. We will see that it was pretty common for Egypt and Babylon to do this name-changing tradition in the book of Daniel. Jehoahaz simply dies in Egypt."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pharaoh wanted Jehoiakim as king because he would be far more obedient to him. Jehoahaz would have just been a problem and difficult to manage. I assume that Pharaoh was making ties with the people of Judah since Josiah went out to battle and died. Because he died from the Pharaoh, it made it easy to strong-arm the people of Judah and threaten them into submission."}]}]},{"start":36,"end":37,"title":"Jehoiakim Reigns","entries":[{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, he did. There is nothing good about this land now that Josiah has died. Peace lasted for a season, and that is why God's anger did not turn away from Judah. Judah will continue to spiral away from the Lord, and the Lord WILL have to lead them into captivity. It will happen because the Lord knows exactly the direction they are going to go in before they get there. It doesn't matter how many times the Lord can provide warnings to them, and it doesn't matter how many examples He could place on the throne, the people will continually turn away from the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As it is written, There is none righteous, no, not one: There is none that understandeth, there is none that seeketh after God.","ref":"Romans 3:10-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Because of this, God's wrath will be executed, and He will have no other choice but to give them what they deserve. Honestly, He is just giving them what they want. They want a land without God, so that is what He is going to give them. They are going to live in a land without God, and they will suffer the consequences of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe our country has slowly turned into that as well. We are living in a land that has been trying to get rid of God for a long time now. We don't want God in our pledge to our nation. We don't want God in our promises in the courtroom. We don't want God on our money, or in our oaths. We only want our own strengths, and our false worship. That will certainly provoke the anger of the Lord, and we will see that be poured out in our nation as we continuously provoke Him. We are already seeing the consequences of it today. I think both sides would agree that we are a nation that is falling apart because of our lack of morality. Morality, ultimately, comes by the standards set before the Lord. He gave us a conscience, and our conscience agrees with HIS moral law. Nobody else has set that up for us."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":37}]},
    "24": {"chapter":24,"intro":[],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Nebuchadnezzar should be a familiar name. \"Nebuchadnezzar\" means \"may Nebo protect the crown.” Who is Nebo? Well, I don't know. Probably a false god. Just his name alone screams idolatry. But he makes Jehoiakim his subject for quite some time until Jehoiakim thinks it's sufficient to try and rebel against Babylon."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord is allowing a captivity to the people of Judah. Not even God was on the side of Judah at this point. He is going to give prosperity to Babylon instead. God DOES promise to bring Judah back, but they must spend some time away from their land and held as prisoners in a foreign land, just as God had promised would happen."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord is judging the land. It was deserved. The writer cannot think of any other reason that this was allowed by the Lord, other than what Manasseh had done. He was a terrible king and a terrible human. He was disgusting and purposely going against the Lord. He WANTED to make God angry. Well, he did it successfully, and the Lord is now making Judah pay the price. They were all accepting of the king’s evil and did the same things as him. They played into the evil of the king’s hand. Jehoiakim was no better either, so he was the perfect king to lead them into this captivity."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jehoiachin\" means \"Jehovah establishes.” Such a promising name. Jehoiachin is taking a failing kingdom over. Babylon is gaining serious control. Even over Egypt:"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Do you think this new king is going to do what's right and turn to the Lord? Of course not. We will read that in a moment. Jehoiachin was set up for failure right from the start. There was no way around it at this point other than to turn to the Lord. But they are too prideful and too arrogant to do that..."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":19,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The apple doesn't fall far from the tree. I don't know how Josiah slipped through the cracks. Perhaps the Lord allowed this man to be a righteous king just to prove (once again) that the people of Judah have a chance to prosper. They don't learn anything from Josiah, though, and so they keep heading downhill towards ownership by Babylon. That has already begun from Jehoiakim."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon won against Judah. No one could stand up against him. It was inevitable. It was a promise from the Lord, because the Lord knew that the people would continually turn away from Him. He (once again) is giving them what they wanted. They will now live in ownership by a different country. They want to be subjects to false gods; what better place to do that than Babylon?"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoiachin had his mother with him in this captivity. I assume this was a very terrible thing for him to witness. Not a single thought towards the Lord, though. They act as if their God doesn't even exist. That is why the Lord gives them over. They have forgotten everything about what the Lord has done and the victories He has given to them. They will have no other choice but to remember it in their captivity."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon doesn't even need the wealth. They were already a wealthy nation. They were powerful. They may have been the most powerful force on Earth at this point in time. There was nothing anyone could do to stop them now. All of Israel, at this point in scripture, has fallen. There's nothing left of them except remnants scattered in different lands. Yet, we know, the Lord has promised to bring them back into the land, and the lineage of David will continue forward."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The poor in the land stayed in the land. Babylon didn't have a need for them. It's quite possible that the Lord can grab the attention of the poor. I think the poor are the most sensitive to God's leading even still today. They don't have much, but they have a spirit of wealth with the Lord. God can make you rich on a deeper level than just on the surface. We want to be financially stable, but the Lord is looking to make you spiritually stable. That is what the poor can receive. Babylon wanted the skin-deep stability, and that is what they will have. However, it won't last forever, and eventually Babylon will become a part of history’s past."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon has taken everything now, but it seems they want to leave some sort of control in the land, so they make Mattaniah (Jehoiachin's uncle) the king of Judah for the time being. The king of Babylon will have an even easier time controlling Zedekiah than he did with Jehoiachin (or at least he assumes). I believe Jehoiachin put up a fight to keep Judah and his possessions, and that is why Babylon has taken them captive, rather than just threatening obedience. They were done asking questions and trying to forcefully take resources from Judah. They just wanted it all right then and there, and now they will be swallowed up by Babylon."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He learned NOTHING from the exile. You'd think that this captivity would have made Zedekiah rethink the land's strategy, but these kings have just simply given up all hope. They have now become submissive, and that will not bode well with the Lord."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":17,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon won against Judah. No one could stand up against him. It was inevitable. It was a promise from the Lord, because the Lord knew that the people would continually turn away from Him. He (once again) is giving them what they wanted. They will now live in ownership by a different country. They want to be subjects to false gods; what better place to do that than Babylon?"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jehoiachin had his mother with him in this captivity. I assume this was a very terrible thing for him to witness. Not a single thought towards the Lord, though. They act as if their God doesn't even exist. That is why the Lord gives them over. They have forgotten everything about what the Lord has done and the victories He has given to them. They will have no other choice but to remember it in their captivity."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon doesn't even need the wealth. They were already a wealthy nation. They were powerful. They may have been the most powerful force on Earth at this point in time. There was nothing anyone could do to stop them now. All of Israel, at this point in scripture, has fallen. There's nothing left of them except remnants scattered in different lands. Yet, we know, the Lord has promised to bring them back into the land, and the lineage of David will continue forward."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The poor in the land stayed in the land. Babylon didn't have a need for them. It's quite possible that the Lord can grab the attention of the poor. I think the poor are the most sensitive to God's leading even still today. They don't have much, but they have a spirit of wealth with the Lord. God can make you rich on a deeper level than just on the surface. We want to be financially stable, but the Lord is looking to make you spiritually stable. That is what the poor can receive. Babylon wanted the skin-deep stability, and that is what they will have. However, it won't last forever, and eventually Babylon will become a part of history’s past."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon has taken everything now, but it seems they want to leave some sort of control in the land, so they make Mattaniah (Jehoiachin's uncle) the king of Judah for the time being. The king of Babylon will have an even easier time controlling Zedekiah than he did with Jehoiachin (or at least he assumes). I believe Jehoiachin put up a fight to keep Judah and his possessions, and that is why Babylon has taken them captive, rather than just threatening obedience. They were done asking questions and trying to forcefully take resources from Judah. They just wanted it all right then and there, and now they will be swallowed up by Babylon."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":20,"title":null,"entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He learned NOTHING from the exile. You'd think that this captivity would have made Zedekiah rethink the land's strategy, but these kings have just simply given up all hope. They have now become submissive, and that will not bode well with the Lord."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Zedekiah was still not even accepting Babylon. I'm sure the king of Babylon just laughed at his defiance. How weak and little Zedekiah must have looked to him. He looked like a tiny ant trying to scream for sovereignty. It wouldn't take much to squish him. This is a very sad chapter. Honestly, most of the chapters of 1&2 Kings have been terribly sad. We have one more chapter to go, and we can't see how anything can get much better at this point…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":19},{"start":10,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":20}]},
    "25": {"chapter":25,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we saw Babylon come in and completely take over Judah. They had left a small remenant there to toy with, but Judah will not be a problem for Babylon in the future (or rather as we study this chapter). The kings of Judah just keep digging themselves deeper and deeper away from the Lord, and the Lord has finally had enough of it, and has given them over to their enemies."},{"kind":"para","text":"Babylon is a powerful nation at this time. There was nothing that Judah could really do to protect themselves against them. Not to mention the Lord is not on their side anymore at the moment. The land will become desolate, and the only people that would be sufficient to continue to live there are the poor people of the land. In this chapter, we will see what becomes of the land, and we will read about Babylon taking over the rest of the land leaving nothing left of the people of Judah except for the weakest among them."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Zedekiah Taken","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think Babylon is just toying with Judah now. They have been setting up kings in place of the kings they have taken captive, then gave them time to build an army, and are now just fighting against it to prove a point. They are proving that there is nothing anyone can do against them, even if they actually give them resources to fight."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So not only does Babylon attack and successfully besiege Judah (again at that), there is a famine going across the land. There is no way for Judah to be successful here. The Lord is against them, and the world is against them. They are backed into a corner with nowhere to go but up. They must look to the Lord. They will not. They will continue to swing and fight at whoever they can, and they will not succeed without God, and they will continue to bring them lower and lower into poverty and captivity."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Zedekiah was not going to prosper today. There was nothing that was going to help him at this point. He could try and run all he wants, but he will never be able to outrun the Will of God. God's Will, here (in this case), was for Judah to be completely taken over. Judah could not exist for this time because of their sin. They don't want to address it, so the Lord will."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This had to be a difficult thing (no matter what kind of mentality you have) to watch. He just watched his children be killed in front of him. Sacrificing your children is one thing, but watching your child be murdered for your lack of authority and providence is a different thing. Both are terrible. Then, to add insult, they take away his sight. The very last thing he was able to see was his children being slaughtered in front of him. That will be the last memory he has with his eyes… That is a miserable thing to go through. I wouldn't wish that on my worst enemies."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":12,"title":"The Poor's Inheritance","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are destroying any remnant of Judah. There will be nothing left for the people to go back to. We will see in the future that the Israelites will come back and rebuild, and in that process, they will rebuild their faith and purpose. They will go back to the original idea of faith. However, that won't last very long either, and eventually, the Lord will allow them to be taken captive in their own land by the Romans. That is what we will see in the New Testament."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The poor are living as well as the rich at this moment. They have jobs to do, and the jobs were to provide some fruits and vegetables to Babylon. They would remain in their land too. That is a comforting thing for them. The poor of the land are getting the better treatment here, honestly. It just goes to show that riches are not all that they’re made up to be. The last are first now."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But many that are first shall be last; and the last shall be first.","ref":"Matthew 19:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"That will be the rule of eternity, and that is the proof of that happening here in the land. Obviously, I don't believe the people of Babylon did this to intentionally help the poor, but it was a byproduct of being poor. There was nothing else that Babylon could do with the poor of the land than this. The people were useless to the world. But they had a great use to the Lord."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":17,"title":"The Temple's Value Destroyed","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have now come full circle back to the beginning of the kings. We opened 1 Kings with the building of all this, and we are closing 2 Kings with the tearing apart of all this. There were a lot of things for them to take. Obviously, that was not the important thing to note here. We are given this information simply because that was what the world liked. Babylon was a picture of prosperity. It was a picture of worldly lust. So the writer is showing this, intentionally, after showing what life was like for the poor now."},{"kind":"para","text":"The poor had a finer life at the moment. They didn't care about the gold and brass and all the other stuff that was being taken from the temple. They had more now than they ever had before. Those things were there in the Lord's temple specifically to provide proof that they had prosperity when they had the Lord. They have lost it all because they lost their sight of God. That is how we lose everything. We lose everything when we lose God."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":21,"title":"No More Strength","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was no more strength left in Judah. Babylon made sure there could be no rebellious uprising, or any other sort of attack on the land of Babylon. Judah and all of Israel are completely taken, and there is no turning back now. If they had continued to follow the Lord, this end would look much different. We have not had many high points reading through the books of 1&2 Kings, and it doesn't sound like it is going to end on a high point either. Judah is no longer prosperous. They are no longer a people unified. The only prosperity of Judah (at the moment) was their poor. That has been something that God has brought to the land’s attention multiple times through scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This poor man cried, and the LORD heard him, and saved him out of all his troubles.","ref":"Psalm 34:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto the LORD; and that which he hath given will he pay him again.","ref":"Proverbs 19:17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If there be among you a poor man of one of thy brethren within any of thy gates in thy land which the LORD thy God giveth thee, thou shalt not harden thine heart, nor shut thine hand from thy poor brother: But thou shalt open thine hand wide unto him, and shalt surely lend him sufficient for his need, in that which he wanteth.","ref":"Deuteronomy 15:7-8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Hearken, my beloved brethren, Hath not God chosen the poor of this world rich in faith, and heirs of the kingdom which he hath promised to them that love him?","ref":"James 2:5"}]}]},{"start":22,"end":26,"title":"A Remenant Rebels","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, Nebuchadnezzar was doing a kindness for them in a way. They could have been without any sort of governing, and that would have commenced chaos. Instead (as we will see in a moment), the weakest people left in the land try to rebel against the leadership of Babylon. Jeremiah will warn against this, and Babylon will also tell them to be content. The Lord didn't want them to get hurt because He knows there is nothing they can do to overthrow this government now."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Gedaliah is trying to provide them with peace. All they would have to do is work. They could be given a place to live with them. At the moment, these people were living in Egypt, and they are coming out of the woodwork to try and take their land back. It is a mistake. Jeremiah is in Egypt at the moment telling them to refrain from this fight. He's in Egypt because his own people took him captive. Now they are going to ignore the advice of Jeremiah to their own detriment."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They just committed an attack on one of the most powerful lands in the world. There was no way they could have ever thought that this would be a good idea. They were warned not to do it, but they did it anyway, and they will have to pay the consequences of their actions."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":30,"title":"A Man Named Evil","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I wouldn't trust anyone that has \"Evil\" in their name. \"Evilmedrodach\" means \"man of Merodach.” Whatever that means. He is in charge after Nebuchadnezzar dies. Babylon seems to have had Jehoiachin in prison for quite some time. He was just sitting in there rotting and suffering."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Strangely enough, \"Evil\" doesn't mean he was actually evil. Well, to be fair, he is making friends with an evil king. So perhaps it DOES mean evil. There was a reason for this. The Lord is giving some alleviation to Jehoiachin. And that is how this book ends. You might be thinking, \"But that doesn't conclude anything! I feel like it's lacking some closure.” The Lord provides some kindness to David's line still, even though he was evil. And He uses a man named Evil to do it. What an interesting ending."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are assured that the line of David continues on, because we get the Savior eventually, and He comes THROUGH that line. However, we will not get closure here. The closure we are looking for is found in other books of the Bible. I think it's sufficient to point you in the right direction now. If you want to read about what happens during the captivity, go to Daniel. If you want to read about the people coming back to the land, go to Nehemiah. If you want to read about what takes place after this incident, go to Jeremiah. There is plenty for you to explore, and the Lord has allowed this book as a stepping stone to discovery. He wants you to care enough to dig deeper, THAT is why we end this book off without closure. He wants you to find the answers with your heart and soul. That is what the kings of Israel didn't do. They didn't care enough to dig deeper. We found that the kings that DID dig deeper found prosperity. This is your path and motive forward into that. Love God's Word, and be interested enough to dig deeper."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":30}]},
  },
};
